  'm_46_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_68',
  'After deploying a machine learning model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint, the data science team observes that the model''s accuracy is gradually declining over time, even though the infrastructure metrics are healthy. Which SageMaker service should they use to identify this issue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Pipelines","Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","Amazon SageMaker Studio","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor. It is specifically designed to detect data and model quality drift, which is the scenario described where model accuracy declines due to evolving real-world data (concept drift). Relying only on infrastructure health metrics is an anti-pattern (ap-4.4-001). Option A (Amazon SageMaker Pipelines) is for automating ML workflows. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Model Registry) is for versioning and managing models. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Studio) is an integrated development environment for ML.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_69',
  'A manufacturing company has developed a deep learning model for quality inspection that needs to be deployed to edge devices with limited computational resources. The model is currently large and computationally intensive. The company needs to optimize the model to run efficiently on these devices, reducing its size and inference latency while preserving accuracy. Which Amazon SageMaker feature is specifically designed to achieve this model optimization for edge deployment?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Neo, which compiles machine learning models to optimize them for various hardware targets, including edge devices, reducing size and improving inference performance.","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, used for processing large datasets offline, not for optimizing models for edge deployment.","Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints, designed for hosting multiple models on shared cloud infrastructure, not for edge optimization.","AWS Inferentia, a cloud-based inference accelerator, which is not directly applicable for optimizing models to run on resource-constrained edge devices."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Neo. SageMaker Neo is a model compilation service that optimizes machine learning models for deployment on various hardware targets, including edge devices, by converting them into an executable format that leverages hardware-specific optimizations. This process can significantly reduce model size, improve inference latency, and reduce memory footprint, making models suitable for resource-constrained environments like edge devices.
Option B (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, which is used for offline inference on large datasets in the cloud. It does not provide model optimization capabilities for edge deployment.
Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints, which is a cloud-based inference solution for hosting multiple models on shared infrastructure. It is not related to optimizing models for edge devices.
Option D (incorrect) is AWS Inferentia, which is a custom AWS-designed chip for high-performance, cost-effective deep learning inference in the cloud. While it''s an optimization target, Inferentia itself is the hardware, not the service that *optimizes* the model for *edge devices*. SageMaker Neo is the service that performs the compilation for such targets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_70',
  'A startup has developed a new computer vision model for identifying specific objects in images. They anticipate a massive influx of customer-specific models, potentially thousands, each trained on unique datasets for different clients. Each client''s model is relatively small, but the total number of models is very high. The startup needs to deploy these models for real-time inference in a way that is highly scalable, minimizes infrastructure costs, and allows for dynamic loading and unloading of models without dedicated endpoints for each. Which Amazon SageMaker feature is the MOST suitable for this architecture?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints, enabling thousands of models to be hosted on a shared fleet of instances, dynamically loading and unloading them as needed.","Deploying each client''s model on a separate Amazon SageMaker Real-time Endpoint with individual auto-scaling configurations.","Utilizing Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform to process inference requests for all client models in a scheduled, offline manner.","Implementing Amazon SageMaker Async Inference to handle the large number of models, leveraging its ability to process large payloads and long-running inferences asynchronously."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) describes Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints. This feature is specifically designed for scenarios where you have a large number of models (thousands, in this case) that can share compute resources. It allows you to host multiple models on a single endpoint instance, dynamically loading and unloading them from memory based on inference requests. This significantly reduces infrastructure costs and operational overhead compared to deploying each model on its own endpoint, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001].
Option B (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern [ap-4.1-001]. Deploying each of the thousands of client models on a separate Amazon SageMaker Real-time Endpoint would be extremely cost-prohibitive and operationally complex. Each endpoint would incur costs for its instances, even if idle, leading to massive over-provisioning and management overhead.
Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, which is suitable for offline inference on large datasets where real-time responses are not required. The scenario explicitly states the need for ''real-time inference'', making Batch Transform an unsuitable choice.
Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Async Inference, which is designed for real-time inference with large payloads or long-running requests, processed asynchronously. While it can handle high throughput, its primary benefit is not cost optimization for hosting thousands of *different* small models on shared infrastructure, which is the core strength of Multi-Model Endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_71',
  'What is the primary benefit of using Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints for deploying many similar machine learning models?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reduced inference costs by sharing compute resources","Improved model training speed for all models","Automated data preprocessing for inference requests","Enhanced security isolation for each individual model"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is reduced inference costs by sharing compute resources. SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints allow multiple models to be hosted on a single endpoint instance, dynamically loading and unloading models as needed, which significantly reduces the cost per model for inference compared to deploying each model on a dedicated endpoint (ap-4.1-001). Option B (incorrect) refers to model training speed, which is unrelated to inference endpoints. Option C (incorrect) is automated data preprocessing, which is not the primary function of Multi-Model Endpoints. Option D (incorrect) is enhanced security isolation; while SageMaker provides security, Multi-Model Endpoints focus on efficiency and cost, not necessarily enhanced isolation compared to dedicated endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_72',
  'A machine learning team wants to ensure that all data used for training and inference, as well as model artifacts, are encrypted at rest and in transit. Which AWS security practice is essential for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Encryption of training data, model artifacts, and inference I/O","Granting SageMaker execution roles full S3 access","Using public S3 buckets for model storage","Disabling VPC isolation for SageMaker workloads"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is encryption of training data, model artifacts, and inference I/O. This is a fundamental security practice to protect sensitive ML assets both when stored (at rest) and when being transferred (in transit), typically using AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). Option B (incorrect) is granting SageMaker execution roles full S3 access, which violates the principle of least privilege and is a security anti-pattern (ap-4.3-001). Option C (incorrect) is using public S3 buckets for model storage, which is highly insecure and exposes sensitive data. Option D (incorrect) is disabling VPC isolation, which would expose SageMaker workloads to the public internet and compromise network security.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_73',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor?',
  'mcq',
  '["To detect data drift, model quality, and bias drift in deployed models","To automate the entire machine learning workflow pipeline","To register and version control machine learning models","To compile models for optimized inference on various hardware"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is to detect data drift, model quality, and bias drift in deployed models. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor continuously analyzes the data collected from SageMaker endpoints and the predictions generated to identify deviations from expected behavior, ensuring models maintain their performance over time (ap-4.4-001). Option B (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Pipelines. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Model Registry. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Neo.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_74',
  'A company wants to automate the entire machine learning lifecycle, from data preparation and model training to deployment and continuous integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD). Which Amazon SageMaker service is designed for this end-to-end automation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Pipelines","Amazon SageMaker Studio","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Amazon SageMaker Neo"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Pipelines. It is a purpose-built CI/CD service for machine learning that allows you to create, manage, and orchestrate automated, end-to-end ML workflows, integrating with services like AWS CodePipeline for full CI/CD. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Studio, an integrated development environment. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, for detecting model drift. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Neo, for model optimization and compilation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_75',
  'A machine learning team needs to ensure that their SageMaker Studio environment is secure and that different users have appropriate access levels to resources and data. Which security feature helps manage multi-user access and permissions within SageMaker Studio?',
  'mcq',
  '["SageMaker Studio access controls and multi-user domain configuration","Granting all users administrator access to the AWS account","Using public internet access for all Studio notebooks","Disabling encryption for SageMaker Studio storage"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is SageMaker Studio access controls and multi-user domain configuration. SageMaker Studio allows administrators to configure domains for multiple users, each with their own isolated environment and fine-grained access controls managed through AWS IAM, ensuring secure and controlled access to ML resources. Option B (incorrect) is granting all users administrator access, which is a severe security risk and violates the principle of least privilege. Option C (incorrect) is using public internet access for all Studio notebooks, which compromises network security. Option D (incorrect) is disabling encryption, which would expose sensitive data at rest.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_76',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon SageMaker Model Registry?',
  'mcq',
  '["To version, catalog, and manage machine learning models for deployment","To monitor the performance and quality of deployed models","To automate the steps of a machine learning workflow","To optimize models for specific hardware targets"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is to version, catalog, and manage machine learning models for deployment. Amazon SageMaker Model Registry provides a central repository to track model versions, approve or reject models for deployment, and maintain a clear lineage of models throughout their lifecycle. Option B (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Pipelines. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Neo.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_77',
  'A data scientist is preparing a model for deployment on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The model is large, and the inference requests are expected to have payloads up to 500 MB, with processing times that can range from a few seconds to several minutes. Which inference option should be chosen to handle these characteristics efficiently?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Async Inference","Amazon SageMaker Real-time Endpoints","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Async Inference. This option is specifically designed for inference requests with large payloads (up to 1 GB) and/or long processing times (up to 1 hour), where clients do not require an immediate synchronous response. It queues requests and sends responses to an Amazon S3 location. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Real-time Endpoints, which are optimized for low-latency, synchronous responses with smaller payloads. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, which is for offline processing of entire datasets, not individual large requests to an endpoint. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints, which optimize hosting for many models but are still primarily for real-time, synchronous inference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_78',
  'Which AWS service allows you to compile trained machine learning models to optimize them for deployment on a variety of hardware platforms, including AWS Inferentia, reducing inference latency and cost?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Neo","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines","Amazon SageMaker Studio"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Neo. SageMaker Neo compiles machine learning models into an executable that runs up to two times faster with less memory footprint, optimizing them for various hardware targets, including cloud instances, edge devices, and AWS Inferentia. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, which tracks model quality. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Pipelines, which automates ML workflows. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Studio, an integrated development environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_79',
  'A company is developing a new application that needs to extract specific information, such as names, dates, and locations, from unstructured text documents. They want to leverage a pre-trained ML service to accelerate development. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Lex","Amazon Personalize"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon Comprehend. It is a natural language processing (NLP) service that uses machine learning to find insights and relationships in text, including entity recognition (names, dates, locations), without requiring custom model development. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon Rekognition, which is for image and video analysis. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Lex, for building conversational interfaces. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon Personalize, for recommendations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_80',
  'A company has deployed a machine learning model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. They want to ensure that the endpoint can automatically adjust its capacity to handle varying inference request loads, scaling up during peak times and scaling down during off-peak hours to optimize costs. Which feature enables this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker endpoint auto-scaling","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Async Inference","Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker endpoint auto-scaling. This feature automatically adjusts the number of instances provisioned for a SageMaker endpoint based on the inference request load, ensuring high availability and performance while optimizing costs. Option B (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, which is for offline inference. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Async Inference, which is for large payloads and long-latency requests, not for dynamic capacity adjustment. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints, which optimize hosting for many models on shared compute but do not inherently provide auto-scaling for the endpoint instances themselves without additional configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_81',
  'When building a custom machine learning model using Amazon SageMaker, what is a crucial security practice for protecting model artifacts and inference input/output data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Encrypting model artifacts and inference I/O with AWS KMS","Storing all model artifacts in a publicly accessible Amazon S3 bucket","Disabling IAM roles for SageMaker training jobs","Using unencrypted HTTP for all inference requests"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is encrypting model artifacts and inference I/O with AWS KMS. This ensures that sensitive data, including the trained model and the data exchanged during inference, is protected at rest and in transit, adhering to security best practices. Option B (incorrect) is storing all model artifacts in a publicly accessible Amazon S3 bucket, which is a major security vulnerability. Option C (incorrect) is disabling IAM roles for SageMaker training jobs, which would prevent SageMaker from accessing necessary resources and is not a security best practice. Option D (incorrect) is using unencrypted HTTP for all inference requests, which exposes data in transit to potential eavesdropping.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

