INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_101',
  'A company has hundreds of small, similar machine learning models that need to be deployed for real-time inference. To reduce costs and operational overhead, they decide to use Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoints. Arrange the following steps to deploy a model to a SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint.',
  'ordering',
  '["Upload the individual model artifacts for each model to an Amazon S3 prefix.","Create a SageMaker model, specifying the Multi-Model Endpoint container image and the S3 prefix for model artifacts.","Create a SageMaker endpoint configuration and deploy a SageMaker endpoint using the multi-model.","Invoke the endpoint, specifying the target model name in the inference request."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to make all individual models available. Option A (Upload the individual model artifacts for each model to an Amazon S3 prefix) centralizes the models. Next, Option B (Create a SageMaker model, specifying the Multi-Model Endpoint container image and the S3 prefix for model artifacts) defines the multi-model container that will host these models. Option C (Create a SageMaker endpoint configuration and deploy a SageMaker endpoint using the multi-model) provisions the actual endpoint. Finally, Option D (Invoke the endpoint, specifying the target model name in the inference request) demonstrates how to route requests to specific models hosted on the shared endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_102',
  'For models that require large input payloads or have long inference times, a development team wants to use Amazon SageMaker Async Inference. Arrange the following steps to configure SageMaker Async Inference for a model.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a SageMaker model from the trained model artifacts.","Create a SageMaker endpoint configuration, enabling asynchronous inference and specifying S3 locations for input and output.","Deploy the endpoint configuration to create a SageMaker endpoint.","Send inference requests to the endpoint''s `InvokeAsync` API, referencing the S3 input location."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process begins with having a model ready. Option A (Create a SageMaker model from the trained model artifacts) is the initial step. Next, Option B (Create a SageMaker endpoint configuration, enabling asynchronous inference and specifying S3 locations for input and output) configures the endpoint specifically for asynchronous operations. Option C (Deploy the endpoint configuration to create a SageMaker endpoint) provisions the actual endpoint. Finally, Option D (Send inference requests to the endpoint''s `InvokeAsync` API, referencing the S3 input location) initiates an asynchronous inference job.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_103',
  'A machine learning team needs to deploy a highly cost-efficient, high-throughput inference solution for their deep learning models. They plan to use AWS Inferentia. Arrange the following steps to deploy a model to AWS Inferentia.',
  'ordering',
  '["Train a deep learning model using a compatible framework (e.g., TensorFlow, PyTorch).","Compile the model for AWS Inferentia using Amazon SageMaker Neo.","Create a SageMaker endpoint configuration that specifies an Inferentia-powered instance type (e.g., `ml.inf1`).","Deploy the endpoint configuration to create a SageMaker endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to have a model that can be optimized. Option A (Train a deep learning model using a compatible framework) provides this. Next, Option B (Compile the model for AWS Inferentia using Amazon SageMaker Neo) optimizes the model specifically for Inferentia hardware. Option C (Create a SageMaker endpoint configuration that specifies an Inferentia-powered instance type) defines the hardware on which the model will run. Finally, Option D (Deploy the endpoint configuration to create a SageMaker endpoint) provisions the actual inference endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_104',
  'An MLOps team aims to automate model retraining whenever data drift is detected, ensuring continuous model performance. This requires integrating Amazon SageMaker Pipelines with Model Monitor. Arrange the following steps to automate model retraining.',
  'ordering',
  '["Set up Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift on a deployed endpoint.","Configure a CloudWatch Event rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function when Model Monitor detects drift.","Develop an AWS Lambda function that starts a SageMaker Pipeline for retraining.","Define a SageMaker Pipeline that includes data preprocessing, model training, and model registration steps."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'The foundation for automated retraining is a defined retraining process. Option D (Define a SageMaker Pipeline that includes data preprocessing, model training, and model registration steps) establishes this workflow. Next, Option A (Set up Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift on a deployed endpoint) configures the trigger for retraining. Option B (Configure a CloudWatch Event rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function when Model Monitor detects drift) creates the automation link. Finally, Option C (Develop an AWS Lambda function that starts a SageMaker Pipeline for retraining) implements the action taken when drift is detected, initiating the defined pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_46_m_46_3_105',
  'A development team wants to implement a CI/CD pipeline for their machine learning models using AWS CodePipeline and Amazon SageMaker Pipelines. Arrange the following steps to integrate these services for automated ML workflows.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a SageMaker Pipeline for the ML workflow (data processing, training, model registration).","Create an AWS CodePipeline that monitors a source code repository for changes.","Add a build stage to CodePipeline that triggers the SageMaker Pipeline execution.","Add a deployment stage to CodePipeline that deploys the registered model to a SageMaker endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The first step is to define the core ML workflow. Option A (Define a SageMaker Pipeline for the ML workflow) encapsulates the ML specific tasks. Next, Option B (Create an AWS CodePipeline that monitors a source code repository for changes) sets up the continuous integration system. Option C (Add a build stage to CodePipeline that triggers the SageMaker Pipeline execution) links the CI system to the ML workflow. Finally, Option D (Add a deployment stage to CodePipeline that deploys the registered model to a SageMaker endpoint) automates the deployment of the newly trained and registered model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain46_10',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_46_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

