INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_34',
  'What is the primary goal of feature engineering in a machine learning workflow?',
  'mcq',
  '["To deploy the trained model to a production endpoint","To transform raw data into features that improve model performance","To automatically select the best machine learning algorithm","To monitor the model''s performance in real-time"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary goal of feature engineering: to transform raw data into a set of features that are more informative and predictive, thereby improving the performance and accuracy of machine learning models. Option A describes model deployment. Option C describes automated machine learning services like Amazon SageMaker Autopilot. Option D describes model monitoring tools like Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_35',
  'When working with text data for an Amazon SageMaker NLP model, which technique breaks down a sentence into individual words or subword units?',
  'mcq',
  '["Feature scaling","Tokenization","Binning","Dimensionality reduction"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option B (correct) is Tokenization, the process of breaking down a sequence of text into smaller units called tokens (words, subwords, or characters). This is a fundamental step in preparing text data for NLP models. Option A (Feature scaling) is for numerical data. Option C (Binning) is for converting continuous numerical data into discrete categories. Option D (Dimensionality reduction) is used to reduce the number of features in a dataset.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_36',
  'A data scientist wants to convert a continuous numerical feature, such as ''Age'', into discrete categories like ''Young'', ''Adult'', and ''Senior''. Which feature engineering technique is being applied?',
  'mcq',
  '["One-hot encoding","Normalization","Tokenization","Binning"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) describes Binning (also known as discretization), which is the process of grouping continuous numerical data into a finite number of intervals or ''bins''. This converts a continuous feature into an ordinal or categorical one. Option A (One-hot encoding) converts categorical data into numerical. Option B (Normalization) scales numerical data to a specific range. Option C (Tokenization) is for processing text data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_37',
  'For a dataset with a very high number of features, which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm can be used to reduce the number of dimensions while retaining most of the variance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Principal Component Analysis (PCA)","XGBoost","K-Means","Linear Learner"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Principal Component Analysis (PCA), an Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm specifically designed for dimensionality reduction. It transforms the data into a new set of orthogonal components, retaining the most significant variance. Option B (XGBoost) is a gradient boosting algorithm for classification and regression. Option C (K-Means) is a clustering algorithm. Option D (Linear Learner) is for linear classification and regression. All are SageMaker built-in algorithms but serve different purposes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_38',
  'What is a common reason to identify and handle outliers in a dataset during feature engineering?',
  'mcq',
  '["Outliers always represent corrupted data that must be removed","Outliers can disproportionately influence model training and lead to poor generalization","Outliers are necessary for robust model performance","Outliers simplify the process of feature selection"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that outliers can disproportionately influence model training. Extreme values can skew statistical measures (like mean and standard deviation) and impact the model''s ability to learn the true underlying patterns, leading to poor generalization. Option A is incorrect; outliers are not always corrupted data; they can be legitimate but rare observations. Option C and Option D are incorrect; outliers typically complicate, rather than simplify, model performance and feature selection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_39',
  'A data scientist is working with a dataset that includes ''Length'' and ''Width'' features for objects. To potentially improve model performance, they decide to create a new feature ''Area'' by multiplying ''Length'' and ''Width''. What type of feature engineering is this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Feature scaling","Binning","Creating synthetic features","One-hot encoding"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes creating synthetic features, also known as feature interaction or feature construction. This involves combining or transforming existing features to generate new ones that might capture more complex relationships in the data and improve model performance. Option A (Feature scaling) is for normalizing numerical ranges. Option B (Binning) converts continuous data to discrete categories. Option D (One-hot encoding) converts categorical data to numerical.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_40',
  'After training a model with Amazon SageMaker, a data scientist wants to understand which input features had the most impact on the model''s predictions. What concept are they trying to evaluate?',
