) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_41',
  'Which of the following best describes the overall objective of performing feature engineering?',
  'mcq',
  '["To improve the predictive power and accuracy of machine learning models","To reduce the cost of storing the raw dataset in Amazon S3","To automate the deployment of models to SageMaker endpoints","To visualize data distributions using scatter plots"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) states the core objective of feature engineering: to enhance the quality of input features, making them more suitable for the machine learning model, which ultimately leads to improved predictive power and accuracy. Option B (reducing storage cost) is not the primary goal of feature engineering. Option C (automating deployment) is part of MLOps. Option D (visualizing data) is part of exploratory data analysis, which informs feature engineering but is not the objective itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_42',
  'What is the primary goal of feature engineering in a machine learning pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["To automatically select the best machine learning algorithm for a given dataset.","To transform raw data into features that better represent the underlying problem to the model, improving its performance.","To deploy the trained machine learning model to a production environment.","To monitor the model''s performance and detect data drift in real-time."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary goal of feature engineering: to transform raw data into features that better represent the underlying problem to the model, thereby improving its performance. This involves creating new features, modifying existing ones, or selecting relevant features to enhance the model''s ability to learn patterns. Option A is incorrect as feature engineering is distinct from algorithm selection, though it can influence it. Option C describes model deployment, and Option D describes model monitoring, neither of which is the goal of feature engineering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_43',
  'Which technique is used to convert categorical text data, such as words or phrases, into numerical representations that machine learning models can process?',
  'mcq',
  '["Binning","Normalization","Tokenization and Encoding","Dimensionality Reduction"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) refers to Tokenization and Encoding. Tokenization breaks text into smaller units (tokens), and encoding (e.g., one-hot encoding, word embeddings) converts these tokens into numerical vectors that machine learning models can understand. Option A, binning, is for continuous numerical data. Option B, normalization, scales numerical data to a standard range. Option D, dimensionality reduction, reduces the number of features, which is a different goal from converting text to numerical format.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_44',
  'A data scientist is working with a dataset containing a continuous numerical feature (e.g., customer age) and wants to group it into discrete intervals (e.g., ''young'', ''middle-aged'', ''senior'') to capture non-linear relationships. What feature engineering technique should be applied?',
  'mcq',
  '["One-hot encoding","Binning (discretization)","Log transformation","Feature scaling"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is binning, also known as discretization. This technique involves grouping continuous numerical data into a set of discrete bins or intervals. This can help capture non-linear relationships, reduce noise, and make the data more interpretable for certain models. Option A, one-hot encoding, is used for converting categorical variables into a numerical format. Option C, log transformation, is used to reduce skewness in data. Option D, feature scaling, normalizes or standardizes numerical features to a specific range or distribution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_45',
  'A machine learning model is underperforming due to a high number of input features, leading to increased training time and potential overfitting. Which feature engineering technique could help address this issue by creating a smaller set of new features that capture most of the variance?',
  'mcq',
  '["One-hot encoding","Data augmentation","Dimensionality reduction (e.g., PCA)","Feature scaling"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is dimensionality reduction, such as Principal Component Analysis (PCA). This technique aims to reduce the number of features by transforming the original features into a new, smaller set of features (components) that retain most of the important information (variance) from the original dataset. This can significantly reduce training time and mitigate overfitting. Option A, one-hot encoding, increases the number of features for categorical data. Option B, data augmentation, increases the size of the training dataset, not reduces feature count. Option D, feature scaling, changes the scale of features but not their count.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_46',
  'What is data augmentation primarily used for in the context of training deep learning models for computer vision tasks?',
  'mcq',
  '["To reduce the number of parameters in a neural network.","To increase the size and diversity of the training dataset by creating modified versions of existing images.","To select the most relevant features from the input images.","To compress image data for more efficient storage."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes data augmentation''s primary use: to increase the size and diversity of the training dataset by creating modified versions of existing images (e.g., rotating, flipping, zooming). This helps prevent overfitting and improves the model''s generalization capabilities. Option A is incorrect as data augmentation doesn''t directly reduce network parameters. Option C describes feature selection. Option D describes data compression, which is a different goal.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_47',
  'A team is building a fraud detection model using tabular data with structured features like transaction amount, time, and merchant ID. They are considering using a complex deep neural network for this problem. Which anti-pattern should they be aware of for this type of data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Optimizing ML metrics (e.g., AUC) without aligning them to business outcomes.","Running hyperparameter tuning with random search instead of Bayesian optimization.","Defaulting to complex neural networks for tabular data problems.","Using the same dataset for validation during training and for final model selection."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001]: ''Defaulting to complex neural networks for tabular data problems''. For tabular data, gradient boosting methods like XGBoost or LightGBM often outperform deep learning models with less data and tuning effort. Deep learning excels more with unstructured data like images or text. Option A is anti-pattern [ap-3.1-001], Option B is anti-pattern [ap-3.3-001], and Option D is anti-pattern [ap-3.5-001]; while valid anti-patterns, they are not specific to the choice of model complexity for tabular data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_48',
  'Which feature engineering technique involves creating new features by combining or transforming existing ones, such as calculating the ratio of two features or extracting day of the week from a timestamp?',
  'mcq',
  '["Missing value imputation","Feature creation (synthetic features)","Outlier removal","Data sampling"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is feature creation, also known as creating synthetic features. This technique involves deriving new features from existing ones to provide more information to the model or to better capture complex relationships. Examples include ratios, differences, polynomial features, or extracting temporal components from timestamps. Option A, missing value imputation, deals with missing data. Option C, outlier removal, addresses anomalous data points. Option D, data sampling, involves selecting a subset of data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_49',
  'To improve the robustness of a computer vision model, a data scientist wants to create variations of existing training images by rotating, flipping, or zooming them. Which technique is being applied?',
  'mcq',
  '["Image compression","Feature extraction","Data augmentation","Dimensionality reduction"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is data augmentation. This technique involves artificially increasing the size of the training dataset by generating modified versions of existing images. Common transformations include rotation, flipping, zooming, cropping, and color adjustments. This helps the model generalize better and reduces overfitting. Option A, image compression, reduces file size. Option B, feature extraction, derives higher-level features from raw image data. Option D, dimensionality reduction, reduces the number of features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_50',
  'A large e-commerce company is developing a fraud detection model using a vast dataset of transactional records, customer demographics, and device information. The data contains several categorical features with high cardinality (e.g., product IDs, merchant IDs) and numerical features with varying scales. The data scientists have identified that the model''s performance is highly sensitive to the encoding of these categorical features and the scaling of numerical features. They need to prepare this data for an Amazon SageMaker XGBoost model, ensuring optimal performance and interpretability, while minimizing manual preprocessing steps and potential data leakage during training. Which approach should the ML engineer recommend for feature engineering?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually apply One-Hot Encoding for high-cardinality categorical features and Min-Max Scaling for numerical features in a custom AWS Lambda function before uploading to Amazon S3 for SageMaker training.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs with a custom scikit-learn script to apply Target Encoding for high-cardinality categorical features and Robust Scaling for numerical features, ensuring the encoding and scaling parameters are learned only from the training set.","Implement Label Encoding for all categorical features and Standard Scaling for numerical features directly within the SageMaker XGBoost algorithm''s input parameters to simplify the pipeline.","Pre-process the data using Amazon Athena SQL queries to convert categorical features into numerical representations and apply Z-score normalization, then store the processed data in Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most robust and best practice approach. Using Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs allows for scalable and managed execution of data preprocessing, including complex feature engineering techniques like Target Encoding for high-cardinality categorical features and Robust Scaling for numerical features, which are less sensitive to outliers. Crucially, performing this within a processing job ensures that encoding and scaling parameters are learned *only* from the training data, preventing data leakage from the validation or test sets. This aligns with best practices for preparing data for modeling and optimizing for model performance.
Option A (incorrect) involves manual preprocessing with AWS Lambda, which is not designed for large-scale data transformation and would introduce significant operational overhead and complexity compared to SageMaker Processing jobs. It also risks data leakage if not carefully implemented.
Option C (incorrect) suggests applying encoding and scaling within the XGBoost algorithm''s input parameters. While some algorithms have built-in basic preprocessing, complex and specific techniques like Target Encoding or Robust Scaling are typically not handled natively by the algorithm itself, and relying on this would limit flexibility and control over the feature engineering process. Label Encoding is also generally not suitable for nominal categorical features as it implies an ordinal relationship.
Option D (incorrect) uses Amazon Athena for preprocessing. While Athena can perform transformations, it''s primarily a query service for data lakes and less suited for complex, iterative feature engineering tasks that require stateful transformations (like learning encoding parameters from a training set). It also doesn''t inherently prevent data leakage without careful manual management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_51',
  'A retail company is developing a recommendation system using a dataset of customer purchase history, product attributes, and browsing behavior. The data scientists observe that the ''customer_age'' feature, while numerical, has a non-linear relationship with purchase probability and that specific age ranges (e.g., 18-24, 25-34, 35-54) are more indicative of certain product preferences. To capture these non-linear patterns effectively for a gradient boosting model, they need to transform this feature while maintaining interpretability and avoiding overfitting. Which feature engineering technique is most appropriate for the ''customer_age'' feature?',
  'mcq',
  '["Apply a logarithmic transformation to ''customer_age'' to reduce skewness and linearize its relationship with the target variable.","Perform one-hot encoding on ''customer_age'' after binning it into predefined age ranges (e.g., 18-24, 25-34, 35-54) to represent each range as a distinct binary feature.","Use polynomial features to create higher-order terms of ''customer_age'' (e.g., age^2, age^3) to capture non-linear relationships.","Normalize ''customer_age'' using Min-Max scaling to bring all values into a fixed range (e.g., 0 to 1) for consistent model input."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate technique. Binning ''customer_age'' into predefined, meaningful age ranges and then applying one-hot encoding allows the model to learn distinct weights for each age group. This directly captures the observed non-linear relationship where specific age ranges are indicative of preferences, without assuming a continuous linear or polynomial relationship. One-hot encoding these bins also maintains interpretability, as each new feature clearly represents an age segment. This is particularly effective for gradient boosting models which can leverage these distinct binary features.
Option A (incorrect) suggests a logarithmic transformation. While this can reduce skewness and sometimes linearize relationships, it assumes a specific mathematical form of non-linearity that might not match the observed age-range specific patterns. It doesn''t allow the model to treat distinct age segments independently.
Option C (incorrect) proposes polynomial features. While polynomial features can capture non-linearity, they can also lead to overfitting if not carefully selected and can make interpretation more complex. They might not perfectly align with the specific, discrete age range preferences observed.
Option D (incorrect) recommends Min-Max scaling. Scaling is important for some models (e.g., SVMs, neural networks) but it doesn''t transform the underlying distribution or capture non-linear relationships. It merely rescales the existing values, which wouldn''t address the need to model age ranges distinctly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

