INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_25',
  'Which type of machine learning problem involves training a model on a dataset where the output labels are explicitly provided for each input example?',
  'mcq',
  '["Unsupervised learning.","Reinforcement learning.","Supervised learning.","Semi-supervised learning."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is supervised learning. In supervised learning, the model learns from a labeled dataset, meaning each training example includes both the input features and the corresponding correct output label. Option A, unsupervised learning, deals with unlabeled data to find hidden patterns. Option B, reinforcement learning, involves an agent learning through trial and error in an environment. Option D, semi-supervised learning, uses a combination of labeled and unlabeled data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_26',
  'A retail company wants to identify distinct customer segments based on their purchasing history, without any predefined categories. Which type of machine learning problem is this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regression.","Classification.","Clustering.","Forecasting."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is clustering. Clustering is an unsupervised learning technique used to group similar data points together into clusters, where the groups are not predefined. This matches the scenario of identifying distinct customer segments without prior labels. Option A, regression, predicts a continuous numerical value. Option B, classification, predicts a discrete category from predefined labels. Option D, forecasting, predicts future values based on historical data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_27',
  'What is the primary characteristic that distinguishes supervised learning from unsupervised learning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised learning models are always more complex.","Supervised learning requires labeled training data, while unsupervised learning does not.","Unsupervised learning always achieves higher accuracy.","Unsupervised learning is used for prediction, while supervised learning is used for discovery."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the fundamental distinction. Supervised learning algorithms learn from input-output pairs (labeled data) to map inputs to outputs, whereas unsupervised learning algorithms work with unlabeled data to discover hidden patterns or structures. Option A is incorrect; complexity varies by algorithm, not solely by learning type. Option C is incorrect; accuracy depends on the problem and data. Option D is incorrect; both can be used for various purposes, but supervised learning is typically for prediction and unsupervised for pattern discovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_28',
  'A streaming service wants to predict whether a new movie will be a ''hit'' or a ''flop'' based on its genre, cast, and budget. Which type of ML problem is this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regression.","Classification.","Clustering.","Anomaly detection."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is classification. The problem involves predicting a discrete category (''hit'' or ''flop'') from a set of predefined labels, which is the definition of a classification task. Option A, regression, predicts a continuous value. Option C, clustering, groups data points without predefined labels. Option D, anomaly detection, identifies unusual data points that deviate from the norm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_29',
  'Which of the following scenarios is best framed as a regression problem?',
  'mcq',
  '["Predicting if an email is spam or not spam.","Categorizing customer reviews as positive, negative, or neutral.","Estimating the future sales revenue for the next quarter.","Grouping similar news articles into topics."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a regression problem because it involves predicting a continuous numerical value (sales revenue). Option A and Option B are classification problems, as they involve predicting discrete categories. Option D is a clustering problem, as it involves grouping items without predefined labels.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_30',
  'A company wants to build a recommendation system that suggests products to users based on their past purchases and browsing behavior. Which type of ML problem is this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Classification.","Regression.","Recommendation.","Time series forecasting."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a recommendation problem. Recommendation systems are a specific category of machine learning applications designed to suggest relevant items to users. While they might use underlying classification or regression techniques, the overarching problem type is recommendation. Option A, classification, and Option B, regression, are too general. Option D, time series forecasting, is for predicting future values over time.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_31',
  'What is the primary goal of framing a business problem as an ML problem?',
  'mcq',
  '["To ensure that a non-ML solution is always considered first.","To define a solvable and measurable objective that can be addressed using machine learning techniques.","To gather as much data as possible, regardless of its relevance.","To immediately deploy a complex deep learning model."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the primary goal. Framing a business problem as an ML problem involves clearly defining the objective, identifying the type of ML task (e.g., classification, regression), and ensuring that the problem is solvable with available data and measurable with appropriate metrics. Option A is incorrect; while non-ML solutions should be considered, the goal of framing an ML problem is to apply ML. Option C is incorrect; data relevance is crucial. Option D is incorrect; immediate deployment of complex models is not the first step and may not be appropriate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_32',
  'Which of the following best describes an unsupervised learning task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Predicting house prices based on features like size and location.","Detecting fraudulent transactions in a financial dataset.","Discovering natural groupings of documents in a large corpus without prior labels.","Identifying whether an image contains a cat or a dog."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes an unsupervised learning task, specifically clustering, where the goal is to find inherent patterns or groupings in unlabeled data. Option A is a regression task (supervised). Option B is a classification task (supervised). Option D is also a classification task (supervised).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_33',
  'Which type of learning involves an agent interacting with an environment, receiving rewards or penalties, and learning to make decisions to maximize cumulative reward?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised Learning.","Unsupervised Learning.","Reinforcement Learning.","Transfer Learning."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Reinforcement Learning. This paradigm involves an agent learning optimal behavior through trial and error by interacting with an environment, receiving feedback in the form of rewards or penalties. Option A, Supervised Learning, uses labeled data. Option B, Unsupervised Learning, finds patterns in unlabeled data. Option D, Transfer Learning, involves reusing a pre-trained model for a new, related task.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_34',
  'A startup is building a system to automatically categorize incoming customer support tickets into predefined topics like ''Billing'', ''Technical Issue'', or ''Feature Request''. Which type of machine learning problem does this scenario represent?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regression","Clustering","Classification","Reinforcement Learning"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Classification. In this scenario, the goal is to assign each support ticket to one of several predefined, discrete categories. This is the hallmark of a classification problem, where the model learns to map input features (ticket text) to output classes (topic categories).
Option A (Regression) is used for predicting a continuous numerical value, such as predicting house prices or temperature, which is not the case here.
Option B (Clustering) is an unsupervised learning technique used to group similar data points together without predefined labels. While it could group similar tickets, the requirement is to categorize into *predefined* topics.
Option D (Reinforcement Learning) involves an agent learning to make decisions by performing actions in an environment to maximize a reward. This is not applicable for categorizing text.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_35',
  'Which type of machine learning involves training a model on a dataset that consists of input features paired with their corresponding correct output labels?',
  'mcq',
  '["Unsupervised Learning","Reinforcement Learning","Supervised Learning","Semi-supervised Learning"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Supervised Learning. This type of machine learning involves learning a mapping from input features to output labels based on a labeled dataset. The model learns from examples where the correct output is already known.
Option A (Unsupervised Learning) deals with unlabeled data, aiming to find patterns or structures within the data, such as clustering or dimensionality reduction.
Option B (Reinforcement Learning) involves an agent learning through trial and error by interacting with an environment and receiving rewards or penalties.
Option D (Semi-supervised Learning) uses a combination of a small amount of labeled data and a large amount of unlabeled data during training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_36',
  'A company wants to identify distinct customer segments within their existing customer base without any prior knowledge of these segments. They have a large dataset of customer demographics and purchase history. Which machine learning approach is MOST suitable for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised Classification","Regression Analysis","Unsupervised Clustering","Time Series Forecasting"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Unsupervised Clustering. The goal is to identify distinct groups (segments) without predefined labels or prior knowledge of these segments. Clustering algorithms, a form of unsupervised learning, are designed to discover inherent groupings in data based on similarity.
Option A (Supervised Classification) requires labeled data (predefined customer segments) to train a model to classify new customers, which is not the case here.
Option B (Regression Analysis) is used for predicting continuous numerical values, not for grouping data points.
Option D (Time Series Forecasting) is used for predicting future values based on historical time-ordered data, which is not the objective of segmenting customers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_37',
  'What is the primary characteristic that differentiates supervised learning from unsupervised learning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised learning models are always more complex than unsupervised learning models.","Supervised learning requires labeled training data, while unsupervised learning works with unlabeled data.","Unsupervised learning always produces more accurate predictions than supervised learning.","Supervised learning is primarily used for deep learning tasks, while unsupervised learning is for traditional ML."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary difference. Supervised learning algorithms learn from input-output pairs (labeled data) to make predictions or classifications. Unsupervised learning algorithms, conversely, work with unlabeled data to find hidden patterns, structures, or groupings within the data.
Option A (incorrect) is false; model complexity depends on the specific algorithm and problem, not inherently on the learning type.
Option C (incorrect) is false; accuracy depends on the problem, data quality, and model effectiveness, not solely on whether it''s supervised or unsupervised.
Option D (incorrect) is false; both supervised and unsupervised learning techniques are used across various machine learning paradigms, including deep learning and traditional ML.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_38',
  'An e-commerce company wants to predict the exact revenue generated by a customer in the next quarter based on their past purchasing behavior and demographic information. Which type of machine learning problem should they frame this as?',
  'mcq',
  '["Classification","Clustering","Regression","Anomaly Detection"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Regression. Predicting an exact revenue figure means predicting a continuous numerical value. Regression models are designed for this type of task, where the output is a real-valued number.
Option A (Classification) is for predicting discrete categories or labels, such as ''high-value'' or ''low-value'' customer, not an exact revenue amount.
Option B (Clustering) is an unsupervised technique for grouping data points based on similarity, not for predicting specific numerical values.
Option D (Anomaly Detection) is for identifying unusual data points or patterns that deviate from the norm, which is not the goal here.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_39',
  'A marketing team wants to segment their customer base to create targeted campaigns. They have a large dataset of customer demographics, browsing history, and purchase data, but no predefined customer groups. Which machine learning task is most appropriate for this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised Learning: Classification","Supervised Learning: Regression","Unsupervised Learning: Clustering","Reinforcement Learning"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Unsupervised Learning: Clustering. Since there are no predefined customer groups (labels), the task is to discover inherent groupings or segments within the customer data. Clustering algorithms, which fall under unsupervised learning, are designed for this purpose.
Option A (Supervised Learning: Classification) would be appropriate if the company already had labeled customer segments and wanted to classify new customers into those existing segments.
Option B (Supervised Learning: Regression) is used for predicting continuous numerical values, such as customer lifetime value, not for segmenting customers into groups.
Option D (Reinforcement Learning) is used for training agents to make decisions in an environment, which is not applicable here.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_40',
  'Which statement best defines a ''forecasting'' problem in machine learning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Predicting a discrete category or label for a given input.","Identifying unusual data points that deviate significantly from the majority.","Predicting a future numerical value or trend based on historical time-series data.","Grouping similar data points together without prior knowledge of their labels."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) accurately defines a forecasting problem. Forecasting involves using historical data, often time-series data, to predict future values or trends, such as stock prices, sales, or weather patterns.
Option A (incorrect) describes a classification problem.
Option B (incorrect) describes an anomaly detection problem.
Option D (incorrect) describes a clustering problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_41',
  'Which type of machine learning problem involves training a model to predict a continuous numerical value, such as predicting house prices or temperature?',
  'mcq',
  '["Classification","Clustering","Regression","Anomaly Detection"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Regression. Regression problems involve predicting a continuous output variable. Examples include predicting the price of a house, the temperature, or the sales volume for a product.
Option A (Classification) involves predicting a discrete category or label.
Option B (Clustering) is an unsupervised learning technique for grouping similar data points without predefined labels.
Option D (Anomaly Detection) is for identifying unusual data points that deviate from the norm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_42',
  'A data scientist needs to build a model for predicting customer churn. The dataset contains various customer attributes, and the target variable is a binary indicator (churned/not churned). Which type of machine learning problem is this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regression","Clustering","Binary Classification","Time Series Forecasting"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Binary Classification. The problem involves predicting one of two discrete outcomes (churned or not churned), which is the definition of a binary classification task.
Option A (Regression) is for predicting continuous numerical values.
Option B (Clustering) is an unsupervised technique for grouping data without predefined labels.
Option D (Time Series Forecasting) is for predicting future values based on historical, time-ordered data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_43',
  'What is the fundamental characteristic that distinguishes supervised learning from unsupervised learning problems in machine learning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Supervised learning algorithms always require GPU acceleration, while unsupervised learning does not.","Supervised learning problems utilize labeled datasets with known output variables, whereas unsupervised learning problems work with unlabeled data to find hidden patterns.","Unsupervised learning models are inherently more complex and require larger datasets than supervised learning models.","Supervised learning is exclusively used for classification tasks, while unsupervised learning is only for regression tasks."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) precisely defines the core difference. Supervised learning algorithms learn from data that has been explicitly labeled with the correct output or target variable (e.g., images labeled ''cat'' or ''dog'', house prices with known values). The goal is to learn a mapping from input features to these known outputs. Unsupervised learning, conversely, deals with unlabeled data, aiming to discover inherent structures, patterns, or groupings within the data without any prior knowledge of output variables.
Option A is incorrect; GPU acceleration depends on model complexity and dataset size, not strictly on the learning paradigm. Option C is incorrect; complexity and dataset size vary widely for both paradigms. Option D is incorrect; supervised learning includes both classification and regression, and unsupervised learning includes clustering, dimensionality reduction, and association rule mining.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_44',
  'A streaming service wants to personalize content recommendations for its users. They have a vast amount of historical user viewing data, including ratings and watch times, but no explicit labels indicating "good" or "bad" recommendations. The goal is to group users with similar tastes and suggest content popular within those groups. Which ML problem type and learning approach should be chosen to achieve this, and what is the MOST efficient way to implement it on AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regression, using a supervised learning approach with Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner to predict user preferences.","Classification, using a supervised learning approach with Amazon SageMaker XGBoost to categorize content into predefined genres.","Clustering, using an unsupervised learning approach with Amazon SageMaker k-means to identify user segments based on viewing patterns.","Forecasting, using an unsupervised learning approach with Amazon Forecast to predict future content popularity."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most appropriate solution. The problem involves grouping users with similar tastes from unlabeled data to make recommendations, which is a classic use case for clustering, an unsupervised learning technique. Amazon SageMaker k-means is a managed service that efficiently performs clustering, making it suitable for identifying user segments based on their viewing patterns with minimal operational overhead.
Option A (Regression, supervised) is incorrect because there are no explicit labels for ''good'' or ''bad'' recommendations, and the goal is grouping, not predicting a numerical value. Option B (Classification, supervised) is incorrect for the same reason regarding labels, and the goal is not to categorize content into predefined genres but to group users. Option D (Forecasting, unsupervised) is incorrect; Amazon Forecast is for time-series forecasting, and while it''s an AWS service, the problem is about user segmentation, not predicting future values.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_45',
  'Which type of machine learning problem is best suited for identifying distinct customer segments within a large, unlabeled dataset based on purchasing behavior and demographics?',
  'mcq',
  '["Regression","Classification","Clustering","Reinforcement Learning"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Clustering. Clustering is an unsupervised learning technique specifically designed to group similar data points together into distinct segments or clusters, based on their inherent characteristics, without any prior labels. This perfectly matches the goal of identifying distinct customer segments from unlabeled data based on purchasing behavior and demographics.
Option A, Regression, is a supervised learning task for predicting continuous numerical values. Option B, Classification, is a supervised learning task for predicting discrete categories or labels. Option D, Reinforcement Learning, involves an agent learning to make decisions through trial and error in an environment, which is not applicable here.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_46',
  'When framing a business problem as an ML problem, what is the primary goal of mapping business Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) to specific ML objectives?',
  'mcq',
  '["To ensure the ML model can be deployed on any cloud provider without modification.","To guarantee that the chosen ML algorithm will achieve 100% accuracy on the production data.","To align the technical success of the ML model directly with the desired business outcomes and measure its impact.","To minimize the amount of data required for training the machine learning model."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the primary goal. Mapping business KPIs to ML objectives is crucial for ensuring that the technical success of the machine learning model (e.g., high accuracy, low error) translates into tangible business value (e.g., increased revenue, reduced costs, improved customer satisfaction). It defines what ''success'' looks like from a business perspective and provides a measurable way to evaluate the model''s impact.
Option A is incorrect; portability is a separate architectural concern. Option B is unrealistic; 100% accuracy is rarely achievable in real-world ML problems. Option D is incorrect; while data efficiency is important, it''s not the primary reason for mapping KPIs to ML objectives.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_47',
  'For a business aiming to predict the exact numerical price of a house based on various features such as size, number of bedrooms, and location, which type of ML problem formulation is most appropriate?',
  'mcq',
  '["Binary Classification","Multi-class Classification","Regression","Anomaly Detection"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Regression. Regression problems involve predicting a continuous numerical output value. Predicting the exact numerical price of a house fits this definition perfectly, as price is a continuous variable that can take any value within a range.
Option A, Binary Classification, is for predicting one of two discrete categories. Option B, Multi-class Classification, is for predicting one of more than two discrete categories. Option D, Anomaly Detection, is for identifying unusual data points that deviate significantly from the majority of the data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_48',
  'What is a critical initial step when determining if a business problem is suitable for a machine learning solution, rather than a rule-based or heuristic approach, especially for complex and evolving patterns?',
  'mcq',
  '["Immediately selecting the most advanced deep learning model available, regardless of data characteristics.","Assessing whether sufficient, high-quality historical data exists with relevant features and labels (if supervised learning is considered).","Developing a comprehensive set of if-then-else rules to solve the problem manually before considering ML.","Prioritizing solutions that require manual intervention to ensure human oversight in all decision-making processes."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is a critical initial step. Machine learning models, especially for complex and evolving patterns, are data-driven. Their success heavily relies on the availability of sufficient, high-quality historical data that contains the patterns to be learned. For supervised learning, this includes having accurate labels. Without adequate data, an ML solution is unlikely to outperform simpler rule-based systems or may not even be feasible.
Option A is incorrect; model selection should be data-driven and problem-appropriate, not based on perceived advancement. Option C describes a rule-based approach, which ML aims to augment or replace for complex patterns. Option D contradicts the efficiency and automation goals of many ML applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

