  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Model training time and computational cost) is a critical practical consideration, as it directly impacts resource allocation and budget. Option B (Model interpretability and explainability) is increasingly important, especially in regulated industries, to understand how a model makes decisions and to build trust. Option C (The specific programming language used for development), Option D (The number of data scientists on the team), and Option E (The version control system utilized for code) are related to development practices and team logistics but are not direct factors for evaluating and selecting the best ML model''s performance or suitability for a business problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_57',
  'Which statements accurately describe offline evaluation of machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It uses historical data to assess model performance before deployment.","It typically involves metrics like AUC-ROC, precision, and recall.","It directly measures the impact of the model on live user experience.","It requires real-time interaction with customers.","It is primarily used for A/B testing in production."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (It uses historical data to assess model performance before deployment) correctly defines offline evaluation, which relies on a static dataset. Option B (It typically involves metrics like AUC-ROC, precision, and recall) is also correct, as these are standard metrics computed on the holdout dataset during offline evaluation. Option C (It directly measures the impact of the model on live user experience) and Option D (It requires real-time interaction with customers) describe online evaluation. Option E (It is primarily used for A/B testing in production) refers to a common online evaluation technique.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_58',
  'Which are valid methods for validating an ML model''s performance on unseen data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using a dedicated holdout dataset","Employing cross-validation techniques","Retraining the model on the entire dataset multiple times","Manually inspecting a few predictions on training data","Deploying the model to production without prior testing"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Using a dedicated holdout dataset) is a fundamental method where a portion of the data is set aside and only used for final evaluation after training. Option B (Employing cross-validation techniques) is another robust method that involves partitioning the data into multiple subsets for training and validation, providing a more reliable estimate of model performance. Option C (Retraining the model on the entire dataset multiple times) does not validate performance on unseen data. Option D (Manually inspecting a few predictions on training data) is insufficient for comprehensive validation. Option E (Deploying the model to production without prior testing) is an irresponsible and incorrect approach.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
