  '["Increasing the learning rate during training","Analyzing the confusion matrix across different demographic groups","Performing A/B testing with diverse user segments","Reducing the number of training epochs","Applying L1 regularization to model weights"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Analyzing the confusion matrix across different demographic groups (Option B) helps identify if the model performs differently (e.g., higher false positive rates) for specific groups, indicating potential bias. Performing A/B testing with diverse user segments (Option C) can reveal if the model''s real-world impact or user experience varies unfairly across different populations. Increasing the learning rate (Option A) and reducing training epochs (Option D) are training adjustments, not bias detection methods. Applying L1 regularization (Option E) is a technique to prevent overfitting and simplify models, not directly for bias detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_71',
  'What are two key benefits of using A/B testing for evaluating machine learning models in production? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It reduces the computational cost of model training.","It allows for real-world performance comparison with actual user interaction.","It helps in identifying the optimal set of hyperparameters offline.","It enables controlled experimentation to measure impact on business metrics.","It simplifies the data preprocessing pipeline for new models."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'A/B testing (Option B) allows for real-world performance comparison with actual user interaction, providing insights into how a model performs in a live environment. It also enables controlled experimentation (Option D) to measure the impact of a new model version on specific business metrics, such as conversion rates or user engagement. Option A is incorrect as A/B testing is a deployment strategy, not a training cost reducer. Option C describes hyperparameter optimization, which is typically an offline process. Option E relates to data engineering, not directly a benefit of A/B testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_72',
  'Which two metrics are most suitable for evaluating the performance of a regression model? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Precision","Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)","Recall","R-squared (Coefficient of Determination)","Accuracy"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) (Option B) is a standard metric for regression models, measuring the average magnitude of the errors. R-squared (Coefficient of Determination) (Option D) indicates the proportion of the variance in the dependent variable that is predictable from the independent variables. Precision (Option A), Recall (Option C), and Accuracy (Option E) are metrics used for classification models, not regression models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_73',
  'Which two components are fundamental elements of a confusion matrix used in classification model evaluation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Learning Rate","True Positives (TP)","False Negatives (FN)","Regularization Strength","Feature Importance"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'A confusion matrix is a table used to describe the performance of a classification model on a set of test data for which the true values are known. True Positives (TP) (Option B) and False Negatives (FN) (Option C) are two of the four fundamental components (along with True Negatives and False Positives). Learning Rate (Option A) and Regularization Strength (Option D) are hyperparameters used during model training. Feature Importance (Option E) is an output of some models, indicating the relevance of features, but not a component of the confusion matrix itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_74',
  'Which two types of evaluation are commonly performed for machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Data ingestion evaluation","Offline evaluation","Feature engineering evaluation","Online evaluation","Model deployment evaluation"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Offline evaluation (Option B) involves assessing model performance using historical datasets, typically during development and before deployment. Online evaluation (Option D), such as A/B testing, assesses model performance in a live production environment with real user traffic. Data ingestion evaluation (Option A), feature engineering evaluation (Option C), and model deployment evaluation (Option E) are related processes but are not the primary categories of model performance evaluation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

