INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_47',
  'Which techniques are commonly used for regularization in machine learning models to prevent overfitting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["L1 regularization (Lasso)","L2 regularization (Ridge)","Cross-validation","Gradient Descent","Batch Normalization"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (L1 regularization (Lasso)) and Option B (L2 regularization (Ridge)) are both widely used techniques to add a penalty term to the loss function, discouraging large weights and thereby reducing model complexity to prevent overfitting. Option C (Cross-validation) is a model evaluation technique, not a regularization method. Option D (Gradient Descent) is an optimization algorithm used to minimize the loss function. Option E (Batch Normalization) is a technique to normalize layer inputs, which can help with training stability and speed, but it''s not primarily a regularization method in the same way L1/L2 are.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_48',
  'When performing hyperparameter optimization, which methods help in finding the best combination of hyperparameters? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Grid Search","Random Search","Feature Scaling","Data Augmentation","One-Hot Encoding"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Grid Search) systematically explores a predefined subset of the hyperparameter space by trying every possible combination. Option B (Random Search) samples hyperparameter values from a specified distribution for a fixed number of iterations, often proving more efficient than grid search for high-dimensional spaces. Option C (Feature Scaling), Option D (Data Augmentation), and Option E (One-Hot Encoding) are data preprocessing techniques, not methods for hyperparameter optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_49',
  'Which statements accurately describe the purpose of cross-validation in machine learning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To obtain a more reliable estimate of model performance on unseen data.","To make better use of limited data for both training and validation.","To reduce the number of features in the dataset.","To speed up the model training process.","To prevent data leakage from the test set to the training set."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (To obtain a more reliable estimate of model performance on unseen data) is a primary goal of cross-validation, as it reduces the variance of the performance estimate compared to a single train-test split. Option B (To make better use of limited data for both training and validation) is also correct, as cross-validation allows each data point to be used for both training and validation across different folds. Option C (To reduce the number of features in the dataset) is a goal of feature selection or dimensionality reduction. Option D (To speed up the model training process) is generally incorrect; cross-validation typically increases training time. Option E (To prevent data leakage from the test set to the training set) is a general best practice, but cross-validation specifically helps in robust evaluation, not solely preventing leakage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_50',
  'When tuning hyperparameters for a neural network, which parameters are commonly adjusted? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Learning rate","Number of hidden layers","Number of CPU cores","Disk storage size","Network bandwidth"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Learning rate) is a critical hyperparameter that controls the step size at each iteration while moving toward a minimum of the loss function. Option B (Number of hidden layers) directly affects the complexity and capacity of the neural network architecture. Option C (Number of CPU cores), Option D (Disk storage size), and Option E (Network bandwidth) are infrastructure or hardware-related parameters, not hyperparameters of the neural network itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_51',
  'Which regularization techniques are often applied to deep learning models to improve generalization and prevent overfitting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Dropout","Early stopping","Weight initialization","Data normalization","Activation function selection"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Dropout) is a regularization technique where randomly selected neurons are ignored during training, preventing complex co-adaptations on the training data. Option B (Early stopping) is another effective regularization technique that involves monitoring the model''s performance on a validation set and stopping training when performance starts to degrade, preventing overfitting. Option C (Weight initialization) is important for training stability but not a regularization technique itself. Option D (Data normalization) is a preprocessing step. Option E (Activation function selection) is an architectural choice, not a regularization method.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_52',
  'Which statements are true about hyperparameter optimization in machine learning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It aims to find the set of hyperparameters that yields the best model performance.","It is an iterative process that often involves experimentation.","It is the same as model training.","It focuses on optimizing the model''s internal weights and biases.","It is only performed once before any model training."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (It aims to find the set of hyperparameters that yields the best model performance) correctly defines the goal of hyperparameter optimization. Option B (It is an iterative process that often involves experimentation) highlights its experimental nature, as finding optimal hyperparameters usually requires multiple trials. Option C (It is the same as model training) is incorrect; model training adjusts internal parameters (weights/biases), while hyperparameter optimization adjusts external parameters. Option D (It focuses on optimizing the model''s internal weights and biases) describes model training. Option E (It is only performed once before any model training) is incorrect; it''s an iterative process that can be refined over time.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_53',
  'Which techniques are commonly used for regularization to prevent overfitting in deep learning models and improve their generalization capabilities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["L1 Regularization","Dropout","Batch Normalization","Data Augmentation","Early Stopping"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'L1 Regularization (Option A), also known as Lasso regularization, adds a penalty equal to the absolute value of the magnitude of coefficients, which can lead to sparse models. Dropout (Option B) is a technique where randomly selected neurons are ignored during training, preventing complex co-adaptations. Batch Normalization (Option C), Data Augmentation (Option D), and Early Stopping (Option E) are also techniques used to improve generalization and prevent overfitting, but L1 and Dropout are direct regularization methods applied to the model''s weights or activations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_54',
  'Which of the following are common hyperparameters to tune for tree-based ensemble models like XGBoost or Random Forests to optimize their performance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Number of estimators (trees)","Maximum depth of individual trees","Activation function","Batch size","Learning rate for neural networks"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'For tree-based ensemble models, the Number of estimators (trees) (Option A) determines how many individual trees are built, impacting model complexity and performance. The Maximum depth of individual trees (Option B) controls the complexity of each tree, influencing bias-variance trade-off. Activation function (Option C) and Batch size (Option D) are hyperparameters specific to neural networks. Learning rate for neural networks (Option E) is also specific to gradient-based optimization in neural networks, not directly for tree-based models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_55',
  'What are the primary benefits of using k-fold cross-validation during machine learning model development to assess performance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It provides a more robust and less biased estimate of model performance.","It helps in reducing the variance of the performance estimate by using all data for validation.","It significantly speeds up the model training process.","It simplifies the process of feature engineering and selection.","It is primarily used for hyperparameter optimization, not performance assessment."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'K-fold cross-validation provides a more robust and less biased estimate of model performance (Option A) compared to a single train-test split, as it evaluates the model on multiple different subsets of the data. It also helps in reducing the variance of the performance estimate (Option B) because each data point gets to be in a validation set exactly once, and in a training set k-1 times. It does not speed up training (Option C) or simplify feature engineering (Option D). While often used *with* hyperparameter optimization, its core benefit is robust performance assessment, not solely HPO (Option E).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_56',
  'A machine learning engineer wants to systematically find the optimal combination of hyperparameters for a complex model being trained on Amazon SageMaker, aiming to improve model performance. Which Amazon SageMaker features can be leveraged for this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)","Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) (Option A) is specifically designed to automate the process of finding the best hyperparameters for a model by running multiple training jobs with different hyperparameter combinations. Amazon SageMaker Experiments (Option B) allows tracking, comparing, and evaluating different ML experiments, including those from HPO jobs, to manage the iterative process of model development. Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth (Option C) is for data labeling. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store (Option D) is for storing and managing features. Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler (Option E) is for data preparation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_57',
  'Which statements accurately describe the role of a learning rate in gradient descent optimization during the training of machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It controls the step size taken in the direction of the negative gradient during parameter updates.","It significantly impacts the convergence speed and stability of the optimization process.","It defines the overall complexity or capacity of the machine learning model.","It specifies the number of training epochs the model will undergo.","It determines the specific loss function used for training."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'The learning rate (Option A) is a crucial hyperparameter in gradient descent, determining the magnitude of the step taken in the direction opposite to the gradient of the loss function. A proper learning rate significantly impacts the convergence speed and stability (Option B) of the optimization process; too high, and the model might overshoot the minimum; too low, and training might be excessively slow. It does not define model complexity (Option C), specify training epochs (Option D), or determine the loss function (Option E).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_58',
  'Which methods are effective for splitting a dataset into training, validation, and test sets to ensure robust model evaluation and prevent data leakage? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Random splitting of data","Stratified splitting (preserving class distribution)","Feature hashing for categorical variables","One-hot encoding for nominal features","Principal Component Analysis (PCA) for dimensionality reduction"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Random splitting of data (Option A) is a fundamental method to create independent training, validation, and test sets, ensuring that the model is evaluated on unseen data. Stratified splitting (Option B) is particularly important for classification problems with imbalanced classes, as it ensures that each split maintains the same proportion of class labels as the original dataset, preventing bias in evaluation. Feature hashing (Option C), one-hot encoding (Option D), and PCA (Option E) are techniques for feature engineering and dimensionality reduction, not for splitting datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_59',
  'Which two techniques are commonly used for regularization in machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Feature scaling","L1 regularization (Lasso)","Cross-validation","Dropout","Gradient boosting"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'L1 regularization (Lasso) (Option B) adds a penalty equal to the absolute value of the magnitude of coefficients, which can lead to sparse models by driving some coefficients to zero. Dropout (Option D) is a regularization technique primarily used in neural networks where randomly selected neurons are ignored during training, preventing complex co-adaptations. Feature scaling (Option A) is a preprocessing step. Cross-validation (Option C) is an evaluation technique. Gradient boosting (Option E) is an ensemble method, not a regularization technique itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_60',
  'What are two primary benefits of employing cross-validation during model training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It reduces the total training time for large datasets.","It provides a more robust estimate of model generalization performance.","It helps in selecting the optimal set of features for the model.","It mitigates the risk of overfitting to the training data.","It automatically performs hyperparameter optimization."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Cross-validation (Option B) provides a more robust estimate of model generalization performance by training and evaluating the model on multiple subsets of the data, reducing the variance of the performance estimate. It also mitigates the risk of overfitting (Option D) by ensuring the model is tested on data it has not seen during training, unlike a simple train-test split. Option A is incorrect as cross-validation typically increases training time. Option C is related to feature selection, not the primary benefit of cross-validation. Option E is incorrect as cross-validation is an evaluation technique, not an optimization method.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_61',
  'Which two are common hyperparameters that need to be tuned for neural networks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Number of decision trees","Learning rate","Number of hidden layers","Regularization type (e.g., L1/L2)","Maximum tree depth"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'For neural networks, the learning rate (Option B) controls the step size at each iteration while moving towards a minimum of the loss function. The number of hidden layers (Option C) determines the depth of the network architecture. The number of decision trees (Option A) and maximum tree depth (Option E) are hyperparameters for tree-based models like Random Forests or XGBoost. Regularization type (Option D) is a broader concept applicable to many models, but specific to neural networks, dropout or L1/L2 strength would be more precise hyperparameters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_62',
  'Which two techniques are effective in preventing overfitting in machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Increasing model complexity significantly","Early stopping during training","Using a larger and more diverse dataset","Reducing the number of training epochs to a very small value","Decreasing the regularization strength"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Early stopping (Option B) is a technique where training is halted when the model''s performance on a validation set starts to degrade, preventing it from learning noise in the training data. Using a larger and more diverse dataset (Option C) helps the model learn more generalizable patterns rather than memorizing specific training examples. Increasing model complexity (Option A) tends to increase overfitting. Reducing training epochs too much (Option D) can lead to underfitting. Decreasing regularization strength (Option E) would make the model more prone to overfitting, as regularization helps constrain model complexity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_63',
  'Which two methods are commonly used for automated hyperparameter optimization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manual trial and error","Grid Search","Feature engineering","Random Search","Data augmentation"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Grid Search (Option B) systematically works through multiple combinations of hyperparameter values, evaluating the model for each combination. Random Search (Option D) samples hyperparameter values from a defined distribution for a fixed number of iterations. Manual trial and error (Option A) is a manual, not automated, method. Feature engineering (Option C) and data augmentation (Option E) are data preparation techniques, not hyperparameter optimization methods.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_64',
  'Which two types of regularization are commonly applied to machine learning models to prevent overfitting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Batch Normalization","L1 Regularization (Lasso)","Cross-validation","L2 Regularization (Ridge)","Data Augmentation"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'L1 Regularization (Lasso) (Option B) and L2 Regularization (Ridge) (Option D) are two common techniques that add a penalty term to the loss function based on the magnitude of the model''s coefficients, discouraging overly complex models and reducing overfitting. Batch Normalization (Option A) is a technique to normalize layer inputs, which can help with training stability but is not primarily a regularization method in the same sense as L1/L2. Cross-validation (Option C) is an evaluation method. Data Augmentation (Option E) is a technique to increase the diversity of training data, which helps reduce overfitting but is not a form of regularization applied to model weights.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

