INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_39',
  'When training machine learning models, which strategies are commonly used for splitting datasets? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Training, validation, and test sets","K-fold cross-validation","Online A/B testing","Feature engineering","Hyperparameter tuning"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Training, validation, and test sets) is a standard approach where data is partitioned into three distinct sets: one for model training, one for hyperparameter tuning and model selection, and a final unseen set for unbiased evaluation. Option B (K-fold cross-validation) is another robust strategy that divides the dataset into ''k'' equal folds, using ''k-1'' folds for training and one for validation, iterating ''k'' times to get a more reliable performance estimate. Option C (Online A/B testing) is an evaluation method for deployed models, not a dataset splitting strategy for training. Option D (Feature engineering) is a data preparation step. Option E (Hyperparameter tuning) is the process of finding optimal model parameters, not a data splitting strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_40',
  'Which optimization algorithms are commonly used to train machine learning models by iteratively adjusting model parameters? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Gradient Descent","Adam","K-Means","DBSCAN","Principal Component Analysis (PCA)"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Gradient Descent) is a foundational optimization algorithm that iteratively moves towards the minimum of the loss function by taking steps proportional to the negative of the gradient. Option B (Adam) is an adaptive learning rate optimization algorithm that computes adaptive learning rates for each parameter, making it highly efficient and widely used, especially in deep learning. Option C (K-Means) and Option D (DBSCAN) are clustering algorithms (unsupervised learning). Option E (Principal Component Analysis (PCA)) is a dimensionality reduction technique.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_41',
  'Which compute resources are primarily utilized for training deep learning models efficiently, especially with large datasets? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Graphics Processing Units (GPUs)","Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)","Standard Central Processing Units (CPUs)","Hard Disk Drives (HDDs)","Network Load Balancers"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Graphics Processing Units (GPUs)) are highly parallel processors that excel at the matrix multiplications and additions central to deep learning, significantly accelerating training. Option B (Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)) are custom-built ASICs by Google specifically designed to accelerate machine learning workloads, particularly for neural networks. Option C (Standard Central Processing Units (CPUs)) can train deep learning models but are significantly slower for large-scale tasks due to their sequential processing nature. Option D (Hard Disk Drives (HDDs)) are storage devices, not compute resources. Option E (Network Load Balancers) distribute network traffic and are irrelevant to model training computation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_42',
  'When training machine learning models, which are common types of loss functions used to measure the difference between predicted and actual values? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Mean Squared Error (MSE)","Cross-Entropy Loss","Accuracy","Precision","F1-Score"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Mean Squared Error (MSE)) is a common loss function used in regression problems, calculating the average of the squared differences between predictions and actual values. Option B (Cross-Entropy Loss) is a widely used loss function for classification problems, especially in neural networks, measuring the performance of a classification model whose output is a probability value between 0 and 1. Option C (Accuracy), Option D (Precision), and Option E (F1-Score) are evaluation metrics, not loss functions used during training to guide optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_43',
  'Which practices are important for handling large datasets efficiently during ML model training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using data streaming techniques to load data in batches","Leveraging distributed storage and processing frameworks","Loading the entire dataset into memory at once","Manually copying data between instances","Avoiding any form of data compression"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Using data streaming techniques to load data in batches) is crucial for large datasets, as it prevents memory overload and allows models to train on subsets of data. Option B (Leveraging distributed storage and processing frameworks) like Apache Spark or Dask helps in parallelizing data loading and preprocessing across multiple nodes, which is essential for large-scale data. Option C (Loading the entire dataset into memory at once) is often impossible or inefficient for large datasets. Option D (Manually copying data between instances) is inefficient and error-prone. Option E (Avoiding any form of data compression) is incorrect; compression can significantly reduce storage and transfer costs for large datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_44',
  'When monitoring training metrics, which indicators suggest that a machine learning model might be overfitting? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Training loss continues to decrease while validation loss starts to increase.","Training accuracy is very high, but validation accuracy is significantly lower.","Both training and validation loss are consistently high.","Both training and validation accuracy are very low.","Validation loss is lower than training loss."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Training loss continues to decrease while validation loss starts to increase) is a classic sign of overfitting, where the model performs well on the training data but fails to generalize to unseen validation data. Option B (Training accuracy is very high, but validation accuracy is significantly lower) also indicates overfitting, as the model has memorized the training data rather than learning general patterns. Option C (Both training and validation loss are consistently high) and Option D (Both training and validation accuracy are very low) suggest underfitting or a poorly performing model in general. Option E (Validation loss is lower than training loss) is an unusual scenario and usually indicates an issue with data splitting or evaluation setup, not typical overfitting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_45',
  'Which compute resources are typically preferred for training large-scale deep learning models due to their parallel processing capabilities and efficiency with tensor operations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["GPUs (Graphics Processing Units)","Specialized ML Accelerators (e.g., AWS Trainium)","CPUs (Central Processing Units)","AWS Graviton Processors","AWS Lambda"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'GPUs (Graphics Processing Units) (Option A) are widely preferred for deep learning training because their architecture is highly optimized for parallel processing of matrix and tensor operations, which are fundamental to neural networks. Specialized ML Accelerators like AWS Trainium (Option B) are purpose-built by AWS to deliver high-performance and cost-effective deep learning training. CPUs (Option C) and AWS Graviton Processors (Option D) are general-purpose processors, less efficient for the highly parallel computations required by deep learning. AWS Lambda (Option E) is a serverless compute service, suitable for event-driven functions but not typically for large-scale, long-running deep learning training jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_46',
  'Which of the following are common optimization algorithms used during the training of machine learning models to iteratively minimize the loss function? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)","Adam","K-Means Clustering","Principal Component Analysis (PCA)","Decision Trees"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) (Option A) is a fundamental optimization algorithm that updates model parameters using the gradient of the loss function computed on a single training example or a small batch. Adam (Option B) is an adaptive learning rate optimization algorithm that combines the benefits of RMSProp and AdaGrad, often achieving faster convergence. K-Means Clustering (Option C) and Principal Component Analysis (PCA) (Option D) are unsupervised learning algorithms, not optimization algorithms. Decision Trees (Option E) are a type of machine learning model, not an optimization algorithm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_47',
  'Which AWS services provide managed environments or frameworks that facilitate distributed training of machine learning models, allowing for faster training on large datasets? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker","AWS Batch","Amazon EC2","AWS Lambda","Amazon SQS"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Amazon SageMaker (Option A) provides built-in support for distributed training, allowing users to easily configure and run training jobs across multiple instances. AWS Batch (Option B) can be used to efficiently run distributed training jobs by orchestrating containers on a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. Amazon EC2 (Option C) provides the underlying compute instances but requires manual setup for distributed training. AWS Lambda (Option D) is a serverless compute service not typically used for large-scale distributed training. Amazon SQS (Option E) is a message queuing service, not a training environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_48',
  'A data scientist is training a large deep learning model and needs to monitor its performance metrics (e.g., loss, accuracy, validation error) in real-time during the training process. Which AWS services can be used for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon S3","AWS Glue","Amazon Kinesis"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Amazon CloudWatch (Option A) is a monitoring and observability service that can collect and track metrics, logs, and events from AWS resources, including SageMaker training jobs, allowing for real-time monitoring. Amazon SageMaker Experiments (Option B) is specifically designed to track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments, including logging metrics and parameters during training. Amazon S3 (Option C) is object storage, not for real-time monitoring. AWS Glue (Option D) is an ETL service. Amazon Kinesis (Option E) is for real-time data streaming, but not directly for monitoring ML training metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_49',
  'A company has a massive dataset (multiple terabytes) stored in Amazon S3 for training a new ML model. They need to efficiently process and transform this data at scale before feeding it into the model. Which AWS services can handle this scalable data preparation? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Glue","Amazon EMR","AWS Lambda","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon Athena"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'AWS Glue (Option A) is a serverless data integration service that makes it easy to discover, prepare, and combine data for analytics and machine learning, suitable for large-scale ETL jobs. Amazon EMR (Option B) provides a managed Hadoop framework, allowing for scalable data processing using tools like Spark, Hive, and Presto, ideal for big data transformations. AWS Lambda (Option C) is for event-driven, short-duration functions and not typically for multi-terabyte data processing. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose (Option D) is for streaming data ingestion. Amazon Athena (Option E) is a query service for S3 data, not primarily for large-scale transformations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_50',
  'What are the primary purposes of a validation set during the machine learning model training process? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["To tune hyperparameters and make model architecture decisions.","To evaluate model performance on unseen data during the training phase.","To provide the final, unbiased evaluation of the model''s generalization.","To train the model''s parameters and learn patterns from the data.","To preprocess raw data and perform feature engineering."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'The validation set is used to tune hyperparameters (Option A) and make decisions about the model architecture without touching the test set. It also serves to evaluate model performance on unseen data (Option B) during the iterative training process, helping to detect overfitting and guide adjustments. The final, unbiased evaluation (Option C) is reserved for the test set. Training the model''s parameters (Option D) is done on the training set. Preprocessing and feature engineering (Option E) are done before splitting the data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_51',
  'Which statements accurately describe how a loss function is used during the training of a machine learning model? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It quantifies the error or discrepancy between the model''s predicted outputs and the actual target values.","It guides the optimization algorithm (e.g., gradient descent) to iteratively update the model''s parameters.","It determines the specific architecture or layers of the neural network model.","It specifies the input features that the model will use for prediction.","It defines the learning rate used by the optimization algorithm."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'A loss function (Option A) is a mathematical function that quantifies the error or discrepancy between the model''s predicted outputs and the actual ground truth values. This error signal is then used to guide the optimization algorithm (Option B), such as gradient descent, which adjusts the model''s internal parameters (weights and biases) in a direction that minimizes this loss. The loss function does not determine the model''s architecture (Option C), specify input features (Option D), or define the learning rate (Option E); these are separate design choices or hyperparameters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_52',
  'Which two common strategies are used for splitting data when training machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Feature engineering and selection","Training, validation, and test sets","Cross-validation","Data augmentation","Hyperparameter optimization"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'The most common strategy is to split data into training, validation, and test sets (Option B) to develop and evaluate the model. Cross-validation (Option C) is another robust strategy where the data is partitioned into multiple folds, and the model is trained and evaluated iteratively on different combinations of these folds. Feature engineering (Option A) and data augmentation (Option D) are data preparation techniques. Hyperparameter optimization (Option E) is about tuning model parameters, not data splitting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_53',
  'Which two optimization algorithms are commonly used for training deep learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Linear Regression","Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)","K-Means Clustering","Adam","Decision Trees"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) (Option B) is a foundational optimization algorithm that updates model parameters based on the gradient of the loss function computed on a single random sample or a small batch of samples. Adam (Option D) is an adaptive learning rate optimization algorithm that combines the benefits of RMSProp and AdaGrad, widely popular for deep learning. Linear Regression (Option A), K-Means Clustering (Option C), and Decision Trees (Option E) are types of models or algorithms themselves, not optimization algorithms used for training deep learning models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_54',
  'Which two compute resources are commonly leveraged for training large-scale machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3 buckets","AWS Lambda functions","Amazon EC2 instances with GPUs","AWS Glue Data Catalog","Amazon SageMaker training jobs"]'::json,
  '{2,4}',
  'Amazon EC2 instances with GPUs (Option C) provide powerful parallel processing capabilities essential for accelerating the training of large deep learning models. Amazon SageMaker training jobs (Option E) offer a fully managed service for training ML models at scale, abstracting away infrastructure management and supporting various compute options including GPUs. Amazon S3 buckets (Option A) are for object storage, AWS Lambda functions (Option B) are for serverless compute of short-duration tasks, and AWS Glue Data Catalog (Option D) is a metadata repository, none of which are primary compute resources for large-scale model training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_55',
  'What are two key benefits of using distributed training for machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It simplifies the model architecture by reducing complexity.","It enables training of models that do not fit into a single machine''s memory.","It always guarantees faster training times regardless of model size.","It allows for faster iteration and experimentation with hyperparameters.","It can significantly reduce the total time required for training large models."]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Distributed training enables the training of models that do not fit into a single machine''s memory (Option B) by distributing the model or data across multiple machines. It can also significantly reduce the total time required for training large models (Option E) by parallelizing computations. Option A is incorrect; distributed training adds complexity. Option C is incorrect; while often faster, it''s not guaranteed for all models or configurations. Option D is a benefit of efficient experimentation pipelines, not solely distributed training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_56',
  'Which two methods are effective for monitoring the training progress of a machine learning model? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manually adjusting feature weights during training.","Observing the loss function on the training set over epochs.","Tracking evaluation metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1 score) on a validation set.","Performing A/B testing in a production environment.","Changing the model''s architecture mid-training."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Observing the loss function on the training set over epochs (Option B) helps understand if the model is learning and converging. Tracking evaluation metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1 score) on a validation set (Option C) is crucial to assess generalization performance and detect overfitting. Manually adjusting feature weights (Option A) and changing the model''s architecture mid-training (Option E) are not standard monitoring practices and can disrupt training. Performing A/B testing (Option D) is for online model evaluation in production, not monitoring training progress.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_57',
  'Which two strategies are effective for handling large datasets during the training phase of a machine learning model? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Loading the entire dataset into memory at once.","Using mini-batch gradient descent.","Implementing distributed training across multiple compute instances.","Reducing the number of features without careful selection.","Training the model on a small, unrepresentative subset of the data."]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Using mini-batch gradient descent (Option B) allows models to be trained on large datasets by processing data in smaller, manageable chunks, which is more memory-efficient than full batch gradient descent. Implementing distributed training across multiple compute instances (Option C) is essential for datasets that are too large to fit on a single machine or to speed up training by parallelizing computations. Loading the entire dataset into memory (Option A) is often not feasible for large datasets. Reducing features arbitrarily (Option D) can lead to loss of information. Training on a small, unrepresentative subset (Option E) can lead to a biased or underfit model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_3_58',
  'Which two are common types of loss functions used in machine learning model training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Feature Scaling","Mean Squared Error (MSE)","Cross-Entropy Loss","Data Augmentation","Principal Component Analysis (PCA)"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Mean Squared Error (MSE) (Option B) is a common loss function for regression problems, measuring the average of the squares of the errors. Cross-Entropy Loss (Option C) is widely used for classification problems, particularly in neural networks, quantifying the difference between predicted and true probability distributions. Feature Scaling (Option A) and Data Augmentation (Option D) are data preprocessing techniques. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) (Option E) is a dimensionality reduction technique.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

