  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_90',
  'A data scientist is preparing a time series dataset for visualization to identify trends and seasonality. Order the steps to ensure the data is correctly formatted and ready for plotting.',
  'ordering',
  '["Convert the timestamp column to a datetime object with the correct format.","Ensure the time series data is sorted chronologically by the timestamp.","Identify the timestamp column and the value column(s) in the dataset.","Resample or aggregate the data if necessary to a suitable frequency for visualization."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Preparing time series data for visualization begins by identifying the timestamp column and the value columns that represent the series (Option C). The timestamp column must then be converted to a proper datetime object, ensuring the correct format for accurate time-based operations (Option A). It''s crucial to ensure the data is sorted chronologically by the timestamp to correctly represent trends (Option B). Finally, resampling or aggregating the data to a suitable frequency (e.g., daily, weekly) might be necessary to make the visualization clear and interpretable, especially for very granular data (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_91',
  'A data science team is tasked with identifying customer segments for a marketing campaign. They have collected transactional data stored in Amazon S3 and need to perform unsupervised learning to discover natural groupings. The team wants to use Amazon SageMaker to perform K-means clustering and then visualize the results to understand the distinct customer profiles. They need to ensure the data is prepared and the clustering results are interpretable for business stakeholders. Order the following steps to achieve this objective, from initial data preparation to result visualization.',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch an Amazon SageMaker Notebook instance to process the data and run the K-means algorithm.","Load the processed data into Amazon SageMaker for training the K-means clustering model.","Perform feature engineering and dimensionality reduction on the raw transactional data.","Visualize the cluster assignments and centroids using a scatter plot or other appropriate charts."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'The correct sequence begins with preparing the data. Option C (Perform feature engineering and dimensionality reduction on the raw transactional data) is the initial step to transform raw data into a suitable format for clustering. Next, Option A (Launch an Amazon SageMaker Notebook instance to process the data and run the K-means algorithm) provides the environment for executing the ML workflow. Following data processing, Option B (Load the processed data into Amazon SageMaker for training the K-means clustering model) involves using SageMaker''s managed services for the actual model training. Finally, Option D (Visualize the cluster assignments and centroids using a scatter plot or other appropriate charts) is crucial for interpreting the clustering results and communicating insights to stakeholders.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_92',
  'A retail company wants to analyze customer purchase patterns over time to forecast demand for specific product categories. They have historical sales data stored in Amazon S3 and wish to use Amazon QuickSight for interactive visualization and preliminary trend analysis. The goal is to identify seasonal trends and potential anomalies before building a predictive model. Order the steps required to set up this data analysis and visualization workflow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new analysis in Amazon QuickSight and build visualizations such as time series charts and histograms.","Configure an Amazon Athena table to query the sales data directly from Amazon S3.","Grant Amazon QuickSight permissions to access the Amazon Athena data source.","Upload the historical sales data, including timestamps and product categories, to an Amazon S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'The process starts with data ingestion. Option D (Upload the historical sales data, including timestamps and product categories, to an Amazon S3 bucket) is the first step to ensure the data is available in a scalable storage solution. Next, Option B (Configure an Amazon Athena table to query the sales data directly from Amazon S3) enables SQL-based querying of the S3 data without needing to load it into a database. Then, Option C (Grant Amazon QuickSight permissions to access the Amazon Athena data source) establishes the necessary security and connectivity between QuickSight and Athena. Finally, Option A (Create a new analysis in Amazon QuickSight and build visualizations such as time series charts and histograms) allows for interactive exploration and visualization of the data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_93',
  'A financial institution is developing a fraud detection model and needs to understand the distribution of transaction amounts and user behavior metrics. They have a large dataset in Amazon S3 and want to use statistical summaries and visualizations to identify potential outliers and skewed distributions. This analysis will guide feature selection and potential data transformations. Order the following steps to perform this exploratory data analysis effectively using AWS services.',
  'ordering',
  '["Generate descriptive statistics (mean, median, standard deviation) for key numerical features.","Create scatter plots and box plots to visualize relationships and identify outliers.","Load the dataset into an Amazon SageMaker processing job for data profiling.","Store the raw transaction data in a structured format, such as Parquet, in Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{3,2,0,1}',
  'The foundational step is data storage. Option D (Store the raw transaction data in a structured format, such as Parquet, in Amazon S3) ensures the data is in a suitable and efficient format for processing. Next, Option C (Load the dataset into an Amazon SageMaker processing job for data profiling) provides a scalable environment to perform initial data analysis. Following this, Option A (Generate descriptive statistics (mean, median, standard deviation) for key numerical features) provides quantitative insights into the data''s central tendency and spread. Finally, Option B (Create scatter plots and box plots to visualize relationships and identify outliers) offers visual confirmation and deeper insights into data distributions and anomalies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_94',
  'An e-commerce company wants to analyze customer review sentiment and identify common themes. They have a large collection of customer reviews stored as text files in Amazon S3. Before applying any machine learning models, the data science team needs to perform initial text analysis, including word frequency counts and basic sentiment distribution visualization. They aim to use AWS services for efficient processing and visualization. Order the steps to perform this preliminary text data analysis.',
  'ordering',
  '["Use an Amazon SageMaker processing job to perform tokenization and calculate word frequencies.","Ingest the raw customer review text files into an Amazon S3 bucket.","Load the processed word frequency data into Amazon QuickSight for visualization.","Create a data catalog in AWS Glue Data Catalog for the S3 data to enable querying."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'The initial step is data ingestion. Option B (Ingest the raw customer review text files into an Amazon S3 bucket) makes the data available in the AWS ecosystem. Next, Option D (Create a data catalog in AWS Glue Data Catalog for the S3 data to enable querying) is essential for defining the schema and making the data queryable by other services. Following this, Option A (Use an Amazon SageMaker processing job to perform tokenization and calculate word frequencies) provides a scalable way to extract meaningful features from the text. Finally, Option C (Load the processed word frequency data into Amazon QuickSight for visualization) allows for interactive exploration of the text analysis results.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_95',
  'A healthcare provider is analyzing patient medical records to identify patterns related to disease progression. They have anonymized patient data, including various numerical and categorical features, stored in Amazon S3. The data science team wants to perform hierarchical clustering to group similar patients and then visualize the dendrogram to understand the relationships between clusters. Order the following steps to execute this hierarchical clustering analysis workflow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Normalize numerical features and encode categorical features in the dataset.","Store the anonymized patient medical records in a secure Amazon S3 bucket.","Perform hierarchical clustering using a SageMaker processing job or a custom script.","Visualize the resulting dendrogram to interpret patient groupings and relationships."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The first step is data storage. Option B (Store the anonymized patient medical records in a secure Amazon S3 bucket) ensures the data is securely available. Next, Option A (Normalize numerical features and encode categorical features in the dataset) is crucial for preparing the data for clustering algorithms, as they are sensitive to feature scales and types. Following this, Option C (Perform hierarchical clustering using a SageMaker processing job or a custom script) executes the clustering algorithm on the prepared data. Finally, Option D (Visualize the resulting dendrogram to interpret patient groupings and relationships) is essential for understanding the hierarchical structure of the clusters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_96',
  'A manufacturing company collects sensor data from machinery to predict equipment failures. The data arrives in real-time and is stored in Amazon S3. The data engineering team needs to perform initial data quality checks, including identifying missing values and inconsistent readings, and visualize these issues using Amazon QuickSight. This will help them understand the data''s reliability before building a predictive maintenance model. Order the steps to set up this data quality analysis and visualization pipeline.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon Athena table over the S3 data to enable SQL queries for data quality checks.","Develop a dashboard in Amazon QuickSight to visualize missing values and data inconsistencies.","Ingest sensor data streams into Amazon S3 in a structured format.","Use SQL queries in Athena to identify and quantify data quality issues like nulls or out-of-range values."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'The process begins with data ingestion. Option C (Ingest sensor data streams into Amazon S3 in a structured format) ensures the data is collected and stored. Next, Option A (Create an Amazon Athena table over the S3 data to enable SQL queries for data quality checks) allows for easy querying of the raw data. Following this, Option D (Use SQL queries in Athena to identify and quantify data quality issues like nulls or out-of-range values) performs the actual data quality assessment. Finally, Option B (Develop a dashboard in Amazon QuickSight to visualize missing values and data inconsistencies) provides an intuitive way to monitor and report on data quality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_97',
  'A marketing analytics team wants to analyze customer demographics and engagement metrics to identify distinct customer personas. They have a dataset containing various customer attributes, including age, income, and website interaction frequency, stored in Amazon S3. The team plans to use K-means clustering to group customers and then visualize the characteristics of each cluster to inform targeted marketing strategies. Order the following steps to execute this customer segmentation and visualization workflow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the optimal number of clusters (k) using the elbow method or silhouette score.","Preprocess the customer data, including scaling numerical features and encoding categorical features.","Store the raw customer demographic and engagement data in Amazon S3.","Train a K-means clustering model on the prepared data using Amazon SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'The initial step is data storage. Option C (Store the raw customer demographic and engagement data in Amazon S3) ensures the data is available for processing. Next, Option B (Preprocess the customer data, including scaling numerical features and encoding categorical features) prepares the data for clustering algorithms, which are sensitive to feature scales and types. Following this, Option A (Select the optimal number of clusters (k) using the elbow method or silhouette score) is a critical step in K-means clustering to determine the appropriate number of groups. Finally, Option D (Train a K-means clustering model on the prepared data using Amazon SageMaker) executes the clustering algorithm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

