  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_86',
  'After training a machine learning model, a data scientist wants to understand which features contributed most to its predictions. Order the steps to evaluate feature importance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select a feature importance method (e.g., permutation importance, SHAP values, model-specific importance).","Train the machine learning model on the prepared dataset.","Interpret the results to identify the most influential features.","Apply the chosen method to the trained model and dataset."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Evaluating feature importance starts with training the machine learning model on the prepared dataset (Option B), as importance is typically derived from a trained model. Next, a suitable feature importance method is selected, such as permutation importance, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) values, or model-specific importance (e.g., from tree-based models) (Option A). The chosen method is then applied to the trained model and the dataset to compute importance scores (Option D). Finally, these results are interpreted to identify which features had the most significant impact on the model''s predictions (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_87',
  'A natural language processing (NLP) team is preparing a dataset of customer feedback for a sentiment analysis model. The raw feedback is stored as unstructured text in Amazon S3. Before training, they need to convert the text into numerical features using tokenization and then create word embeddings. This process requires careful ordering to ensure the text is properly preprocessed for the embedding model. Order the following steps to perform text feature engineering for word embeddings.',
  'ordering',
  '["Train a Word2Vec or similar embedding model on the tokenized text using Amazon SageMaker.","Store the raw customer feedback text files in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Perform text cleaning, such as lowercasing and removing punctuation, on the raw text.","Tokenize the cleaned text into individual words or subword units."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'The process begins with data ingestion. Option B (Store the raw customer feedback text files in an Amazon S3 bucket) makes the raw data available. Next, Option C (Perform text cleaning, such as lowercasing and removing punctuation, on the raw text) is essential to standardize the text and remove noise. Following this, Option D (Tokenize the cleaned text into individual words or subword units) breaks down the text into discrete units suitable for embedding. Finally, Option A (Train a Word2Vec or similar embedding model on the tokenized text using Amazon SageMaker) generates the numerical representations (embeddings) for the tokens.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_88',
  'A marketing department wants to predict customer churn based on demographic and behavioral data. Their dataset includes a ''CustomerAge'' feature, which is a continuous numerical variable. To improve model performance and handle potential non-linear relationships, the data science team decides to convert ''CustomerAge'' into categorical bins. They need to define appropriate age ranges and then apply this transformation. Order the steps to perform numerical feature binning using Amazon SageMaker.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the bin boundaries (e.g., age ranges like 18-25, 26-35) for the ''CustomerAge'' feature.","Store the raw customer data, including ''CustomerAge'', in an Amazon S3 bucket.","Apply the defined binning strategy to the ''CustomerAge'' feature using a SageMaker processing job.","Verify the new binned feature''s distribution and impact on model performance."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The initial step is data storage. Option B (Store the raw customer data, including ''CustomerAge'', in an Amazon S3 bucket) ensures the data is accessible. Next, Option A (Define the bin boundaries (e.g., age ranges like 18-25, 26-35) for the ''CustomerAge'' feature) is a crucial design step for the binning strategy. Following this, Option C (Apply the defined binning strategy to the ''CustomerAge'' feature using a SageMaker processing job) executes the transformation on the dataset. Finally, Option D (Verify the new binned feature''s distribution and impact on model performance) is essential to ensure the feature engineering step was effective and beneficial.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_89',
  'An analytics team is preparing a dataset for a classification model that predicts loan default. The dataset contains several categorical features, such as ''EmploymentType'' (e.g., ''Salaried'', ''Self-Employed'', ''Unemployed'') and ''EducationLevel''. To make these features suitable for most machine learning algorithms, which typically require numerical input, the team decides to apply one-hot encoding. Order the steps to correctly apply one-hot encoding to categorical features using Amazon SageMaker.',
  'ordering',
  '["Store the raw loan application data, including categorical features, in Amazon S3.","Apply one-hot encoding to the selected categorical features using a SageMaker processing job.","Identify the categorical features that require one-hot encoding.","Verify the transformed dataset to ensure new binary columns are correctly generated."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'The process begins with data storage. Option A (Store the raw loan application data, including categorical features, in Amazon S3) makes the data available for processing. Next, Option C (Identify the categorical features that require one-hot encoding) is a necessary analytical step to determine which features need transformation. Following this, Option B (Apply one-hot encoding to the selected categorical features using a SageMaker processing job) executes the transformation in a scalable manner. Finally, Option D (Verify the transformed dataset to ensure new binary columns are correctly generated) is crucial for data quality assurance before model training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_90',
  'A data science team is developing a recommendation engine for an online streaming service. They have user interaction data (e.g., views, likes) and movie metadata (e.g., genre, actors) stored in Amazon S3. To enhance the model''s ability to capture complex relationships, they decide to create synthetic features by combining existing ones, such as ''UserEngagementScore'' from views and likes, or ''GenrePopularity'' from movie genre and overall view counts. Order the steps to create synthetic features for a tabular dataset using Amazon SageMaker.',
  'ordering',
  '["Store the raw user interaction and movie metadata in Amazon S3.","Define the logic for creating new synthetic features by combining existing features.","Implement and execute a SageMaker processing job to generate the synthetic features.","Evaluate the impact of the new synthetic features on the recommendation model''s performance."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The process begins with data storage. Option A (Store the raw user interaction and movie metadata in Amazon S3) makes the base data available. Next, Option B (Define the logic for creating new synthetic features by combining existing features) is the design phase where the feature engineering strategy is formulated. Following this, Option C (Implement and execute a SageMaker processing job to generate the synthetic features) performs the actual feature creation in a scalable environment. Finally, Option D (Evaluate the impact of the new synthetic features on the recommendation model''s performance) is crucial to validate the effectiveness of the feature engineering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_91',
  'A pharmaceutical company is building a model to predict drug efficacy based on patient genetic markers and clinical trial data. They have a large dataset in Amazon S3 with hundreds of features, and not all of them are equally important for prediction. To optimize model complexity and interpretability, the data science team needs to evaluate feature importance before final model training. Order the steps to effectively evaluate feature importance using Amazon SageMaker.',
  'ordering',
  '["Train an initial machine learning model (e.g., XGBoost) on the dataset using Amazon SageMaker.","Store the raw patient genetic markers and clinical trial data in Amazon S3.","Use SageMaker Clarify or a custom script to compute and rank feature importance scores.","Analyze the feature importance scores to select the most impactful features for the final model."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The initial step is data storage. Option B (Store the raw patient genetic markers and clinical trial data in Amazon S3) ensures the data is accessible. Next, Option A (Train an initial machine learning model (e.g., XGBoost) on the dataset using Amazon SageMaker) is required because feature importance is often derived from a trained model. Following this, Option C (Use SageMaker Clarify or a custom script to compute and rank feature importance scores) calculates the importance of each feature. Finally, Option D (Analyze the feature importance scores to select the most impactful features for the final model) uses these insights to refine the feature set.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_92',
  'An anomaly detection system for network security is being developed, relying on network traffic logs. The logs, stored in Amazon S3, contain various numerical metrics, but some entries exhibit extreme values (outliers) that could skew model training. The data science team decides to handle these outliers by capping them at a certain percentile to prevent them from unduly influencing the model. Order the steps to effectively handle outliers during feature engineering using Amazon SageMaker.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the features containing significant outliers using statistical methods or visualizations.","Store the raw network traffic logs in Amazon S3.","Apply a capping or transformation strategy (e.g., winsorization) to the identified outliers using a SageMaker processing job.","Evaluate the impact of outlier handling on the distribution of features and model performance."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The process begins with data storage. Option B (Store the raw network traffic logs in Amazon S3) makes the data available. Next, Option A (Identify the features containing significant outliers using statistical methods or visualizations) is crucial for pinpointing which features need outlier treatment. Following this, Option C (Apply a capping or transformation strategy (e.g., winsorization) to the identified outliers using a SageMaker processing job) executes the chosen outlier handling method. Finally, Option D (Evaluate the impact of outlier handling on the distribution of features and model performance) is essential to confirm the effectiveness and positive impact of the transformation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_93',
  'A large-scale advertising platform needs to process billions of user impression logs daily to extract features for a click-through rate (CTR) prediction model. The raw logs are in Amazon S3. The data engineering team wants to automate the entire feature engineering pipeline, including parsing, cleaning, and transforming logs into a structured format with specific features like ''hour of day'' and ''device type''. They plan to use Amazon SageMaker Processing for this purpose. Order the steps to automate a feature engineering pipeline for this scenario.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the feature engineering logic, including parsing, cleaning, and transformations, in a Python script.","Store the raw user impression logs in Amazon S3.","Configure and launch an Amazon SageMaker Processing job to execute the feature engineering script on the S3 data.","Verify the output features in S3 for correctness and schema compliance."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The initial step is data storage. Option B (Store the raw user impression logs in Amazon S3) ensures the raw data is accessible. Next, Option A (Define the feature engineering logic, including parsing, cleaning, and transformations, in a Python script) is where the core logic for the pipeline is developed. Following this, Option C (Configure and launch an Amazon SageMaker Processing job to execute the feature engineering script on the S3 data) provides a scalable and managed environment to run the script. Finally, Option D (Verify the output features in S3 for correctness and schema compliance) is crucial for ensuring the quality of the engineered features before model training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

