  'A Line Plot (Option A in left) is the most fundamental visualization for time series data, effectively displaying trends, cycles, and overall patterns over time. The Autocorrelation Function (ACF) (Option B in left) helps identify dependencies between observations at different time lags, which is crucial for understanding seasonality and autoregressive components. Seasonal Decomposition (Option C in left) is a technique that separates a time series into its underlying components: trend, seasonal, and residual, allowing for a clearer understanding of each element''s contribution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  19,
  '{"left":["Line Plot","Autocorrelation Function (ACF)","Seasonal Decomposition"],"right":["Display trends and patterns over time","Identify dependencies between observations at different lags","Separate time series into trend, seasonal, and residual components"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_117',
  'A manufacturing company collects sensor data from machinery to predict equipment failures. Before building predictive models, the data science team needs to diagnose various data quality issues that could impact model performance. Match each data issue to the most effective visualization for its detection.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Outliers","B":"Skewness","C":"Missing Values"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Outliers (Option A in left) are most effectively detected using a Box plot, which visually represents the interquartile range and points outside the whiskers, or a Scatter plot, where isolated points can be easily spotted. Skewness (Option B in left), which describes the asymmetry of a distribution, is best identified with a Histogram, showing the shape of the data, or a Q-Q plot, comparing the data''s quantiles against a theoretical distribution. Missing Values (Option C in left) can be visualized using a heatmap of missingness, showing patterns of missing data across features, or a bar chart indicating the count of missing values per feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  20,
  '{"left":["Outliers","Skewness","Missing Values"],"right":["Box plot or Scatter plot","Histogram or Q-Q plot","Heatmap of missingness or Bar chart"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_118',
  'A data science team is iterating on several machine learning models for a new product recommendation system. They need a robust way to track different experiments, compare model performance metrics, and visualize the impact of various hyperparameters and feature sets. Match each SageMaker service or concept to its primary role in this experimental workflow.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Experiments","B":"Amazon SageMaker Studio","C":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Amazon SageMaker Experiments (Option A in left) is specifically designed to track, compare, and analyze machine learning experiments, including different model versions, hyperparameters, and datasets. Amazon SageMaker Studio (Option B in left) provides an integrated development environment (IDE) for the entire machine learning workflow, including data preparation, model building, training, and deployment. Amazon SageMaker Clarify (Option C in left) is used for detecting bias in ML models and datasets, and for explaining model predictions, which is typically a post-training evaluation step rather than an experimental tracking tool.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  21,
  '{"left":["Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon SageMaker Studio","Amazon SageMaker Clarify"],"right":["Track, compare, and analyze ML experiments","Integrated development environment for ML","Detect bias and explain model predictions"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_3_119',
  'A retail company is using Amazon SageMaker to build a customer churn prediction model. After preparing their data in Amazon S3, they need to perform interactive data exploration and create dashboards to visualize customer segments and churn drivers. Match each AWS service to its primary capability for this visualization task.',
