INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_107',
  'A company is building a natural language processing (NLP) model to categorize customer feedback. The raw text data contains various linguistic nuances that need to be converted into a numerical format suitable for machine learning algorithms. Match each feature engineering technique to its primary method of extracting features from text.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Tokenization","B":"Word Embeddings","C":"TF-IDF"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Tokenization (Option A in left) is the process of breaking down raw text into smaller units called tokens, which can be words, subwords, or characters. Word Embeddings (Option B in left) represent words as dense, low-dimensional vectors in a continuous vector space, capturing semantic relationships between words. TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency) (Option C in left) is a statistical measure that evaluates how relevant a word is to a document in a collection of documents, by weighting words based on their frequency in a document and their rarity across all documents.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  37,
  '{"left":["Tokenization","Word Embeddings","TF-IDF"],"right":["Breaking text into individual words or subwords","Representing words as dense vectors in a continuous space","Weighting words by their frequency and inverse document frequency"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_108',
  'A data science team is working on a credit risk assessment model, and the dataset contains several numerical features with extreme values that could disproportionately influence the model. They need to apply appropriate techniques to handle these outliers without losing valuable information. Match each outlier handling strategy to its common approach.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Trimming/Capping","B":"Imputation with Median","C":"Log Transformation"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Trimming/Capping (Option A in left) involves either removing data points that fall outside a certain percentile range (trimming) or replacing them with a specified maximum or minimum value (capping). Imputation with Median (Option B in left) is a robust method for replacing outliers with the median value of the feature, as the median is less sensitive to extreme values than the mean. Log Transformation (Option C in left) is a technique used to reduce the impact of skewed distributions and large values by compressing the range of the data, making it less sensitive to outliers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  38,
  '{"left":["Trimming/Capping","Imputation with Median","Log Transformation"],"right":["Removing or limiting extreme values to a threshold","Replacing outliers with a robust central tendency measure","Reducing the impact of skewed distributions and large values"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_109',
  'A company is training a machine learning model on a dataset with hundreds of features, leading to high computational costs and potential overfitting. They need to reduce the number of features while retaining as much relevant information as possible. Match each dimensionality reduction technique to its core mechanism.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Principal Component Analysis (PCA)","B":"Feature Selection","C":"Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Principal Component Analysis (PCA) (Option A in left) is a technique that transforms the original features into a new set of orthogonal features (principal components) that capture the most variance in the data, thus reducing dimensionality. Feature Selection (Option B in left) involves choosing a subset of the original features that are most relevant to the target variable, discarding redundant or irrelevant ones. Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) (Option C in left) is a supervised dimensionality reduction technique that finds a linear combination of features that maximizes the separability between classes, making it suitable for classification problems.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  39,
  '{"left":["Principal Component Analysis (PCA)","Feature Selection","Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)"],"right":["Transforming features into a new lower-dimensional space","Choosing a subset of original features based on relevance","Maximizing class separability in a lower-dimensional space"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_110',
  'A data scientist is preparing a dataset for a classification model where several features are categorical (e.g., ''product_category'', ''region''). These categorical features need to be converted into a numerical format that machine learning algorithms can process effectively. Match each encoding method to its primary approach for handling categorical data.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"One-Hot Encoding","B":"Label Encoding","C":"Target Encoding"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'One-Hot Encoding (Option A in left) converts categorical variables into a binary vector representation, creating new binary columns for each unique category. Label Encoding (Option B in left) assigns a unique integer to each category, which can introduce an artificial ordinal relationship if not handled carefully. Target Encoding (Option C in left), also known as Mean Encoding, replaces each category with the mean of the target variable for that category, which can be very effective but also prone to overfitting if not regularized.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  40,
  '{"left":["One-Hot Encoding","Label Encoding","Target Encoding"],"right":["Creating binary columns for each category","Assigning unique integer to each category","Replacing categories with the mean target value"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_111',
  'A retail company is developing a recommendation engine and wants to understand which features from their customer data (e.g., purchase history, browsing behavior, demographics) are most influential in predicting customer preferences. This understanding will guide further feature engineering efforts. Match each feature importance evaluation method to its underlying principle.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Permutation Importance","B":"SHAP Values","C":"Coefficient Magnitude (Linear Models)"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Permutation Importance (Option A in left) measures the decrease in model performance when a single feature''s values are randomly shuffled, indicating how much the model relies on that feature. SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) Values (Option B in left) are a game-theoretic approach to explain the output of any machine learning model by attributing the prediction to the contribution of each individual feature. In Linear Models, the Coefficient Magnitude (Option C in left) directly reflects the weight or importance of each feature in predicting the target variable, assuming features are on a comparable scale.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  41,
  '{"left":["Permutation Importance","SHAP Values","Coefficient Magnitude (Linear Models)"],"right":["Measuring performance drop when feature is shuffled","Attributing prediction to individual feature contributions","Magnitude of feature weights in a linear model"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_112',
  'A data science team is working on a machine learning model for a highly competitive e-commerce platform. To improve model accuracy and capture more complex patterns, they decide to create new features from existing ones. Match each synthetic feature creation technique to its common application.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Polynomial Features","B":"Interaction Features","C":"Date/Time Features"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Polynomial Features (Option A in left) are created by raising existing features to a power (e.g., x^2, x^3) or combining them, which helps models capture non-linear relationships. Interaction Features (Option B in left) are formed by multiplying two or more existing features (e.g., feature_A * feature_B) to model their combined effect on the target variable. Date/Time Features (Option C in left) involve extracting components like day of week, month, year, or hour from timestamps to capture cyclical patterns and temporal trends, which are crucial for time-series related problems.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  42,
  '{"left":["Polynomial Features","Interaction Features","Date/Time Features"],"right":["Capturing non-linear relationships","Modeling combined effect of two features","Extracting cyclical patterns from timestamps"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_113',
  'A large e-commerce company processes terabytes of raw customer clickstream data daily, which needs extensive feature engineering before being used for a real-time recommendation system. They require a scalable and automated solution for these data transformations. Match each SageMaker service to its primary role in building an automated feature engineering pipeline.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Processing","B":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","C":"Amazon SageMaker Pipelines"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Amazon SageMaker Processing (Option A in left) provides managed compute infrastructure for running large-scale data processing jobs, including feature engineering, data validation, and model evaluation, making it ideal for transforming raw data. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store (Option B in left) is a purpose-built repository to store, update, retrieve, and share machine learning features for both training and real-time inference, ensuring consistency and reusability. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines (Option C in left) is a CI/CD service for machine learning that orchestrates and automates end-to-end ML workflows, including data preparation, training, and deployment steps, which would integrate Processing and Feature Store.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  43,
  '{"left":["Amazon SageMaker Processing","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines"],"right":["Run large-scale data processing jobs","Store and serve curated features for training and inference","Orchestrate and automate ML workflows"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_2_114',
  'A media company is developing a content moderation system that uses computer vision to analyze images and videos. To improve the model''s accuracy, they need to extract meaningful features from the visual data. Match each image feature engineering concept to its description.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Embeddings","B":"Image Augmentation","C":"Edge Detection"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Embeddings (Option A in left) are dense vector representations of images (or parts of images) learned by deep CNNs, capturing high-level semantic features. Image Augmentation (Option B in left) is a technique that creates new training samples by applying various transformations (e.g., rotation, flipping, cropping) to existing images, increasing dataset diversity and model robustness. Edge Detection (Option C in left) is a classic image processing technique used to identify the boundaries of objects or regions within an image, often used as a preliminary step for feature extraction.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  44,
  '{"left":["Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) Embeddings","Image Augmentation","Edge Detection"],"right":["Dense vector representations learned by deep networks","Creating new training samples by transforming existing images","Identifying boundaries of objects in an image"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

