INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_1_121',
  'A computer vision team is training an image classification model for a new product line, but they have a limited number of labeled images. To prevent overfitting and improve the model''s generalization capabilities, they decide to use various data augmentation techniques. Match each augmentation technique to its effect on the training data.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Random Horizontal Flip","B":"Random Crop","C":"Color Jitter"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Random Horizontal Flip (Option A in left) mirrors the image along its vertical axis, creating a new training sample that helps the model learn invariance to horizontal orientation. Random Crop (Option B in left) extracts a random section of the image, simulating variations in object positioning and scale within the frame. Color Jitter (Option C in left) randomly changes the brightness, contrast, saturation, and hue of an image, making the model more robust to variations in lighting conditions and color profiles.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  63,
  '{"left":["Random Horizontal Flip","Random Crop","Color Jitter"],"right":["Mirrors image along vertical axis","Extracts a random section of the image","Randomly changes brightness, contrast, saturation, and hue"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_1_122',
  'A startup is developing a specialized image recognition model for rare medical conditions, requiring highly accurate and domain-specific labels for thousands of images. They need a scalable and cost-effective solution for human-in-the-loop data labeling. Match each AWS service or concept to its role in this labeling process.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Amazon Mechanical Turk","B":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","C":"Amazon S3"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Amazon Mechanical Turk (Option A in left) is a crowdsourcing marketplace that allows businesses to outsource simple, repetitive tasks, including data labeling, to a distributed workforce. Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth (Option B in left) is a fully managed data labeling service that makes it easy to build highly accurate training datasets for machine learning, leveraging human annotators (including Mechanical Turk workers or private teams) and active learning. Amazon S3 (Option C in left) provides scalable object storage, which is typically used to store the raw images to be labeled and the resulting labeled datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  64,
  '{"left":["Amazon Mechanical Turk","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon S3"],"right":["Crowdsourcing marketplace for human intelligence tasks","Managed data labeling service for ML datasets","Scalable object storage for raw and labeled data"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_1_123',
  'A research team is developing a new ML model for a niche domain where acquiring large amounts of labeled data is extremely expensive and time-consuming. They need to explore strategies to build an effective model despite having insufficient labeled examples. Match each technique to its approach for mitigating limited labeled data.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Transfer Learning","B":"Semi-Supervised Learning","C":"Data Augmentation"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Transfer Learning (Option A in left) involves leveraging a model pre-trained on a large, related dataset and fine-tuning it on a smaller, target-specific dataset, which is highly effective when labeled data is scarce. Semi-Supervised Learning (Option B in left) combines a small amount of labeled data with a large amount of unlabeled data during training, often by using the model''s predictions on unlabeled data to generate ''pseudo-labels''. Data Augmentation (Option C in left) generates synthetic training data by applying various transformations to existing labeled samples, effectively increasing the size and diversity of the training set without collecting new data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  65,
  '{"left":["Transfer Learning","Semi-Supervised Learning","Data Augmentation"],"right":["Leveraging pre-trained models on related tasks","Combining small labeled data with large unlabeled data","Generating synthetic data from existing samples"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_1_124',
  'A data engineer is preparing a dataset for an NLP model that processes customer reviews and a recommendation engine that uses categorical product IDs. They need to convert these raw data types into numerical formats that are compatible with machine learning algorithms. Match each data formatting technique to its typical application.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Word Embeddings","B":"One-Hot Encoding","C":"Feature Hashing"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Word Embeddings (Option A in left) are dense vector representations of words that capture semantic relationships, commonly used in NLP models for tasks requiring an understanding of word meaning. One-Hot Encoding (Option B in left) converts categorical features into a binary vector representation, where each category becomes a new binary feature, suitable for nominal categorical data. Feature Hashing (Option C in left) is a technique that maps categorical features to a fixed-size numerical vector using a hash function, which is useful for high-cardinality categorical data as it avoids creating a large number of new columns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  66,
  '{"left":["Word Embeddings","One-Hot Encoding","Feature Hashing"],"right":["Representing words as dense vectors for semantic understanding","Converting categorical features into binary vectors","Mapping categorical features to fixed-size numerical vectors"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_47_m_47_1_125',
  'A financial services company is building a fraud detection model using sensitive transaction data. Maintaining high data quality and consistency is paramount to ensure the model''s reliability and compliance with regulations. They need to implement checks throughout their data pipeline. Match each data quality check to the type of issue it primarily addresses.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Schema Validation","B":"Range and Constraint Checks","C":"Uniqueness Checks"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Schema Validation (Option A in left) ensures that incoming data conforms to a predefined structure, data types, and column names, preventing issues from malformed data. Range and Constraint Checks (Option B in left) verify that numerical values fall within acceptable boundaries (e.g., age between 0 and 120) and that categorical values adhere to a predefined set of options, catching invalid or out-of-bounds data. Uniqueness Checks (Option C in left) confirm that specific identifiers or combinations of features are unique across the dataset, preventing duplicate records or inconsistent entries that could skew model training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain47_10',
  NOW(),
  67,
  '{"left":["Schema Validation","Range and Constraint Checks","Uniqueness Checks"],"right":["Ensuring data conforms to expected structure and types","Verifying values fall within acceptable boundaries","Confirming no duplicate records or identifiers exist"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2],"right":[0,1,2]}'::jsonb,
  'm_47_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

