INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_29',
  'Which operational practice involves continuously re-evaluating and updating an ML model with new data to maintain its accuracy and relevance over time?',
  'mcq',
  '["One-time model deployment","Manual model tuning","Automated model retraining","Static model versioning"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Automated model retraining, which is a critical MLOps practice. It involves setting up pipelines to automatically retrain ML models with fresh data at regular intervals or when performance degradation is detected, ensuring the model remains accurate and relevant in dynamic environments. Option A (One-time model deployment) is not sustainable for models that experience data drift or concept drift. Option B (Manual model tuning) is an iterative process during development, but not an operational practice for continuous accuracy. Option D (Static model versioning) tracks changes but doesn''t inherently update the model''s knowledge.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_30',
  'A data science team wants to automate the entire lifecycle of their ML models, from data preparation and training to deployment and monitoring. Which concept describes this end-to-end automation?',
  'mcq',
  '["MLOps pipeline","Manual model deployment","Ad-hoc scripting","Data warehousing"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) refers to an MLOps pipeline, which automates the steps involved in building, deploying, and managing machine learning models in production. This includes data ingestion, feature engineering, model training, evaluation, deployment, and monitoring. Option B (Manual model deployment) is the opposite of automation and prone to errors. Option C (Ad-hoc scripting) lacks structure, reproducibility, and scalability for production ML. Option D (Data warehousing) is for storing and analyzing large datasets, not for automating the ML lifecycle.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_31',
  'After deploying an ML model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint, which AWS service should be used to monitor its inference latency and error rates in real-time?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Step Functions","Amazon SQS"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon CloudWatch, which is the primary AWS service for monitoring the performance and health of AWS resources and applications. It can collect metrics like inference latency, error rates, and CPU utilization from Amazon SageMaker endpoints, allowing for real-time observation and alerting. Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is for infrastructure as code. Option C (AWS Step Functions) is a serverless workflow service. Option D (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_32',
  'An ML model deployed on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint starts showing a significant drop in prediction accuracy. What is the MOST immediate operational step to investigate this issue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Immediately retrain the model with new data","Increase the number of endpoint instances","Review Amazon CloudWatch metrics and logs for the endpoint","Migrate the endpoint to a different AWS Region"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most immediate and effective operational step. Reviewing Amazon CloudWatch metrics (e.g., CPU utilization, memory usage, latency) and logs (e.g., inference logs, error logs) for the SageMaker endpoint will provide crucial insights into whether the performance drop is due to infrastructure issues, data drift, or model errors. Option A (Immediately retrain the model) might be a solution, but it''s premature without understanding the root cause. Option B (Increase the number of endpoint instances) addresses scalability, not necessarily accuracy issues. Option D (Migrate the endpoint to a different AWS Region) is a drastic step for disaster recovery, not for debugging an accuracy drop.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_33',
  'When deploying a new version of an ML model to a production Amazon SageMaker endpoint, which deployment strategy minimizes risk by gradually shifting a small portion of live traffic to the new model first?',
  'mcq',
  '["All-at-once deployment","Blue/Green deployment","Canary deployment","Rolling update"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a Canary deployment. This strategy involves routing a small, controlled percentage of live traffic to the new model version while the majority of traffic still goes to the old version. This allows for real-time monitoring of the new model''s performance and stability before a full rollout, minimizing risk. Option A (All-at-once deployment) carries high risk as any issue with the new model immediately impacts all users. Option B (Blue/Green deployment) involves running two identical environments and switching traffic, which is less granular than canary. Option D (Rolling update) updates instances one by one, but doesn''t necessarily start with a very small, controlled traffic shift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_34',
  'If a newly deployed ML model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint shows critical errors, which operational capability allows quickly reverting to the previous stable model version?',
  'mcq',
  '["Forward deployment","Model rollback","A/B testing","Blue/Green deployment"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Model rollback, which is the ability to quickly revert a deployed model to a previous, stable version in case of critical errors or performance degradation. This is a crucial operational capability for maintaining service availability and reliability. Option A (Forward deployment) refers to deploying a new version, not reverting. Option C (A/B testing) is for comparing models, not for emergency reverts. Option D (Blue/Green deployment) is a deployment strategy that facilitates rollbacks by switching traffic between two environments, but ''model rollback'' is the specific action of reverting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_35',
  'Which operational challenge arises when the statistical properties of the data used for model inference change significantly over time, leading to degraded model performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Overfitting","Underfitting","Data drift","Model bias"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Data drift, which occurs when the statistical properties of the target variable or input features change over time. This can cause a deployed ML model to become less accurate because the data it was trained on no longer represents the current reality. Option A (Overfitting) is when a model learns the training data too well, including noise, and performs poorly on new data. Option B (Underfitting) is when a model is too simple to capture the underlying patterns in the data. Option D (Model bias) refers to systematic errors in a model''s predictions due to unfair assumptions in the training data or algorithm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_36',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker feature allows data scientists to compare the performance of two different model versions in production by routing a portion of live traffic to each, enabling controlled experimentation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon SageMaker A/B testing with production variants","Amazon SageMaker Clarify"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) Amazon SageMaker supports A/B testing by allowing you to deploy multiple model versions as ''production variants'' on a single endpoint and configure traffic splitting. This enables controlled experimentation to compare model performance with live traffic. Option A (incorrect) Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is for processing large datasets for inference asynchronously, not for real-time A/B testing. Option B (incorrect) Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth is for data labeling. Option D (incorrect) Amazon SageMaker Clarify helps detect bias in ML models and explain predictions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_37',
  'An ML model''s accuracy degrades over time due to data drift in the production environment. To maintain model performance and ensure it remains relevant, what is the MOST effective operational strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually retrain the model once a year regardless of performance.","Increase the Amazon SageMaker endpoint instance count to handle more traffic.","Switch to a different AWS Region to improve data processing speed.","Implement an automated retraining pipeline that periodically retrains the model with fresh data."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) implementing an automated retraining pipeline is the most effective strategy to combat model degradation due to data drift. This ensures the model is regularly updated with the latest data patterns, maintaining its accuracy and relevance in production. Option A (incorrect) manual, infrequent retraining is reactive and insufficient for dynamic data environments. Option B (incorrect) increasing instance count addresses scalability, not model accuracy. Option C (incorrect) switching regions does not address model accuracy issues caused by data drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_38',
  'What is the primary purpose of an Amazon SageMaker endpoint in the context of deploying machine learning solutions?',
  'mcq',
  '["To host trained ML models for real-time inference requests.","To store large datasets for machine learning model training.","To preprocess raw data and engineer features for ML models.","To provide a managed service for labeling data for supervised learning."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) an Amazon SageMaker endpoint is a fully managed HTTPs endpoint that allows you to deploy trained machine learning models and invoke them to get real-time predictions. Option B (incorrect) describes services like Amazon S3. Option C (incorrect) describes data preparation services or SageMaker processing jobs. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_39',
  'After deploying a new ML model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint, customers report slower response times. To diagnose this, which metric should be primarily monitored in Amazon CloudWatch to identify performance bottlenecks?',
  'mcq',
  '["DataCapturePercentage for model monitoring.","ModelLatency and InvocationLatency.","CPUUtilization of the training job instances.","TrainingJobRuntime for the model training process."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) `ModelLatency` and `InvocationLatency` are direct indicators of the time taken for the model to process a request and the total time taken for an invocation, respectively. Monitoring these metrics in Amazon CloudWatch will directly help diagnose slow response times. Option A (incorrect) `DataCapturePercentage` is related to data capture for model monitoring, not direct endpoint performance. Option C (incorrect) `CPUUtilization` of training job instances is relevant during training, not for endpoint inference performance. Option D (incorrect) `TrainingJobRuntime` is also related to the training phase, not the deployed endpoint''s performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_40',
  'A company wants to deploy a new version of an ML model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint with minimal risk of impacting production users. Which Amazon SageMaker deployment strategy allows for gradual rollout and easy rollback?',
  'mcq',
  '["Replacing the existing endpoint directly with the new model.","Creating a completely new endpoint for the new model and manually switching traffic.","Using Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform to process all requests with the new model.","Deploying the new model as a production variant and gradually shifting traffic to it."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) deploying the new model as a production variant and gradually shifting traffic is Amazon SageMaker''s recommended approach for controlled rollouts (e.g., canary or blue/green deployments). This allows you to test the new model with a small percentage of live traffic and easily roll back if issues arise, minimizing risk. Option A (incorrect) directly replacing the endpoint is a high-risk ''big bang'' deployment. Option B (incorrect) creating a new endpoint and manually switching traffic is possible but lacks the integrated traffic shifting and monitoring capabilities of production variants. Option C (incorrect) Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is for offline, asynchronous inference, not for real-time endpoint deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_41',
  'What is the role of Amazon CloudWatch Logs in operationalizing ML solutions, particularly for debugging and troubleshooting Amazon SageMaker endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["It provides centralized logging for application and system logs from SageMaker endpoints for troubleshooting.","It stores model artifacts and training data for long-term archival.","It provides a visual interface for data scientists to develop and train models.","It manages IAM permissions and access control for SageMaker resources."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) Amazon CloudWatch Logs provides a centralized service for collecting, monitoring, and analyzing log data from various AWS services, including Amazon SageMaker endpoints. This is critical for debugging issues, monitoring application behavior, and troubleshooting errors in production ML systems. Option B (incorrect) describes Amazon S3. Option C (incorrect) describes the SageMaker Studio interface. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_42',
  'An ML team observes a sudden increase in error rates from their SageMaker endpoint. To quickly identify the root cause, they need to inspect the input data sent to the model. Which Amazon SageMaker feature can help with this, while also monitoring model quality?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually restart the SageMaker endpoint to clear any errors.","Enable Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor with data capture to log inference requests and responses.","Increase the endpoint instance count to handle the error load.","Use Amazon SQS to queue inference requests and inspect them before processing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, when configured with data capture, automatically logs the input and output data for inference requests. This captured data can then be analyzed to identify issues like data drift or malformed inputs that might be causing increased error rates. Option A (incorrect) restarting the endpoint might temporarily resolve some transient issues but doesn''t help diagnose the root cause. Option C (incorrect) increasing instance count addresses scalability, not error diagnosis. Option D (incorrect) using SQS for inspection would add complexity and latency, and SageMaker''s built-in data capture is more suitable for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_43',
  'What is the primary purpose of a continuous integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipeline in the context of MLOps?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manually monitor the performance and accuracy of deployed ML models.","To provide a graphical user interface for data scientists to label training data.","To automate the process of building, testing, and deploying ML models and applications.","To store large datasets and model artifacts for long-term archival."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) a CI/CD pipeline in MLOps automates the entire lifecycle from code changes and model training to testing, versioning, and deployment of ML models into production. This automation ensures faster, more reliable, and repeatable deployments. Option A (incorrect) monitoring is typically done by services like Amazon CloudWatch and SageMaker Model Monitor, not the CI/CD pipeline itself. Option B (incorrect) data labeling is handled by services like Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth. Option D (incorrect) data and artifact storage is handled by services like Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_44',
  'A global e-commerce platform uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for real-time product recommendations. The model''s performance can degrade over time due to changes in customer preferences (concept drift). The data science team needs to implement a robust MLOps pipeline that automatically retrains the model when performance metrics drop below a certain threshold or when new data becomes available, ensuring the model remains accurate and relevant. The solution must minimize manual intervention and provide an auditable trail of model versions. Which architectural pattern should be implemented to automate model retraining and versioning?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually trigger SageMaker training jobs periodically and update the endpoint after each successful training.","Develop an AWS Lambda function to monitor model accuracy metrics from Amazon CloudWatch, and if a threshold is breached, trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline that orchestrates data preparation, model training, evaluation, and conditional model registration in Amazon SageMaker Model Registry.","Use Amazon EventBridge to schedule daily SageMaker training jobs, and then manually deploy the new model to the endpoint.","Implement a custom CI/CD pipeline using Jenkins on an Amazon EC2 instance to manage model retraining and deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the most robust and automated MLOps pipeline for continuous model retraining and versioning. An AWS Lambda function can monitor model accuracy metrics (e.g., from Amazon CloudWatch or SageMaker Model Monitor) and, upon a threshold breach or new data event, trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline. SageMaker Pipelines orchestrate the entire ML workflow, including data preparation, model training, evaluation, and conditional model registration in Amazon SageMaker Model Registry. The Model Registry provides versioning and approval workflows for models, ensuring an auditable trail. This approach minimizes manual intervention and leverages fully managed AWS services for a scalable and automated MLOps solution.
Option A (incorrect) involves manual triggering and updates, which is not scalable, automated, or efficient for continuous model improvement in a production environment.
Option C (incorrect) uses Amazon EventBridge for scheduled training, but still requires manual deployment of the new model, which introduces a manual step and lacks the full orchestration, evaluation, and versioning capabilities of SageMaker Pipelines and Model Registry.
Option D (incorrect) suggests a custom CI/CD pipeline using Jenkins on an Amazon EC2 instance. While feasible, this approach introduces significant operational overhead for managing and maintaining the Jenkins server and custom scripts, duplicating much of the functionality offered by managed services like SageMaker Pipelines.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_45',
  'A media company has deployed a video content moderation model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The model''s performance is critical, and any increase in inference latency or error rates can directly impact user experience. The MLOps team needs to implement a strategy to detect and debug performance drops in real-time, specifically focusing on identifying whether the issue is related to the model itself, the underlying infrastructure, or data input. Which combination of AWS services and practices offers the MOST effective solution for real-time debugging of SageMaker endpoint performance drops?',
  'mcq',
  '["Rely solely on Amazon CloudWatch metrics for `ModelLatency` and `OverheadLatency`, and manually inspect SageMaker endpoint logs for errors.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch alarms on SageMaker endpoint metrics (`ModelLatency`, `CPUUtilization`, `MemoryUtilization`), enable Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor for data quality and model quality drift detection, and integrate SageMaker endpoint logs with Amazon CloudWatch Logs for detailed analysis and troubleshooting.","Implement a custom logging solution within the SageMaker model container to capture detailed performance metrics and send them to an Amazon S3 bucket for batch analysis.","Use AWS CloudTrail to monitor SageMaker API calls and identify any configuration changes that might have led to performance degradation."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the most effective solution for real-time debugging of Amazon SageMaker endpoint performance drops. Configuring Amazon CloudWatch alarms on key SageMaker endpoint metrics like `ModelLatency` (for model execution time), `CPUUtilization`, and `MemoryUtilization` (for infrastructure health) allows for immediate detection of performance bottlenecks. Enabling Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is crucial for identifying if the performance drop is due to data quality issues (e.g., input data drift) or model quality degradation (concept drift). Integrating SageMaker endpoint logs with Amazon CloudWatch Logs allows for detailed, centralized analysis and troubleshooting of errors, stack traces, and custom logs from the model container, enabling the team to pinpoint the root cause efficiently.
Option A (incorrect) relies on limited CloudWatch metrics and manual log inspection, which is reactive, inefficient, and does not provide comprehensive insights into data quality or model quality issues.
Option C (incorrect) involves a custom logging solution to Amazon S3 for batch analysis. This introduces operational overhead and is not suitable for real-time debugging, as data in S3 would need to be processed before insights can be gained.
Option D (incorrect) uses AWS CloudTrail, which is for auditing API calls and identifying configuration changes, not for real-time performance monitoring or debugging of model execution or infrastructure health.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_46',
  'An autonomous driving company is developing a perception model that requires continuous retraining due to evolving road conditions and sensor data. The training data, stored in Amazon S3, is constantly growing. The MLOps team needs to automate the retraining pipeline to trigger whenever new data batches arrive in S3, ensuring the model is always up-to-date. The pipeline must include data validation, training, evaluation, and conditional deployment. Which combination of AWS services provides the MOST automated and scalable solution for this continuous retraining pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon EventBridge to schedule daily SageMaker training jobs, and manually check for new data before each run.","Configure an Amazon S3 event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function, which then initiates an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline that includes steps for data validation, model training, evaluation, and conditional model registration/deployment.","Manually run SageMaker training jobs from a Jupyter notebook whenever new data is detected in S3.","Implement a custom cron job on an Amazon EC2 instance to poll the S3 bucket for new data and then start a SageMaker training job."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the most automated and scalable solution for a continuous retraining pipeline. An Amazon S3 event notification can be configured to trigger an AWS Lambda function whenever new data batches arrive in the specified S3 bucket. This Lambda function then initiates an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline. SageMaker Pipelines are designed to orchestrate end-to-end ML workflows, including data validation, model training, evaluation, and conditional model registration/deployment. This fully automated, event-driven approach ensures the model is continuously updated with new data, minimizing manual intervention and scaling efficiently with data growth.
Option A (incorrect) uses Amazon EventBridge for scheduled jobs and requires manual data checks, which is not event-driven or fully automated, failing to meet the requirement for triggering on new data arrival.
Option C (incorrect) involves manual execution from a Jupyter notebook, which is not scalable, automated, or suitable for a production MLOps pipeline.
Option D (incorrect) suggests a custom cron job on an Amazon EC2 instance. This introduces significant operational overhead for managing the EC2 instance and custom polling logic, duplicating functionality readily available through S3 event notifications and SageMaker Pipelines.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_47',
  'A retail company has deployed a personalized product recommendation model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. To ensure the model remains relevant, the MLOps team needs to implement a strategy for automated model retraining and deployment whenever new product catalog data or customer interaction data becomes available in Amazon S3. The pipeline must be robust, auditable, and capable of rolling back to a previous model version if the new model performs worse in evaluation. Which combination of AWS services provides the MOST robust and automated MLOps pipeline for continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) of ML models?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually trigger SageMaker training jobs and endpoint updates from a Jupyter notebook.","Use Amazon EventBridge to schedule daily retraining jobs, and then manually deploy the best performing model.","Implement an event-driven pipeline where Amazon S3 events trigger an AWS Lambda function, which then initiates an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline that includes steps for data processing, training, model evaluation, conditional registration in Amazon SageMaker Model Registry, and automated deployment to the SageMaker endpoint.","Build a custom CI/CD system using AWS CodePipeline and AWS CodeBuild to orchestrate SageMaker training and deployment, managing model versions in a custom database."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) provides the most robust and automated MLOps CI/CD pipeline. An event-driven approach, where Amazon S3 events (e.g., new data arrival) trigger an AWS Lambda function, is highly scalable and reactive. The Lambda function then initiates an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline, which orchestrates the entire ML workflow: data processing, model training, evaluation, and conditional registration in Amazon SageMaker Model Registry. The Model Registry provides versioning, approval workflows, and allows for automated deployment to the SageMaker endpoint. This setup also facilitates rollbacks by allowing easy deployment of previously registered model versions. This approach leverages fully managed AWS services for a comprehensive and auditable CI/CD solution.
Option A (incorrect) involves manual operations, which is not scalable, automated, or suitable for a production CI/CD pipeline.
Option B (incorrect) uses scheduled retraining and manual deployment, which lacks the event-driven automation, comprehensive orchestration, and rollback capabilities required for a robust CI/CD pipeline.
Option D (incorrect) suggests building a custom CI/CD system with AWS CodePipeline and CodeBuild. While feasible, this introduces significant operational overhead for developing and maintaining custom integrations and a custom model versioning database, duplicating much of the functionality already provided by SageMaker Pipelines and Model Registry.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_48',
  'A company is developing a machine learning model to detect anomalies in sensor data from industrial equipment. The model is trained on Amazon SageMaker. During the development phase, the data science team frequently encounters issues where the model''s performance degrades unexpectedly after certain code changes or hyperparameter adjustments. They need a way to quickly debug and troubleshoot these performance drops, specifically identifying if the issue is with the training data, the model architecture, or the training process itself. Which Amazon SageMaker feature, combined with other AWS services, provides the MOST effective solution for debugging and analyzing training job performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually inspect the SageMaker training job logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs after each training run.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Debugger to automatically capture and analyze training job metrics and tensors, detect common issues like vanishing gradients or overfitting, and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch for alerts.","Implement custom logging within the training script to output all intermediate tensor values to an Amazon S3 bucket for later analysis.","Use AWS CloudTrail to review SageMaker API calls and identify any configuration changes that might have impacted training performance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) provides the most effective solution for debugging and analyzing Amazon SageMaker training job performance. Amazon SageMaker Debugger is specifically designed to automatically capture and analyze training job metrics and tensors in real-time. It can detect common issues like vanishing/exploding gradients, overfitting, data imbalance, and other training anomalies. Debugger integrates with Amazon CloudWatch for alerts, notifying the data science team when predefined rules are violated. This allows for proactive identification and quick resolution of performance drops related to the training process, data, or model architecture, significantly reducing debugging time.
Option A (incorrect) involves manual inspection of CloudWatch Logs, which is reactive, time-consuming, and requires deep expertise to interpret raw logs for complex training issues. It lacks automated detection capabilities.
Option C (incorrect) suggests custom logging of intermediate tensor values to Amazon S3. This introduces significant operational overhead for implementing, storing, and analyzing large volumes of custom logs, and does not provide real-time automated detection of issues.
Option D (incorrect) uses AWS CloudTrail, which logs API calls for auditing and governance, not for debugging the internal performance or behavior of a running training job. CloudTrail cannot provide insights into model-specific issues like vanishing gradients.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_49',
  'A data science team has developed two different versions of a machine learning model for predicting customer churn. They want to test both models in a production environment to determine which one performs better with real user traffic before fully deploying the superior model. Which Amazon SageMaker feature is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon SageMaker A/B testing (via endpoint variants)","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon SageMaker A/B testing, typically implemented using endpoint variants) is specifically designed for this scenario. It allows you to deploy multiple model versions behind a single endpoint, route a configurable percentage of live traffic to each version, and compare their performance metrics to determine which model is superior. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is used to detect data and model quality issues in production, not for comparing different models with live traffic. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is a data labeling service used to build high-quality training datasets, which is a pre-training step, not a deployment or operationalization feature for comparing models. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Pipelines) is for orchestrating and automating machine learning workflows, including training and deployment, but not for A/B testing deployed models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_50',
  'Over time, the performance of a deployed machine learning model can degrade due to changes in the underlying data distribution (data drift) or shifts in user behavior. Which process is essential to maintain the model''s accuracy and relevance in a production environment?',
  'mcq',
  '["Model versioning","Hyperparameter tuning","Data augmentation","Model retraining"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (Model retraining) is essential to maintain a model''s accuracy and relevance in a production environment when its performance degrades due to data drift or other changes. Retraining involves feeding the model new, more recent data to update its understanding and adapt to evolving patterns. Option A (Model versioning) is important for managing different iterations of a model, but it doesn''t address the need to update the model''s knowledge with new data. Option B (Hyperparameter tuning) is a process performed during model training to find the optimal set of hyperparameters for a given dataset, not a continuous process for maintaining accuracy of an already deployed model against data drift. Option C (Data augmentation) is a technique used during training to artificially increase the size of a training dataset by creating modified copies of existing data, which helps improve generalization but doesn''t address ongoing performance degradation in production.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_4_51',
  'An online retail company uses a fraud detection model deployed on Amazon SageMaker. The model''s accuracy is critical, but it has shown signs of degradation over the past month. The MLOps team wants to establish an automated process to monitor the model''s performance in production and trigger a retraining pipeline whenever its accuracy falls below a predefined threshold, with the LEAST amount of manual intervention. Which combination of AWS services should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect drift, triggering an AWS Lambda function to start an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline.","AWS CloudWatch Alarms to monitor inference latency, manually triggering a new Amazon SageMaker training job.","Amazon S3 event notifications to detect new data, triggering an AWS Step Functions workflow to retrain the model.","AWS Config rules to check model configuration, sending notifications to Amazon SNS for manual review and subsequent manual retraining initiation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect drift, triggering an AWS Lambda function to start an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline) is the most effective and automated solution. SageMaker Model Monitor continuously analyzes the data sent to and from the endpoint, detecting data drift or model quality degradation. It can emit Amazon CloudWatch events when a threshold is breached, which can then trigger an AWS Lambda function. This Lambda function can, in turn, initiate an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline to retrain and redeploy the model, providing a fully automated MLOps loop with minimal manual intervention. Option B (AWS CloudWatch Alarms to monitor inference latency, manually triggering a new Amazon SageMaker training job) is insufficient because latency doesn''t directly measure model accuracy degradation, and it requires manual intervention for retraining. Option C (Amazon S3 event notifications to detect new data, triggering an AWS Step Functions workflow to retrain the model) addresses new data but doesn''t directly monitor model performance degradation or data drift, which is the core problem. Option D (AWS Config rules to check model configuration, sending notifications to Amazon SNS for manual review and subsequent manual retraining initiation) focuses on configuration compliance, not model performance, and still requires manual review, which is not the least manual intervention.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

