INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_87',
  'A machine learning engineer is tasked with setting up a comprehensive A/B testing framework in Amazon SageMaker to compare two different versions of a deployed inference endpoint. The goal is to evaluate which model performs better in a production environment based on real-time user interactions and specific business metrics, ensuring minimal downtime and controlled traffic distribution. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to successfully implement this A/B testing strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new SageMaker Endpoint Configuration that specifies both model variants and their initial traffic distribution.","Deploy the updated Endpoint Configuration to the existing SageMaker Endpoint, initiating the A/B test.","Develop and train two distinct model versions (Model A and Model B) and register them in SageMaker Model Registry.","Monitor the performance metrics of both model variants and analyze the results to determine the winning model."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'To successfully implement an A/B testing strategy for SageMaker endpoints, the logical sequence of steps is crucial. Option C (correct) is the initial step, where two distinct model versions must first be developed, trained, and registered. This ensures there are actual models to compare. Following this, Option A (correct) involves creating a new SageMaker Endpoint Configuration that defines both model variants and their initial traffic split (e.g., 50/50). This configuration dictates how traffic will be routed. Next, Option B (correct) is to deploy this new Endpoint Configuration to the existing SageMaker Endpoint. This action updates the endpoint to use the new configuration, effectively starting the A/B test by routing traffic to both models. Finally, Option D (correct) is to continuously monitor the performance of both models in real-time and analyze the collected metrics to make an informed decision about which model is superior. Option A cannot precede Option C because models must exist before they can be specified in a configuration. Option B cannot precede Option A as the configuration must be defined before deployment. Option D is the final step, occurring after the test has been initiated and data collected.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_88',
  'A data scientist is tasked with evaluating a binary classification model''s performance using a confusion matrix. The model predicts whether a customer will churn or not. To accurately assess the model''s effectiveness, the data scientist needs to calculate key metrics derived from the confusion matrix. Arrange the following actions in the correct sequence to perform a comprehensive evaluation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate the F1-score, which is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, to assess overall balance.","Determine the True Positives (TP), True Negatives (TN), False Positives (FP), and False Negatives (FN) from the confusion matrix.","Compute the recall (sensitivity), representing the proportion of actual positive cases correctly identified.","Calculate the precision, which indicates the proportion of positive identifications that were actually correct."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'To perform a comprehensive evaluation of a binary classification model using a confusion matrix, the initial step is to correctly identify the fundamental components. Option B (correct) involves determining the True Positives (TP), True Negatives (TN), False Positives (FP), and False Negatives (FN) from the confusion matrix. These values are the building blocks for all subsequent metrics. Once these are established, Option D (correct) is to calculate precision, which relies on TP and FP. Following precision, Option C (correct) is to compute recall (sensitivity), which relies on TP and FN. Finally, Option A (correct) is to calculate the F1-score, which requires both precision and recall, providing a balanced measure of the model''s accuracy. Calculating F1-score before precision or recall is not possible as it depends on them. Similarly, precision and recall cannot be calculated without first identifying TP, TN, FP, and FN.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_89',
  'An ML team is developing a new fraud detection model and needs to ensure its robustness and generalization capabilities before deployment. They have a large dataset and want to use a holdout validation strategy to rigorously test the model''s performance on unseen data. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to implement a proper holdout validation workflow.',
  'ordering',
  '["Train the model exclusively on the designated training dataset.","Split the entire dataset into three distinct subsets: training, validation, and test sets.","Evaluate the trained model''s performance on the validation set to tune hyperparameters and select the best model.","Perform a final, unbiased evaluation of the best model''s performance using the completely separate test set."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Implementing a proper holdout validation workflow requires a specific sequence to ensure unbiased model evaluation. Option B (correct) is the essential first step: splitting the entire dataset into training, validation, and test sets. This ensures that the test set remains untouched until the final evaluation. Next, Option A (correct) is to train the model solely on the training dataset, preventing any data leakage from the validation or test sets. After training, Option C (correct) involves evaluating the model on the validation set. This step is crucial for hyperparameter tuning and model selection without touching the final test set. Finally, Option D (correct) is to perform a final, unbiased evaluation on the test set, which provides a true measure of the model''s generalization performance on completely unseen data. Training before splitting (Option A before B) would lead to data leakage. Evaluating on the test set before validation (Option D before C) would result in an overfit model to the test data, providing an optimistic and biased performance estimate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_90',
  'A data science team has developed several machine learning models for a critical business application and needs to select the single best model for production deployment. Each model has been trained and evaluated, producing various performance metrics. The team must now systematically compare these models to make an informed decision, considering both statistical performance and business impact. Arrange the following steps to ensure a robust model selection process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Perform a final sanity check and interpretability analysis on the top-performing model to ensure it aligns with business logic.","Define clear evaluation metrics and business objectives that the deployed model must satisfy.","Compare the performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1-score, RMSE, latency) of all candidate models against the defined objectives.","Select the model that best balances statistical performance, business impact, and operational constraints."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'A robust model selection process begins with a clear understanding of what success looks like. Option B (correct) is the foundational step: defining clear evaluation metrics and business objectives. Without these, comparing models is arbitrary. Once objectives are set, Option C (correct) involves comparing the performance metrics of all candidate models against these predefined objectives. This is where the quantitative assessment happens. Based on this comparison, Option D (correct) is to select the model that best balances statistical performance, business impact, and any operational constraints (like inference latency or cost). Finally, Option A (correct) is to perform a final sanity check and interpretability analysis. This step ensures the chosen model is not only statistically sound but also makes sense from a business perspective and is explainable, which is critical for trust and adoption. Comparing models before defining objectives (Option C before B) would lead to an unguided and potentially misaligned selection. Selecting a model before a thorough comparison (Option D before C) would be premature. The final sanity check (Option A) should always be the last step after a model has been provisionally selected.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_91',
  'An ML engineering team is developing a credit risk assessment model and is concerned about potential biases in the training data leading to unfair predictions for certain demographic groups. To address this, they decide to implement a systematic process for detecting and mitigating bias in their model. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to effectively manage model bias.',
  'ordering',
  '["Retrain the model using bias mitigation techniques, such as re-sampling, re-weighting, or adversarial debiasing.","Define and identify sensitive attributes (e.g., age, gender, ethnicity) and fairness metrics (e.g., demographic parity, equalized odds).","Analyze the model''s predictions and performance across different sensitive groups to detect statistical disparities.","Collect and preprocess data, ensuring a diverse and representative dataset, and analyze for inherent data biases."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'Effectively managing model bias requires a structured approach. Option B (correct) is the crucial first step: defining sensitive attributes and fairness metrics. This establishes the criteria for bias detection. Following this, Option D (correct) involves collecting and preprocessing data, with a specific focus on ensuring diversity and analyzing for inherent data biases, as data bias is often the root cause of model bias. Once the data is prepared and initial biases understood, Option C (correct) is to analyze the model''s predictions and performance across different sensitive groups using the defined fairness metrics to detect statistical disparities. Finally, Option A (correct) is to retrain the model using appropriate bias mitigation techniques, which should only be applied after bias has been detected and understood. Retraining before detection (Option A before C) would be a blind attempt. Analyzing predictions before defining metrics (Option C before B) would lack direction. Data collection (Option D) is a prerequisite for any analysis or training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_92',
  'A product team wants to deploy a new recommendation engine and evaluate its impact on user engagement metrics (e.g., click-through rate, conversion rate) in real-time without affecting all users. They decide to use an online evaluation strategy, specifically A/B testing, for a controlled rollout. Arrange the following steps to correctly set up and execute an online A/B test for a deployed model.',
  'ordering',
  '["Gradually increase traffic to the better-performing model and eventually decommission the inferior one.","Deploy both the control (existing) and treatment (new) models to production, routing a small percentage of live traffic to the treatment model.","Define the key performance indicators (KPIs) and success metrics for the recommendation engine.","Collect and analyze real-time user interaction data and monitor the defined KPIs for both models."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'Setting up and executing an online A/B test requires a logical progression of steps to ensure valid results and controlled deployment. Option C (correct) is the initial and most critical step: defining the key performance indicators (KPIs) and success metrics. Without clear metrics, it''s impossible to objectively evaluate which model is better. Once metrics are defined, Option B (correct) involves deploying both models and routing a small percentage of live traffic to the new model, initiating the test. Following deployment, Option D (correct) is to collect and analyze real-time user interaction data and monitor the defined KPIs for both models to observe their performance. Finally, Option A (correct) is to gradually increase traffic to the better-performing model and decommission the inferior one, based on the observed metrics, completing the rollout. Deploying models before defining KPIs (Option B before C) would lead to an undirected test. Analyzing data before deployment (Option D before B) is impossible. Scaling traffic (Option A) should only happen after sufficient data collection and analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_93',
  'A data scientist is tasked with evaluating the performance of a binary classification model, specifically focusing on its ability to distinguish between positive and negative classes across various classification thresholds. To achieve this, they decide to calculate and interpret the Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUC-ROC) curve. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to compute and understand the AUC-ROC.',
  'ordering',
  '["Plot the True Positive Rate (TPR) against the False Positive Rate (FPR) at various threshold settings to create the ROC curve.","Obtain the predicted probabilities for the positive class from the binary classification model.","Calculate the area under the generated ROC curve, which represents the AUC-ROC score.","Vary the classification threshold from 0 to 1, and for each threshold, calculate the TPR and FPR."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'To compute and interpret the AUC-ROC curve, a specific sequence of actions is necessary. Option B (correct) is the initial step: obtaining the predicted probabilities for the positive class from the model. These probabilities are essential for varying the classification threshold. Next, Option D (correct) involves varying the classification threshold from 0 to 1 and, for each threshold, calculating the True Positive Rate (TPR) and False Positive Rate (FPR). This generates the data points for the curve. Following this, Option A (correct) is to plot these calculated TPR and FPR values to create the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve itself. Finally, Option C (correct) is to calculate the area under this generated ROC curve, which yields the AUC-ROC score, a single metric summarizing the model''s performance across all thresholds. Plotting the ROC curve before calculating TPR/FPR (Option A before D) is not possible. Calculating AUC-ROC before plotting the curve (Option C before A) is also not feasible. Obtaining probabilities (Option B) is a prerequisite for all other steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_94',
  'An ML team needs to establish a robust offline model evaluation workflow to thoroughly assess new model iterations before they are considered for online A/B testing or production deployment. This workflow should cover data preparation, metric calculation, and comparison against baselines. Arrange the following steps in the correct order for a comprehensive offline model evaluation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Compare the model''s performance metrics against a predefined baseline or previous model versions.","Split the prepared dataset into training, validation, and test sets to ensure unbiased evaluation.","Train the new machine learning model on the training dataset and tune hyperparameters using the validation set.","Calculate a comprehensive set of evaluation metrics (e.g., accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, RMSE) on the unseen test set."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'A comprehensive offline model evaluation workflow starts with proper data handling. Option B (correct) is the first step: splitting the dataset into training, validation, and test sets. This is fundamental for unbiased evaluation. Next, Option C (correct) involves training the model on the training set and tuning hyperparameters using the validation set, ensuring the model is optimized without touching the final test data. Following training and tuning, Option D (correct) is to calculate a comprehensive set of evaluation metrics on the completely unseen test set. This provides the true measure of the model''s generalization. Finally, Option A (correct) is to compare these calculated metrics against a predefined baseline or previous model versions to determine if the new model offers a significant improvement. Training before splitting (Option C before B) would lead to data leakage. Comparing before calculating metrics (Option A before D) is impossible. Calculating metrics on the test set (Option D) must happen after training and tuning (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_5_95',
  'A data science team needs to perform an A/B test to evaluate a new recommendation model against the current production model. Order the following steps to ensure a reliable evaluation with minimal user disruption.',
  'ordering',
  '["Split incoming user traffic into two groups (A and B).","Analyze metrics from both groups and determine the winning model.","Direct Group A to the existing model and Group B to the new model.","Deploy the new model alongside the existing production model."]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}',
  'The correct sequence for an A/B test begins with deploying the new model alongside the existing one (Option D) to make it available for testing. Next, incoming user traffic must be split into distinct groups (Option A) to ensure a controlled experiment. Then, traffic is directed, with Group A receiving the existing model''s recommendations and Group B receiving the new model''s recommendations (Option C). Finally, after a sufficient testing period, metrics from both groups are analyzed to determine the winning model (Option B). Placing analysis or traffic direction before deployment or splitting traffic would lead to an invalid or impossible test setup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_5',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

