INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_78',
  'Order the steps to map business Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) to machine learning objectives.',
  'ordering',
  '["Translate the business objective into a measurable ML task (e.g., increase conversion rate -> predict conversion)","Identify the core business problem and its associated KPIs (e.g., customer churn rate, revenue growth)","Define the specific ML model output that directly impacts the chosen ML task (e.g., probability of churn)","Select appropriate ML metrics that align with the business KPIs (e.g., precision for fraud detection, RMSE for sales forecasting)"]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'The process begins with a clear understanding of the business. Option A (Identify the core business problem and its associated KPIs) establishes the starting point. This business problem is then converted into an ML task in Option B (Translate the business objective into a measurable ML task). Next, Option C (Define the specific ML model output) specifies what the model will actually produce. Finally, Option D (Select appropriate ML metrics) ensures that the model''s performance is measured in a way that reflects the business KPIs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_79',
  'A retail company wants to predict future sales for inventory management. Order the steps to frame this as a forecasting problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify historical sales data, promotional events, and external factors as potential features","Define ''future sales'' as the target variable to be predicted (e.g., sales for next week/month)","Determine the forecasting horizon and granularity (e.g., daily, weekly, monthly sales for next 3 months)","Confirm that the problem requires predicting continuous numerical values over time"]'::json,
  '{3,1,2,0}',
  'Framing a forecasting problem starts with recognizing its nature. Option D (Confirm that the problem requires predicting continuous numerical values over time) establishes this. Next, Option B (Define ''future sales'' as the target variable) specifies what needs to be predicted. Option C (Determine the forecasting horizon and granularity) sets the scope of the prediction. Finally, Option A (Identify historical sales data, promotional events, and external factors) outlines the necessary input data for the model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_80',
  'Order the steps to identify if clustering is an appropriate machine learning approach for a given dataset.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify that the data lacks explicit labels or predefined categories","Determine if the goal is to discover inherent groupings or segments within the data","Assess if the identified clusters would provide meaningful business insights","Explore the data for natural patterns or densities that suggest distinct groups"]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Clustering is primarily used for discovering structure in data. Option B (Determine if the goal is to discover inherent groupings) defines this purpose. A key characteristic of clustering is the absence of labels, so Option A (Verify that the data lacks explicit labels) is crucial. Before applying, Option D (Explore the data for natural patterns or densities) helps confirm the presence of potential clusters. Finally, Option C (Assess if the identified clusters would provide meaningful business insights) ensures the effort will yield value.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_81',
  'A customer support team wants to categorize incoming support tickets automatically. Order the steps to frame this as a classification problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the distinct categories (classes) for the support tickets (e.g., ''Billing'', ''Technical'', ''Feature Request'')","Identify the ticket text and metadata (e.g., subject, description, customer type) as input features","Confirm that the problem requires assigning a discrete label to each ticket based on its content","Gather historical support tickets with their manually assigned categories as labeled training data"]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Framing a classification problem starts with recognizing its nature. Option C (Confirm that the problem requires assigning a discrete label) establishes this. Next, Option A (Define the distinct categories) specifies the possible outputs. Option B (Identify the ticket text and metadata) determines the inputs the model will use. Finally, Option D (Gather historical support tickets with their manually assigned categories) provides the necessary labeled data for training a supervised classification model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_82',
  'Order the steps to decide between a classification and a regression machine learning approach for a given problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Determine if the output variable is discrete (categories) or continuous (numerical values)","Confirm if the problem requires predicting a specific class or a quantity","Evaluate if the available data supports the chosen output type","Select classification if the output is discrete, or regression if it is continuous"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The fundamental difference between classification and regression lies in the nature of the output. Option A (Determine if the output variable is discrete or continuous) is the primary differentiator. This directly relates to Option B (Confirm if the problem requires predicting a specific class or a quantity). Option C (Evaluate if the available data supports the chosen output type) is a practical check to ensure feasibility. Finally, Option D (Select classification if the output is discrete, or regression if it is continuous) formalizes the decision based on these observations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_83',
  'A business analyst identifies a challenge where customers frequently abandon their shopping carts before completing a purchase. The goal is to reduce cart abandonment. The ML team needs to frame this business problem as a supervised machine learning problem to predict which customers are likely to abandon their carts. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to frame this problem effectively.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the target variable as ''cart abandonment'' (binary: yes/no) and define what constitutes an abandonment.","Gather historical customer interaction data, including browsing history, cart contents, and purchase outcomes.","Select a classification algorithm suitable for predicting a binary outcome, such as logistic regression or XGBoost.","Define the problem as predicting the likelihood of a customer abandoning their shopping cart within a specific timeframe."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'Framing a business problem as a supervised ML problem requires a structured approach. Option D (correct) is the crucial first step: clearly defining the problem as predicting the likelihood of cart abandonment within a specific timeframe. This sets the scope. Following this, Option A (correct) involves identifying the target variable (''cart abandonment'' as a binary outcome) and precisely defining what constitutes an abandonment. This is essential for labeling data. Next, Option B (correct) is to gather relevant historical customer interaction data that can serve as features for the model. This data will be used to train the model. Finally, Option C (correct) is to select a classification algorithm appropriate for predicting a binary outcome, which comes after the problem, target, and data are understood. Identifying the target before defining the problem (Option A before D) would be premature. Gathering data before knowing the target (Option B before A) might lead to collecting irrelevant data. Selecting an algorithm before understanding the problem and data (Option C before D or B) is an anti-pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_84',
  'An e-commerce company wants to segment its customer base to develop targeted marketing campaigns without prior knowledge of customer groups. The ML team decides to frame this as an unsupervised learning problem to discover natural groupings within the customer data. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to frame this unsupervised learning problem effectively.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select a clustering algorithm (e.g., K-Means, DBSCAN) appropriate for identifying distinct customer segments.","Define the problem as discovering inherent customer segments based on their behavioral and demographic attributes.","Collect comprehensive customer data, including purchase history, browsing behavior, and demographic information.","Identify relevant features from the collected data that can characterize customer behavior and preferences."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'Framing an unsupervised learning problem, such as customer segmentation, requires a logical sequence. Option B (correct) is the initial step: defining the problem as discovering inherent customer segments based on their attributes. This sets the overall objective. Following this, Option C (correct) involves collecting comprehensive customer data, which will serve as the input for the segmentation. Next, Option D (correct) is to identify relevant features from the collected data that can effectively characterize customer behavior and preferences. This step is crucial for preparing the data for the algorithm. Finally, Option A (correct) is to select a clustering algorithm suitable for identifying distinct customer segments, which is chosen after the problem, data, and features are understood. Collecting data before defining the problem (Option C before B) might lead to collecting irrelevant data. Selecting an algorithm before collecting data or identifying features (Option A before C or D) is premature. Identifying features (Option D) must happen after data collection (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_85',
  'A manufacturing company is experiencing frequent machine breakdowns and is considering implementing a machine learning solution to predict maintenance needs. Before investing significant resources, the ML lead needs to determine if an ML solution is truly appropriate for this problem. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to make this determination.',
  'ordering',
  '["Assess the availability and quality of historical data related to machine operation, sensor readings, and maintenance logs.","Identify if the problem can be framed as a solvable ML task (e.g., classification for failure prediction, regression for remaining useful life).","Evaluate the potential business value and return on investment (ROI) of an ML solution versus traditional approaches.","Determine if there is a clear, measurable objective that an ML model can optimize, such as reducing downtime or maintenance costs."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'Determining the appropriateness of an ML solution begins with understanding the problem''s core. Option D (correct) is the first step: determining if there is a clear, measurable objective that an ML model can optimize. Without a measurable objective, the success of an ML solution cannot be quantified. Following this, Option B (correct) is to identify if the problem can be framed as a solvable ML task. This checks the technical feasibility. Next, Option A (correct) involves assessing the availability and quality of historical data, which is a prerequisite for any ML project. Finally, Option C (correct) is to evaluate the potential business value and ROI of an ML solution compared to traditional approaches, which is critical for justifying the investment. Assessing data before defining a measurable objective (Option A before D) might lead to collecting data for an ill-defined problem. Evaluating ROI before understanding feasibility (Option C before B) is premature. Framing as an ML task (Option B) should follow objective definition.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_86',
  'A financial institution plans to build a machine learning model to detect fraudulent transactions. This is a supervised learning task. The ML team needs to identify the specific data requirements to ensure the model can be effectively trained and evaluated. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to define the data requirements for this supervised learning problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify potential features that could indicate fraud, such as transaction amount, location, time, and frequency.","Determine the target variable: a binary label indicating whether a transaction is ''fraudulent'' or ''legitimate''.","Specify the required data sources and collection methods, including historical transaction logs and fraud labels.","Define the scope of the problem: predicting fraud for individual transactions in real-time."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'Defining data requirements for a supervised learning task begins with understanding the problem''s scope. Option D (correct) is the initial step: defining the scope of the problem, which is predicting fraud for individual transactions. This narrows down the focus. Following this, Option B (correct) involves determining the target variable, which is a binary label for ''fraudulent'' or ''legitimate''. This is what the model will learn to predict. Next, Option A (correct) is to identify potential features that could indicate fraud, which will be the input to the model. Finally, Option C (correct) is to specify the required data sources and collection methods, which can only be done effectively once the target and features are known. Identifying features before defining the target (Option A before B) might lead to irrelevant feature collection. Specifying data sources before identifying features (Option C before A) would be inefficient. Defining the problem scope (Option D) is the prerequisite for all other steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_87',
  'A media company wants to personalize content recommendations for its users by grouping them into distinct interest categories without relying on explicit labels. This is an unsupervised learning problem. The ML team needs to identify the necessary data requirements to support this initiative. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to define the data requirements for this unsupervised learning problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify relevant user attributes and behavioral data points that can characterize interests, such as viewing history, search queries, and demographic information.","Determine the desired granularity of user segments (e.g., broad categories vs. niche interests).","Specify the data sources and collection mechanisms for user interaction logs and profile data.","Define the objective: to discover natural groupings of users based on their implicit preferences."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}',
  'Defining data requirements for an unsupervised learning problem like content personalization starts with a clear objective. Option D (correct) is the initial step: defining the objective, which is to discover natural groupings of users based on their implicit preferences. This sets the direction for data collection. Following this, Option B (correct) involves determining the desired granularity of user segments. This influences the type and detail of data needed. Next, Option A (correct) is to identify relevant user attributes and behavioral data points that can characterize interests, which will form the features for clustering. Finally, Option C (correct) is to specify the data sources and collection mechanisms for user interaction logs and profile data, which can only be done effectively once the features are identified. Identifying attributes before defining the objective (Option A before D) might lead to collecting irrelevant data. Specifying data sources before identifying attributes (Option C before A) is inefficient. Defining the objective (Option D) is the prerequisite for all other steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_88',
  'A healthcare provider wants to predict patient readmission risk. The ML team needs to decide between a classification and a regression approach. The primary goal is to identify high-risk patients for targeted intervention. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to select the most appropriate ML approach for this predictive task.',
  'ordering',
  '["Consider the desired output: a binary prediction (readmit/not readmit) for classification or a continuous risk score for regression.","Evaluate the business impact of each approach: identifying high-risk patients for intervention (classification) vs. quantifying risk level (regression).","Define the problem: predicting patient readmission risk within 30 days.","Assess data availability and label quality for both binary outcomes and continuous risk factors."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'Selecting the most appropriate ML approach begins with a clear problem definition. Option C (correct) is the initial step: defining the problem as predicting patient readmission risk within 30 days. This establishes the context. Following this, Option A (correct) involves considering the desired output: a binary prediction (readmit/not readmit) for classification or a continuous risk score for regression. This directly influences the choice of model type. Next, Option D (correct) is to assess data availability and label quality for both binary outcomes and continuous risk factors, as data limitations might constrain the choice. Finally, Option B (correct) is to evaluate the business impact of each approach, which is crucial for aligning the ML solution with the overall business goal of targeted intervention. Assessing data before considering desired output (Option D before A) might lead to collecting data for an unsuitable approach. Evaluating business impact before defining the problem (Option B before C) is premature. Defining the problem (Option C) is the prerequisite for all other steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_89',
  'A startup aims to develop a system that automatically categorizes incoming customer support tickets to route them to the correct department. The high-level business goal is to improve response times and customer satisfaction. The ML lead needs to define this as a solvable and measurable ML problem. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to effectively define this ML problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the required data: historical support tickets with their corresponding categories and text content.","Define the ML task as a multi-class text classification problem.","Establish a clear, measurable objective: ''achieve 90% accuracy in ticket categorization'' or ''reduce manual routing time by 50%''.","Determine the scope: categorize new incoming support tickets based on their text content."]'::json,
  '{3,2,1,0}',
  'Effectively defining an ML problem starts with determining its scope. Option D (correct) is the initial step: determining the scope, which is to categorize new incoming support tickets based on their text content. This sets the boundaries of the problem. Following this, Option C (correct) involves establishing a clear, measurable objective, such as achieving 90% accuracy or reducing manual routing time. This provides quantifiable success criteria. Next, Option B (correct) is to define the ML task as a multi-class text classification problem, which is the technical approach to achieve the objective. Finally, Option A (correct) is to identify the required data, such as historical tickets and categories, which are necessary inputs for the defined ML task. Identifying data before defining the ML task (Option A before B) might lead to collecting irrelevant data. Defining the ML task before establishing an objective (Option B before C) would lack clear success criteria. Determining the scope (Option D) is the prerequisite for all other steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_1_90',
  'A business analyst is evaluating a new problem to determine if it can be effectively addressed using supervised machine learning. Order the following steps to correctly frame the problem for a supervised approach.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the specific target variable that needs to be predicted.","Select an appropriate supervised learning algorithm based on the problem type (e.g., classification, regression).","Identify and collect a dataset with labeled examples of the desired outcome.","Determine if historical data exists with features that correlate to the target."]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}',
  'To frame a problem for supervised machine learning, the first step is to clearly define the specific target variable (Option A) that the model will predict. Following this, it''s crucial to determine if historical data exists with features that could correlate to this target (Option D), as supervised learning relies on past observations. Once data availability is confirmed, the next step is to identify and collect a dataset that includes labeled examples of the target variable (Option C). Only after these foundational steps are complete can an appropriate supervised learning algorithm be selected (Option B) that matches the problem type and data characteristics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

