INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_72',
  'Order the steps to balance bias-variance trade-off during model development.',
  'ordering',
  '["Adjust model complexity (e.g., add/remove features, change model type)","Evaluate model performance on both training and validation sets","Identify if the model suffers from high bias (underfitting) or high variance (overfitting)","Collect more data or perform data augmentation if variance is high"]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'Understanding the current state of the model is key. Option B (Evaluate model performance on both training and validation sets) provides the necessary data. Option C (Identify if the model suffers from high bias (underfitting) or high variance (overfitting)) diagnoses the problem. Option A (Adjust model complexity (e.g., add/remove features, change model type)) is a direct way to address bias or variance. Finally, Option D (Collect more data or perform data augmentation if variance is high) is a specific strategy to reduce variance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_73',
  'Order the general steps for performing hyperparameter optimization for a machine learning model.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the search space for each hyperparameter (e.g., ranges, discrete values)","Select an optimization strategy (e.g., grid search, random search, Bayesian optimization)","Evaluate model performance for each hyperparameter combination using a validation set","Train the model with the optimal hyperparameters found"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Hyperparameter optimization begins by defining the boundaries for tuning. Option A (Define the search space) sets these limits. Next, Option B (Select an optimization strategy) determines how different combinations will be explored. Option C (Evaluate model performance) assesses the effectiveness of each combination. Finally, Option D (Train the model with the optimal hyperparameters) applies the best configuration found to the final model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_74',
  'Order the steps for implementing k-fold cross-validation during machine learning model training.',
  'ordering',
  '["Divide the entire dataset into ''k'' equally sized folds","Train the model ''k'' times, each time using ''k-1'' folds for training and one fold for validation","Compute the average performance metric across all ''k'' validation runs","Aggregate the performance metrics from each validation fold"]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}',
  'Cross-validation starts with data partitioning. Option A (Divide the entire dataset into ''k'' equally sized folds) creates the necessary subsets. Next, Option B (Train the model ''k'' times) performs the iterative training and validation process. Option D (Aggregate the performance metrics from each validation fold) collects the results from each iteration. Finally, Option C (Compute the average performance metric) provides a robust estimate of the model''s generalization ability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_75',
  'Order the steps to apply L1 regularization during machine learning model training.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add a penalty term proportional to the absolute value of the model''s weights to the loss function","Choose an appropriate regularization strength (lambda or alpha) hyperparameter","Train the model using an optimization algorithm (e.g., gradient descent) that minimizes the modified loss function","Observe the effect of regularization on model complexity and feature sparsity"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Applying L1 regularization starts with modifying the objective function. Option A (Add a penalty term) directly incorporates the L1 penalty. Next, Option B (Choose an appropriate regularization strength) controls the impact of this penalty. Option C (Train the model using an optimization algorithm) then minimizes this new loss function. Finally, Option D (Observe the effect of regularization) allows for analysis of how the regularization influenced the model, particularly in terms of feature selection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_76',
  'An ML engineer is setting up an automated hyperparameter tuning job in Amazon SageMaker. Order the steps for this process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the training script that accepts hyperparameters as arguments","Specify the hyperparameter ranges and objective metric for the tuning job","Launch the SageMaker Hyperparameter Tuning job","Analyze the tuning job results to identify the best performing hyperparameter combination"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Setting up SageMaker HPO begins with the training logic. Option A (Define the training script) is essential as it''s what SageMaker will execute. Next, Option B (Specify the hyperparameter ranges and objective metric) tells SageMaker what to tune and how to evaluate. Option C (Launch the SageMaker Hyperparameter Tuning job) initiates the automated process. Finally, Option D (Analyze the tuning job results) helps in selecting the optimal model configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_77',
  'A data scientist is tuning a deep learning model to prevent overfitting. Order the steps to apply dropout regularization.',
  'ordering',
  '["Specify the dropout rate (e.g., 0.2 to 0.5) for each dropout layer","Insert dropout layers into the neural network architecture, typically after activation functions","Train the neural network, where neurons are randomly dropped during each training iteration","Evaluate the model''s performance on the validation set to observe the impact on generalization"]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Applying dropout starts with modifying the model''s structure. Option B (Insert dropout layers) physically adds the regularization mechanism. Next, Option A (Specify the dropout rate) configures how aggressively neurons will be dropped. Option C (Train the neural network) then executes the training process with dropout active. Finally, Option D (Evaluate the model''s performance) assesses whether dropout successfully improved the model''s ability to generalize to unseen data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_78',
  'Order the steps to initialize weights in a neural network before training.',
  'ordering',
  '["Choose an appropriate weight initialization strategy (e.g., Xavier, He, random normal)","Apply the chosen strategy to set initial values for all weights in each layer","Ensure that biases are typically initialized to zero or a small constant value","Begin the training process, allowing the optimization algorithm to update weights"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'Weight initialization begins with selecting a method. Option A (Choose an appropriate weight initialization strategy) is the first decision. Next, Option B (Apply the chosen strategy) sets the actual weight values. Option C (Ensure that biases are typically initialized to zero) addresses the initialization of bias terms. Finally, Option D (Begin the training process) starts the learning phase where these initial weights are iteratively adjusted.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_79',
  'A data scientist is optimizing an XGBoost model. Order the steps for tuning tree-specific hyperparameters.',
  'ordering',
  '["Tune `max_depth` to control the depth of individual trees, balancing bias and variance","Tune `min_child_weight` to control the minimum sum of instance weight (hessian) needed in a child, preventing overfitting","Tune `subsample` and `colsample_bytree` to reduce variance by sampling data and features","Tune `eta` (learning rate) to control the step size shrinkage, preventing overfitting"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'When tuning XGBoost, it''s often effective to start with parameters that control individual tree complexity and then move to regularization. Option A (Tune `max_depth`) is a primary control for tree complexity. Following that, Option B (Tune `min_child_weight`) further controls the splitting process. Option C (Tune `subsample` and `colsample_bytree`) introduces sampling to reduce variance. Finally, Option D (Tune `eta` (learning rate)) controls the contribution of each tree, which is critical for overall model performance and preventing overfitting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_80',
  'An ML engineer is setting up a hyperparameter tuning job for a deep learning model in Amazon SageMaker. The goal is to find the optimal combination of hyperparameters to maximize model accuracy while minimizing training time. The engineer needs to define the search space, objective metric, and resource allocation. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to configure and launch a SageMaker hyperparameter tuning job.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the hyperparameter ranges (e.g., learning rate, batch size) and the search strategy (e.g., Bayesian optimization, random search).","Specify the objective metric (e.g., validation accuracy) to be optimized and whether to maximize or minimize it.","Configure the SageMaker training job details, including the estimator, instance types, and number of training jobs.","Launch the hyperparameter tuning job and monitor its progress in the SageMaker console."]'::json,
  '{2,1,0,3}',
  'Configuring and launching a SageMaker hyperparameter tuning job requires a specific sequence of setup. Option C (correct) is the initial step: configuring the SageMaker training job details, including the estimator, instance types, and number of training jobs. This defines the underlying training process that will be tuned. Following this, Option B (correct) involves specifying the objective metric to be optimized (e.g., validation accuracy) and whether to maximize or minimize it. This tells SageMaker what to improve. Next, Option A (correct) is to define the hyperparameter ranges and the search strategy. This outlines the parameters to explore and how to explore them. Finally, Option D (correct) is to launch the hyperparameter tuning job and monitor its progress. Defining hyperparameter ranges before configuring the training job (Option A before C) would be out of order, as the estimator defines the hyperparameters. Specifying the objective metric (Option B) depends on the training job''s output. Launching the job (Option D) is the final action after all configurations are complete.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_81',
  'A data scientist is training a linear regression model and observes that it is overfitting the training data, indicated by high variance. To address this, they decide to apply L1 (Lasso) and L2 (Ridge) regularization techniques. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to effectively apply regularization during model training.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add a penalty term (L1 or L2 norm of coefficients) to the model''s loss function.","Train the regularized model on the training data, allowing the penalty term to shrink coefficients.","Evaluate the regularized model''s performance on a validation set to ensure improved generalization.","Select the regularization type (L1, L2, or Elastic Net) and determine the regularization strength (lambda or alpha)."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'Effectively applying regularization during model training requires a structured approach. Option D (correct) is the initial step: selecting the regularization type (L1, L2, or Elastic Net) and determining the regularization strength (lambda or alpha). This defines the specific regularization strategy. Following this, Option A (correct) involves adding the chosen penalty term to the model''s loss function. This modifies the objective that the model optimizes. Next, Option B (correct) is to train the regularized model on the training data, allowing the penalty term to shrink coefficients and prevent overfitting. Finally, Option C (correct) is to evaluate the regularized model''s performance on a validation set to ensure improved generalization and to fine-tune the regularization strength if necessary. Adding the penalty term before selecting its type and strength (Option A before D) is illogical. Training before adding the penalty (Option B before A) would result in an unregularized model. Evaluation (Option C) must follow training (Option B).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_82',
  'An ML engineer is developing a new image classification model and needs to rigorously evaluate its performance and generalization capability. To minimize bias in the performance estimate and ensure the model performs well on unseen data, they decide to use k-fold cross-validation. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to perform k-fold cross-validation for model evaluation.',
  'ordering',
  '["Average the performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1-score) across all k folds to get a robust estimate of the model''s performance.","Divide the entire dataset into k equally sized, non-overlapping folds.","For each fold, designate it as the validation set and use the remaining k-1 folds as the training set.","Train the model on the training set for the current fold and evaluate its performance on the validation set."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'Performing k-fold cross-validation systematically evaluates a model. Option B (correct) is the initial step: dividing the entire dataset into k equally sized, non-overlapping folds. This creates the partitions for validation. Following this, Option C (correct) involves iterating through each fold, designating one as the validation set and the remaining k-1 as the training set. This sets up the training/validation split for each iteration. Next, Option D (correct) is to train the model on the training set for the current fold and evaluate its performance on the corresponding validation set. This step is repeated for all k folds. Finally, Option A (correct) is to average the performance metrics across all k folds to obtain a robust and less biased estimate of the model''s performance. Training before dividing the dataset (Option D before B) would not be k-fold cross-validation. Designating folds (Option C) must happen after dividing the dataset (Option B). Averaging metrics (Option A) is the final step after all folds have been processed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_83',
  'A data scientist is working on a complex deep learning model for natural language processing and observes that the model exhibits high bias, meaning it underfits the training data. To address this, they plan to adjust hyperparameters to reduce bias and improve model performance. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to balance the bias-variance tradeoff through hyperparameter tuning.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the model''s performance on both training and validation sets to diagnose bias (underfitting) or variance (overfitting).","Adjust hyperparameters that influence model complexity, such as increasing the number of layers or neurons, or decreasing regularization strength.","Iteratively repeat the training and evaluation process, making further hyperparameter adjustments based on observed performance.","Train the deep learning model with an initial set of hyperparameters."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}',
  'Balancing the bias-variance tradeoff through hyperparameter tuning is an iterative process. Option D (correct) is the initial step: training the deep learning model with an initial set of hyperparameters. This provides a baseline. Following this, Option A (correct) involves evaluating the model''s performance on both training and validation sets to diagnose whether the model is suffering from high bias (underfitting) or high variance (overfitting). This diagnostic step is crucial. Next, Option B (correct) is to adjust hyperparameters that influence model complexity. For high bias (underfitting), this would mean increasing model complexity (e.g., more layers, fewer regularization). Finally, Option C (correct) is to iteratively repeat the training and evaluation process, making further adjustments based on observed performance until an optimal balance is achieved. Adjusting hyperparameters before initial training and evaluation (Option B before D or A) is premature. Iterating (Option C) must follow an initial adjustment and evaluation (Option B and A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_84',
  'An ML engineer is optimizing a deep learning model for a critical real-time application. The learning rate is a crucial hyperparameter that significantly impacts convergence and final model performance. They need to systematically tune the learning rate to achieve optimal results. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to effectively optimize a deep learning model''s learning rate.',
  'ordering',
  '["Train the model for a few epochs with the chosen learning rate and monitor its performance on a validation set.","Select an initial learning rate, often by starting with a small value and gradually increasing it (learning rate finder).","Adjust the learning rate schedule (e.g., step decay, cosine annealing) or use adaptive optimizers (e.g., Adam, RMSprop).","Iteratively refine the learning rate based on observed convergence behavior and validation performance."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Optimizing a deep learning model''s learning rate is an iterative and systematic process. Option B (correct) is the initial step: selecting an initial learning rate, often using techniques like a learning rate finder to identify a suitable starting point. Following this, Option A (correct) involves training the model for a few epochs with the chosen learning rate and monitoring its performance on a validation set. This provides feedback on the learning rate''s effectiveness. Next, Option D (correct) is to iteratively refine the learning rate based on observed convergence behavior and validation performance, adjusting it up or down as needed. Finally, Option C (correct) is to adjust the learning rate schedule or use adaptive optimizers, which are more advanced techniques applied once a good base learning rate is established and further optimization is needed. Training before selecting an initial learning rate (Option A before B) is not possible. Adjusting the schedule (Option C) is usually a refinement after initial tuning, not the first step. Iterative refinement (Option D) must follow initial training and monitoring (Option A).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_85',
  'A deep learning practitioner is building a new neural network for a complex image recognition task. To ensure efficient training and prevent issues like vanishing or exploding gradients, proper weight initialization is critical. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to effectively initialize weights in a neural network to improve convergence.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select an appropriate weight initialization strategy (e.g., Xavier/Glorot, He, LeCun) based on the activation function used in the layers.","Construct the neural network architecture, defining the number of layers, neurons, and activation functions.","Apply the chosen initialization strategy to the weights of each layer in the neural network.","Begin the training process, allowing the initialized weights to be updated by the optimization algorithm."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Effectively initializing weights in a neural network is a foundational step for training. Option B (correct) is the initial step: constructing the neural network architecture, defining layers, neurons, and activation functions. This provides the structure for which weights need to be initialized. Following this, Option A (correct) involves selecting an appropriate weight initialization strategy based on the activation functions used. Different activation functions (e.g., ReLU, sigmoid) benefit from different initialization methods. Next, Option C (correct) is to apply the chosen initialization strategy to the weights of each layer in the neural network. This sets the initial values. Finally, Option D (correct) is to begin the training process, allowing the initialized weights to be updated by the optimization algorithm. Selecting an initialization strategy before defining the network architecture (Option A before B) is premature. Applying the strategy (Option C) must follow its selection (Option A). Training (Option D) is the final step after initialization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_86',
  'A data scientist is preparing to train an XGBoost model for a tabular classification problem. XGBoost has numerous hyperparameters that can significantly impact model performance and training time. To achieve optimal results, the data scientist needs to systematically select and tune these hyperparameters. Arrange the following steps in the correct order for selecting appropriate hyperparameters for an XGBoost model.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the objective function (e.g., ''binary:logistic'' for classification) and evaluation metric (e.g., ''auc'', ''error'').","Perform a grid search or Bayesian optimization to systematically explore the hyperparameter space and find optimal values.","Start with default or commonly recommended values for key hyperparameters like `n_estimators`, `learning_rate`, `max_depth`.","Evaluate the model''s performance on a validation set and analyze the impact of hyperparameter changes on bias and variance."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'Selecting appropriate hyperparameters for an XGBoost model begins with defining the problem''s core. Option A (correct) is the initial step: defining the objective function and evaluation metric. This sets the goal for the model. Following this, Option C (correct) involves starting with default or commonly recommended values for key hyperparameters. This provides a baseline configuration. Next, Option D (correct) is to evaluate the model''s performance on a validation set and analyze the impact of hyperparameter changes on bias and variance. This diagnostic step helps understand the current model behavior. Finally, Option B (correct) is to perform a more systematic search like grid search or Bayesian optimization to explore the hyperparameter space and find optimal values, which is done after initial understanding and evaluation. Starting with default values before defining the objective (Option C before A) would be unguided. Performing optimization (Option B) before initial evaluation (Option D) is premature. Evaluation (Option D) must follow initial training with some hyperparameters (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_48_m_48_4_87',
  'An ML engineer is implementing k-fold cross-validation to evaluate a model''s performance and optimize hyperparameters. Order the following steps for a typical k-fold cross-validation process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Split the entire dataset into k equally sized, non-overlapping folds.","Average the performance metrics from all k iterations to get the final evaluation.","Train the model on the k-1 folds and evaluate it on the remaining fold.","Repeat the training and evaluation process k times, using a different fold as the validation set each time."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'The k-fold cross-validation process begins by splitting the entire dataset into k equally sized, non-overlapping folds (Option A). Next, in the first iteration, the model is trained on k-1 of these folds and evaluated on the single remaining fold (Option C). This training and evaluation process is then repeated k times (Option D), ensuring that each fold serves as the validation set exactly once. Finally, to obtain a robust estimate of the model''s performance, the performance metrics from all k iterations are averaged (Option B). Any deviation from this order would compromise the integrity and reliability of the cross-validation results.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain48_10',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_48_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

