) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_57',
  'Which AWS services are suitable for storing and managing large volumes of unstructured data, such as images and videos, for ML training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon RDS","Amazon S3","Amazon EBS","Amazon EFS","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Amazon S3) is ideal for storing large volumes of unstructured data like images and videos due to its object storage model, scalability, durability, and cost-effectiveness. Option D (Amazon EFS) provides scalable, elastic, shared file storage that can be mounted to multiple EC2 instances or SageMaker notebooks, offering file system semantics suitable for certain ML workloads that require shared access to file-based datasets. Option A (Amazon RDS) is a relational database. Option C (Amazon EBS) provides block storage for single EC2 instances. Option E (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL key-value and document database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_58',
  'Which AWS services can be used to implement a cost-effective and scalable solution for batch inference of ML models on large datasets? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances","AWS Lambda with a 15-minute timeout","AWS Batch","Amazon RDS"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform) is a fully managed service that processes large datasets and generates inferences without persistent endpoints. It''s designed for batch processing and is cost-effective. Option D (AWS Batch) enables developers, scientists, and engineers to easily and efficiently run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS. It can provision and manage compute resources (including Spot Instances) to run custom inference scripts, making it scalable and cost-effective for batch inference. Option B (Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances) are generally more expensive than batch-optimized solutions. Option C (AWS Lambda with a 15-minute timeout) is suitable for smaller, short-lived tasks, not typically for large-scale batch inference. Option E (Amazon RDS) is a relational database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_59',
  'A company is looking to integrate pre-trained machine learning capabilities into their applications to process text and extract insights without needing to build, train, and deploy custom models. Which AWS AI services are designed for natural language processing (NLP) tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Lex","Amazon Polly"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Amazon Comprehend) is correct because it is a natural language processing (NLP) service that uses machine learning to find insights and relationships in text. It can identify entities, key phrases, language, sentiments, and more. Option D (Amazon Lex) is correct because it is a service for building conversational interfaces into any application using voice and text. It uses advanced deep learning functionalities of automatic speech recognition (ASR) for converting speech to text, and natural language understanding (NLU) to recognize the intent of the text, making it an NLP service. Option B (Amazon Rekognition) is incorrect as it is a computer vision service that makes it easy to add image and video analysis to your applications. Option C (Amazon Transcribe) is incorrect as it is an automatic speech recognition (ASR) service that converts speech to text. Option E (Amazon Polly) is incorrect as it is a text-to-speech service that turns text into lifelike speech.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_60',
  'A media company wants to automatically analyze images and videos to identify objects, people, text, and activities. They need a fully managed service that provides pre-trained computer vision capabilities. Which AWS AI services can fulfill these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Textract","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Polly"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Rekognition) is correct because it is a computer vision service that makes it easy to add image and video analysis to your applications. It can identify objects, people, text, scenes, and activities, as well as detect inappropriate content. Option B (Amazon Textract) is correct because it is a machine learning service that automatically extracts text, handwriting, and data from scanned documents. While focused on documents, it uses computer vision to analyze the layout and content of images. Option C (Amazon Comprehend) is incorrect as it is a natural language processing (NLP) service that finds insights and relationships in text. Option D (Amazon Transcribe) is incorrect as it is an automatic speech recognition (ASR) service that converts speech to text. Option E (Amazon Polly) is incorrect as it is a text-to-speech service that turns text into lifelike speech.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_61',
  'A startup is developing a novel machine learning model for a highly specialized industry where no pre-trained models exist. They require full control over the model architecture, training environment, and deployment. Which AWS services are most appropriate for building and deploying this custom ML solution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker","AWS Lambda","Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Lex"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker) is correct because it is a fully managed service that provides every developer and data scientist with the ability to build, train, and deploy machine learning models quickly. It offers flexibility for custom algorithms, frameworks, and environments, making it ideal for novel models. Option B (AWS Lambda) is correct because it is a serverless compute service that lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. It can be used to deploy custom inference code for models that have low latency requirements or are invoked infrequently, especially when combined with API Gateway. Option C (Amazon Rekognition) is incorrect as it is a pre-trained computer vision service, not suitable for building custom models from scratch. Option D (Amazon Comprehend) is incorrect as it is a pre-trained natural language processing (NLP) service, not for custom model development. Option E (Amazon Lex) is incorrect as it is a service for building conversational interfaces using pre-trained NLP capabilities, not for custom ML model development.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_62',
  'A company wants to develop an application that can convert spoken language into text for transcription services and generate natural-sounding speech from text for voice assistants. Which AWS AI services should they use to implement these functionalities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Polly","Amazon Lex","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Rekognition"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Transcribe) is correct because it is an automatic speech recognition (ASR) service that makes it easy to add speech-to-text capability to your applications. It can convert audio into accurate text transcripts. Option B (Amazon Polly) is correct because it is a text-to-speech service that turns text into lifelike speech. It allows you to create applications that talk, and build entirely new categories of speech-enabled products. Option C (Amazon Lex) is incorrect as it is a service for building conversational interfaces using voice and text, which includes ASR and NLU, but Transcribe is the dedicated speech-to-text service. Option D (Amazon Comprehend) is incorrect as it is a natural language processing (NLP) service that finds insights and relationships in text. Option E (Amazon Rekognition) is incorrect as it is a computer vision service that makes it easy to add image and video analysis to your applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_63',
  'A data science team needs to train a large deep learning model that requires significant compute resources. To minimize costs, they are looking for ways to leverage AWS''s flexible pricing models for their training jobs. Which AWS services or features can help optimize the cost of these compute-intensive ML training workloads? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances","Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances","AWS Fargate"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon EC2 Spot Instances) is correct because Spot Instances allow you to bid on unused EC2 capacity, often at significantly lower prices than On-Demand instances. They are ideal for fault-tolerant workloads like ML training jobs that can be interrupted and resumed. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training) is correct because it allows you to use Spot Instances for SageMaker training jobs, automatically managing the bidding and interruption handling. This significantly reduces the cost of training large models without manual intervention. Option C (Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances) is incorrect because while they provide uninterrupted compute capacity, they are the most expensive option and do not offer cost optimization for flexible workloads. Option D (Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances) is incorrect because they are suitable for workloads with predictable, long-term usage, offering discounts in exchange for a commitment, but they are not as flexible or cost-effective for transient training jobs as Spot Instances. Option E (AWS Fargate) is incorrect as it is a serverless compute engine for containers, which can be used for ML workloads, but it doesn''t offer the same cost-saving mechanisms as Spot Instances for interruptible training jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_64',
  'A developer wants to quickly add pre-trained machine learning capabilities to an application without managing any underlying infrastructure or training models. Which AWS services provide ready-to-use AI functionalities for common tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Rekognition","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon SageMaker","AWS Glue","Amazon EC2"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Rekognition) is correct because it is a fully managed computer vision service that provides pre-trained models for image and video analysis, such as object detection, facial analysis, and text detection, without requiring any ML expertise. Option B (Amazon Comprehend) is correct because it is a fully managed natural language processing (NLP) service that provides pre-trained models for tasks like sentiment analysis, entity recognition, and key phrase extraction from text, without needing to train custom models. Option C (Amazon SageMaker) is incorrect as it is a platform for building, training, and deploying custom machine learning models, requiring more ML expertise and infrastructure management. Option D (AWS Glue) is incorrect as it is a serverless data integration service for ETL operations, not a pre-trained AI service. Option E (Amazon EC2) is incorrect as it provides virtual servers (instances) for running applications, requiring users to manage infrastructure and install ML frameworks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_65',
  'An e-commerce company wants to implement a conversational chatbot on their website to assist customers with product inquiries and order tracking. Which AWS AI services are best suited for building such an intelligent conversational interface? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Lex","Amazon Polly","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon SageMaker"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Lex) is correct because it is a service for building conversational interfaces into any application using voice and text. It uses advanced deep learning functionalities of automatic speech recognition (ASR) for converting speech to text, and natural language understanding (NLU) to recognize the intent of the text, making it ideal for chatbots. Option B (Amazon Polly) is correct because it is a text-to-speech service that turns text into lifelike speech. It can be integrated with Amazon Lex to provide natural-sounding voice responses for the chatbot, enhancing the conversational experience. Option C (Amazon Comprehend) is incorrect as it is a natural language processing (NLP) service that finds insights and relationships in text, but it doesn''t build conversational interfaces directly. Option D (Amazon Transcribe) is incorrect as it is an automatic speech recognition (ASR) service that converts speech to text, which is a component of a chatbot but not the full solution. Option E (Amazon SageMaker) is incorrect as it is a platform for building, training, and deploying custom machine learning models, which is an overkill for a standard chatbot that can be built with managed AI services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_66',
  'When deciding between using Amazon SageMaker''s built-in algorithms and developing a custom machine learning model, a data scientist needs to consider several factors. Which considerations are important when making this choice? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["The uniqueness of the problem and data characteristics","The availability of pre-trained models that meet requirements","The desired programming language for model development","The cost of data storage in Amazon S3","The number of EC2 instances available in the account"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (The uniqueness of the problem and data characteristics) is correct because if the problem is highly specialized or the data has unique characteristics that off-the-shelf algorithms cannot effectively handle, developing a custom model provides the necessary flexibility and control. Option B (The availability of pre-trained models that meet requirements) is correct because if a pre-trained model or a SageMaker built-in algorithm already performs well for the specific task and data, it can significantly reduce development time and effort compared to building a custom model. Option C (The desired programming language for model development) is incorrect as SageMaker supports various popular ML frameworks and languages (Python, R, etc.) for both built-in and custom models, so it''s less of a differentiator. Option D (The cost of data storage in Amazon S3) is incorrect as S3 storage costs are generally consistent regardless of whether built-in or custom models are used, and it''s not a primary factor in choosing the model development approach. Option E (The number of EC2 instances available in the account) is incorrect as SageMaker manages the underlying infrastructure, so the number of available EC2 instances is not a direct decision factor for the data scientist.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_67',
  'A company wants to enhance its internal knowledge base with intelligent search capabilities, allowing employees to quickly find relevant information from documents, FAQs, and internal wikis. Which AWS services can be leveraged to build a comprehensive intelligent search solution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kendra","Amazon OpenSearch Service","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Textract","Amazon Lex"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon Kendra) is correct because it is an intelligent search service powered by machine learning. It provides a highly accurate and easy-to-use enterprise search experience, allowing users to find answers to their questions from various data sources using natural language queries. Option B (Amazon OpenSearch Service) is correct because it is a managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch clusters. It provides powerful full-text search, data visualization, and analytics capabilities, which are fundamental for building custom search solutions. Option C (Amazon Comprehend) is incorrect as it is a natural language processing (NLP) service that finds insights and relationships in text, which can enrich data for search but doesn''t provide the search engine itself. Option D (Amazon Textract) is incorrect as it extracts text and data from documents, which can be a data source for search but not the search service itself. Option E (Amazon Lex) is incorrect as it is a service for building conversational interfaces, not an intelligent search engine.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_68',
  'A large manufacturing company wants to implement predictive maintenance using machine learning. They have a vast amount of sensor data from industrial machinery, which needs to be processed, transformed, and used to train models. The company requires a fully managed service that can handle large-scale data processing and provide a comprehensive environment for building, training, and deploying ML models without managing underlying infrastructure. They also want to leverage pre-built algorithms and tools to accelerate development. Which two AWS services are best suited for this end-to-end ML solution, focusing on managed services and rapid development? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker","AWS Glue","Amazon EC2","Amazon EMR","Amazon Rekognition"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the core service for building, training, and deploying ML models. Amazon SageMaker provides a fully managed service with a comprehensive set of tools, including notebooks, built-in algorithms, and hosting capabilities, allowing the manufacturing company to develop and operationalize their predictive maintenance models without managing servers. Option B (correct) is ideal for large-scale data processing and transformation. AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that can prepare the vast amount of sensor data for machine learning. It integrates seamlessly with Amazon S3 (where sensor data might be stored) and SageMaker, providing a serverless way to clean, normalize, and transform data into a format suitable for model training. Option C (incorrect) is an unmanaged compute service. Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers, but managing these instances for data processing and ML development would increase operational overhead, which contradicts the requirement for a fully managed solution. Option D (incorrect) is a managed Hadoop framework. Amazon EMR is a managed cluster platform for big data processing. While it can handle large-scale data, AWS Glue offers a more serverless and fully managed ETL experience, aligning better with the ''fully managed service'' and ''minimize operational overhead'' implicit in rapid development. Option E (incorrect) is an AI service for image and video analysis. Amazon Rekognition is a pre-trained AI service for computer vision tasks. While useful for specific vision problems, it''s not a general-purpose platform for building custom predictive maintenance models from sensor data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_69',
  'A large enterprise is migrating its legacy data analytics platform to AWS. Part of this migration involves processing petabytes of historical data stored in various formats, including CSV, JSON, and Parquet, into a unified data lake on Amazon S3. The data needs to be cleaned, transformed, and cataloged before it can be used for machine learning model training and business intelligence dashboards. The enterprise wants to minimize operational overhead for managing ETL infrastructure and ensure that the data catalog is automatically updated. Which two AWS services are best suited to build this scalable and managed data processing and cataloging solution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Glue","Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS DataSync"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service. AWS Glue can automatically discover schema, catalog data in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and perform large-scale data transformations using Apache Spark. This directly addresses the need to process petabytes of data in various formats, clean, transform, and catalog it with minimal operational overhead for infrastructure management. Option B (correct) is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Amazon Athena integrates with the AWS Glue Data Catalog, allowing users to query the transformed and cataloged data without provisioning or managing servers. This is ideal for both ML model training data access and business intelligence dashboards. Option C (incorrect) is a fully managed petabyte-scale data warehouse service. While Amazon Redshift is excellent for analytical queries, the scenario emphasizes processing data into a data lake on Amazon S3 and minimizing ETL infrastructure overhead, which AWS Glue and Athena are better suited for in a serverless data lake context. Option D (incorrect) is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3. While useful for ingesting streaming data, it''s not designed for batch ETL of historical data or cataloging. Option E (incorrect) is a data transfer service that simplifies, automates, and accelerates moving data between on-premises storage and AWS storage services. It''s for migration, not for ongoing ETL or data cataloging within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_70',
  'A research institution is developing a deep learning model for genomic sequence analysis. The training process requires access to a massive dataset of genomic files, often terabytes in size, which are stored in Amazon S3. The training jobs run on Amazon EC2 instances with powerful GPUs and need high-throughput, low-latency access to these files. The institution wants to optimize the data loading process to prevent I/O bottlenecks and ensure efficient utilization of GPU resources, while also minimizing the cost of data access. Which two AWS services or features can be leveraged to efficiently provide the training instances with high-performance access to the S3 data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon FSx for Lustre","Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","Amazon S3 Mountpoint","AWS DataSync"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) is a high-performance file system. Amazon FSx for Lustre provides a high-performance file system optimized for compute-intensive workloads like deep learning. It can be linked to an Amazon S3 bucket, allowing EC2 instances to access S3 data as if it were a local file system, providing the high-throughput and low-latency access required for large genomic files and preventing I/O bottlenecks. Option D (correct) provides a file interface to S3. Amazon S3 Mountpoint allows you to mount an S3 bucket as a local file system on your EC2 instances. This enables applications to access S3 objects using file system operations, which is beneficial for workloads that expect a file system interface but need to access data directly from S3, providing efficient data streaming and reducing the need to copy entire datasets. Option B (incorrect) is for accelerating data uploads to S3 over long distances. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up data transfers to and from S3 buckets but is primarily for network transfer performance, not for optimizing the file system access performance for compute instances once the data is in S3. Option C (incorrect) provides block storage for EC2 instances. While Amazon EBS volumes can offer high IOPS, copying terabytes of genomic data from S3 to EBS volumes before each training job would be time-consuming and costly, and it doesn''t provide a direct, efficient way to stream data from S3 for large datasets. Option E (incorrect) is a data transfer service for moving data between on-premises storage and AWS, or between AWS storage services. It''s not designed for direct, high-performance file system access from EC2 instances to S3 data during active training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_71',
  'A global logistics company uses machine learning to optimize delivery routes. They have a complex model that requires significant computational resources, often running for several hours. To manage costs, they want to leverage Spot Instances for training jobs, but need a robust way to orchestrate these jobs, handle interruptions gracefully, and ensure that the training process can resume from the last checkpoint. Which two AWS services or features are most suitable for managing and executing these fault-tolerant, cost-optimized ML training jobs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Batch","Amazon SageMaker Training Jobs","Amazon EC2 Spot Fleet","AWS Step Functions","Amazon EMR"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fully managed batch computing service. AWS Batch allows users to run batch computing workloads on AWS, dynamically provisioning compute resources (including Spot Instances) based on the volume of work. It can manage job queues, dependencies, and automatically retry failed jobs, making it suitable for orchestrating ML training jobs that need to be fault-tolerant and cost-optimized using Spot Instances. Option B (correct) provides a managed environment for ML training. Amazon SageMaker Training Jobs directly supports using Spot Instances for cost optimization. It also integrates with S3 for checkpointing, allowing training jobs to save their state periodically and resume from the last saved checkpoint in case of an interruption, which is crucial for long-running jobs on Spot Instances. Option C (incorrect) is a low-level service for managing Spot Instances. Amazon EC2 Spot Fleet allows users to request a fleet of Spot Instances, but it requires significant manual effort to manage job orchestration, checkpointing, and graceful handling of interruptions for complex ML training workflows compared to higher-level managed services like AWS Batch or SageMaker Training Jobs. Option D (incorrect) is a serverless workflow service. AWS Step Functions can orchestrate complex workflows, but it typically orchestrates calls to other services like AWS Batch or SageMaker. It''s an orchestration layer, not the primary service for executing the compute-intensive training job itself or handling Spot Instance interruptions directly at the compute level. Option E (incorrect) is a managed Hadoop framework. Amazon EMR is primarily for big data processing using frameworks like Hadoop and Spark. While it can run ML workloads, it''s not specifically optimized for the fault-tolerant, checkpointing requirements of long-running, cost-optimized ML training jobs on Spot Instances in the same way SageMaker Training Jobs or AWS Batch are.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_72',
  'A research team is developing a natural language processing (NLP) model to analyze scientific papers. They need to extract key entities (e.g., genes, proteins, diseases) and relationships between them from large volumes of unstructured text. The team has limited ML expertise and wants to leverage pre-trained models where possible, but also needs the flexibility to fine-tune models for domain-specific entities if necessary. Which two AWS services would be most appropriate for this task, balancing ease of use with customization capabilities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Comprehend Medical","Amazon SageMaker","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Textract","Amazon Lex"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a specialized NLP service for healthcare and life sciences. Amazon Comprehend Medical is pre-trained to extract medical information, including entities like genes, proteins, and diseases, from unstructured clinical text. This directly addresses the need for extracting key entities from scientific papers with minimal ML expertise. Option B (correct) provides a comprehensive platform for custom ML. Amazon SageMaker allows the team to fine-tune pre-trained NLP models (e.g., from Hugging Face via SageMaker JumpStart) or build custom models for domain-specific entities if the pre-trained services don''t fully meet their needs. It offers the flexibility and control required for advanced customization. Option C (incorrect) is a speech-to-text service. Amazon Transcribe converts audio to text, which is not relevant for analyzing existing text documents. Option D (incorrect) is for extracting text and data from documents. Amazon Textract is an OCR service that extracts text, handwriting, and data from scanned documents. While useful for digitizing papers, it doesn''t perform the NLP entity extraction required. Option E (incorrect) is for building conversational interfaces. Amazon Lex is used to build chatbots and virtual assistants, which is not the primary use case for extracting entities and relationships from scientific papers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_73',
  'A media company wants to automatically generate summaries of news articles and detect breaking news topics in real-time. They need a solution that can process a continuous stream of incoming articles, identify key information, and categorize content without requiring extensive machine learning model development. The solution should be cost-effective and easy to integrate with their existing content management system. Which two AWS services are most suitable for this real-time text analysis and summarization task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","Amazon Polly","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon SageMaker"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a natural language processing (NLP) service that excels at text analysis. Amazon Comprehend can identify key phrases, entities, and topics from unstructured text, making it suitable for detecting breaking news topics and extracting information for summarization. It is a pre-trained AI service, requiring no ML expertise. Option B (correct) is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Splunk. It can also integrate with AWS Lambda for custom processing. This allows the company to ingest a continuous stream of news articles and then trigger services like Amazon Comprehend for real-time analysis. Option C (incorrect) is a text-to-speech service. Amazon Polly converts text into lifelike speech, which is not relevant for text analysis or summarization. Option D (incorrect) is a speech-to-text service. Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text, which is not applicable for analyzing text-based news articles. Option E (incorrect) is a fully managed service for building, training, and deploying ML models. While Amazon SageMaker could be used to build custom summarization models, the requirement for ''without requiring extensive machine learning model development'' and ''cost-effective and easy to integrate'' points towards pre-trained AI services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_74',
  'A manufacturing company is implementing a quality control system using computer vision. They need to analyze images of manufactured parts to detect defects in real-time on the factory floor. The solution requires low-latency inference and the ability to process images from multiple camera feeds simultaneously. The company wants to leverage a managed service that can handle the inference workload efficiently, potentially using specialized hardware, and integrate with their existing data processing pipeline. Which two AWS services are most appropriate for deploying and managing the real-time inference endpoints for this computer vision task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Inference Endpoints","Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels","AWS Lambda","Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","Amazon Kinesis Video Streams"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) provides a fully managed environment for deploying ML models for real-time inference. Amazon SageMaker Inference Endpoints can host custom computer vision models, scale automatically to handle multiple camera feeds, and support instances with GPUs for low-latency processing, meeting the requirements for efficient inference workload management. Option B (correct) is a specialized computer vision service. Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels allows customers to train custom object detection and image classification models without writing code. Once trained, these models can be deployed as inference endpoints, providing a managed, scalable solution for real-time defect detection with minimal ML expertise. Option C (incorrect) is a serverless compute service. While AWS Lambda can be used for inference, it has payload size limits and execution duration limits that make it less suitable for high-throughput, low-latency computer vision inference on large images or video streams, especially if GPU acceleration is needed. Option D (incorrect) are cost-effective EC2 instances. While Amazon EC2 Spot Instances can be used for cost-effective compute, managing them directly for real-time inference endpoints adds significant operational overhead compared to managed services like SageMaker or Rekognition Custom Labels, which handle the underlying infrastructure. Option E (incorrect) is a service for securely streaming video from connected devices to AWS. Amazon Kinesis Video Streams is for ingesting video data, not for deploying and managing ML inference endpoints for computer vision.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_75',
  'A large retail chain is building a personalized shopping assistant using Amazon Lex for conversational AI. The assistant needs to integrate with various backend systems, including inventory management, order processing, and customer profiles, to provide accurate and real-time responses. The development team wants to ensure that the conversational flows are robust, can handle complex user requests, and are easily extensible. Which two AWS services are commonly integrated with Amazon Lex to provide backend logic, data retrieval, and fulfillment capabilities for complex conversational interactions? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Lambda","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon Polly","Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Kinesis"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a serverless compute service. AWS Lambda functions are commonly used as the backend fulfillment for Amazon Lex intents. When Lex identifies a user''s intent, it can invoke a Lambda function to perform custom business logic, interact with other AWS services (like DynamoDB, RDS, etc.), or call external APIs (e.g., inventory management, order processing systems) to gather information and formulate a response. Option B (correct) is a fast, flexible NoSQL database service. Amazon DynamoDB is frequently used with Amazon Lex and AWS Lambda to store session attributes, user profiles, order details, or other dynamic data needed to maintain context and fulfill complex conversational interactions. It provides the low-latency data retrieval required for real-time responses. Option C (incorrect) is a text-to-speech service. Amazon Polly converts text into lifelike speech, which is used by Lex for voice interactions, but it doesn''t provide backend logic or data fulfillment. Option D (incorrect) is a speech-to-text service. Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text, which is used by Lex to process voice input, but it doesn''t provide backend logic or data fulfillment. Option E (incorrect) is a real-time data streaming service. Amazon Kinesis is used for processing large streams of data. While it could be part of a larger data pipeline, it''s not a direct backend fulfillment or data storage service for conversational AI in the way Lambda and DynamoDB are.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_76',
  'A global news agency wants to automate the process of converting audio interviews into searchable text and then translating that text into multiple languages for international distribution. The solution needs to be highly scalable, accurate, and require minimal manual intervention. The agency has a large archive of audio files and receives new audio content daily. Which two AWS AI services are best suited to build this automated audio-to-text and translation pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Transcribe","Amazon Translate","Amazon Polly","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Lex"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is a fully managed speech-to-text service. Amazon Transcribe can accurately convert audio interviews into searchable text, supporting various audio formats and languages. It''s highly scalable and ideal for processing large archives and daily new audio content. Option B (correct) is a neural machine translation service. Amazon Translate can take the text output from Amazon Transcribe and accurately translate it into multiple target languages, enabling international distribution with minimal manual intervention. Option C (incorrect) is a text-to-speech service. Amazon Polly converts text into lifelike speech, which is the reverse of what is needed (audio-to-text). Option D (incorrect) is a natural language processing (NLP) service. Amazon Comprehend analyzes text for sentiment, entities, and topics. While it could analyze the transcribed text, its primary purpose is not translation. Option E (incorrect) is a conversational AI service. Amazon Lex is used to build chatbots and virtual assistants, which is not relevant for transcribing audio or translating text.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_77',
  'A manufacturing company is implementing a quality control system that uses computer vision to inspect products on an assembly line. They have a large dataset of images of both good and defective products. The company needs to train a custom image classification model quickly, with minimal machine learning expertise, and deploy it for real-time inference. The solution must be able to adapt to new types of defects over time. Which two AWS services are most appropriate for building and deploying this custom computer vision solution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels","Amazon SageMaker Canvas","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon Textract","Amazon Polly"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is an AI service that allows users to train custom computer vision models without writing code. Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels enables the manufacturing company to use their specific images of good and defective products to train a model for defect detection. It simplifies the ML process, requiring minimal expertise, and the trained model can be deployed for real-time inference. Option B (correct) is a visual point-and-click interface that enables business analysts and citizen data scientists to build ML models without writing any code. Amazon SageMaker Canvas can be used to quickly build and train custom image classification models from image datasets, making it an excellent choice for users with minimal ML expertise who need to adapt to new defect types over time. Option C (incorrect) is a data labeling service. Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth helps build high-quality training datasets by providing human labeling workflows. While it could be used to label the images, it''s not the service for building and deploying the model itself. Option D (incorrect) is an OCR service. Amazon Textract extracts text and data from scanned documents. It is not relevant for image classification to detect product defects. Option E (incorrect) is a text-to-speech service. Amazon Polly converts text into lifelike speech, which is not relevant for computer vision tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_2_78',
  'Which of the following AWS AI services are specifically designed to provide Natural Language Processing (NLP) capabilities? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Rekognition for image and video analysis.","Amazon Polly for text-to-speech conversion.","Amazon Comprehend","Amazon Lex","Amazon Transcribe for speech-to-text conversion."]'::json,
  '{2,3}',
  'Option C (Amazon Comprehend) is an NLP service that uses machine learning to find insights and relationships in text, such as sentiment analysis, entity recognition, and key phrase extraction. Option D (Amazon Lex) is a service for building conversational interfaces, which includes Natural Language Understanding (NLU) capabilities to recognize the intent of the user and extract relevant information. Option A (Amazon Rekognition) is for computer vision tasks (image and video analysis). Option B (Amazon Polly) is a text-to-speech service, and Option E (Amazon Transcribe) is a speech-to-text service; while related to language, they do not provide core NLP capabilities like text analysis or understanding intent.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

