VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_1_80',
  'Order the steps to create a Docker image for a custom machine learning model and prepare it for deployment on Amazon SageMaker.',
  'ordering',
  '["Write a Dockerfile to define the image environment, dependencies, and entrypoint.","Build the Docker image locally using the `docker build` command.","Tag the Docker image with the Amazon ECR repository URI.","Push the tagged Docker image to an Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) repository."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To prepare a custom model for SageMaker, first write a Dockerfile (Option A) that specifies the base image, installs dependencies, and defines the model''s entrypoint. Next, build the Docker image locally (Option B) using the `docker build` command. Then, tag the Docker image (Option C) with the full Amazon ECR repository URI. Finally, push the tagged Docker image (Option D) to your Amazon ECR repository, making it accessible for SageMaker deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_1_81',
  'A data scientist needs to ensure their ML application can recover gracefully from instance failures during training. Order the steps to implement a resilient architecture using Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling and S3.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon S3 bucket to store training data and model checkpoints.","Configure an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group to launch instances with the necessary ML environment.","Launch an Amazon EC2 instance and install the ML framework and dependencies.","Create an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) from the configured EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}',
  'To build a resilient ML training architecture, first create an Amazon S3 bucket (Option A) for storing training data and model checkpoints. Next, launch an Amazon EC2 instance (Option C) and set up the ML framework and dependencies. Then, create an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) (Option D) from this configured instance, capturing the environment. Finally, configure an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group (Option B) to launch new instances from this AMI, ensuring that new instances can replace failed ones and resume training by loading checkpoints from S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_1_82',
  'A developer needs to create a custom Docker image for an Amazon SageMaker training job. Order the steps to build and push this image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR).',
  'ordering',
  '["Build the Docker image using the Dockerfile and tag it appropriately.","Create an Amazon ECR repository to store the Docker image.","Push the built Docker image to the Amazon ECR repository.","Authenticate your Docker client to the Amazon ECR registry."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'To prepare a custom Docker image for SageMaker, first, create an Amazon ECR repository (Option B) where the image will be stored. Next, authenticate your Docker client (Option D) to the ECR registry to ensure you have the necessary permissions to push images. After authentication, build the Docker image (Option A) from your Dockerfile and tag it with the ECR repository URI. Finally, push the built Docker image (Option C) to the Amazon ECR repository, making it available for SageMaker training jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_1_83',
  'An ML team wants to monitor the performance of their Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint and trigger alerts if latency exceeds a threshold. Order the steps to set this up using Amazon CloudWatch.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm based on the ''Invocations'' or ''ModelLatency'' metric for the SageMaker endpoint.","Deploy the Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint.","Configure the CloudWatch alarm to send notifications to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic.","Publish custom metrics from the SageMaker endpoint if default metrics are insufficient."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To monitor and alert on endpoint performance, first, deploy the Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint (Option B) so that it starts generating metrics. Next, create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm (Option A) that monitors relevant SageMaker endpoint metrics like ''Invocations'' or ''ModelLatency''. Then, configure this CloudWatch alarm (Option C) to send notifications to an Amazon SNS topic, which can then notify subscribers (e.g., email, SMS). Optionally, if default metrics do not provide enough detail, publish custom metrics (Option D) from the SageMaker endpoint to CloudWatch for more granular monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_1_84',
  'A data engineer needs to set up a resilient data processing pipeline for ML training data that can withstand component failures. Order the steps to achieve this using highly available AWS services.',
  'ordering',
  '["Store raw data in an Amazon S3 bucket, leveraging its inherent durability and availability.","Use AWS Lambda functions for data transformation, benefiting from its serverless and highly available nature.","Orchestrate the pipeline using AWS Step Functions for state management and retry logic.","Process data using Amazon EMR clusters deployed across multiple Availability Zones."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'To build a resilient data processing pipeline, the first step is to store raw data in an Amazon S3 bucket (Option A), which offers high durability and availability by default. Next, for processing large datasets, use Amazon EMR clusters (Option D) deployed across multiple Availability Zones to ensure fault tolerance. For specific data transformations, AWS Lambda functions (Option B) can be used due to their serverless, highly available, and scalable nature. Finally, orchestrate the entire pipeline using AWS Step Functions (Option C), which provides state management, error handling, and retry logic, making the workflow resilient to individual component failures.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_49_m_49_1_85',
  'An ML team wants to implement Auto Scaling for a custom ML inference application running on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). Order the steps to configure this setup.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Auto Scaling group and attach it to the target group of the Application Load Balancer.","Launch an Amazon EC2 instance and install the custom ML inference application.","Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) and a target group for the EC2 instances.","Define scaling policies for the Auto Scaling group based on metrics like CPU utilization or request count."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To set up Auto Scaling for a custom ML inference application, first, launch an Amazon EC2 instance (Option B) and install your custom ML inference application on it, ensuring it''s ready to serve predictions. Next, create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) and a target group (Option C) to distribute incoming traffic across your instances. Then, create an Auto Scaling group (Option A) and attach it to the ALB''s target group, which will manage the number of EC2 instances. Finally, define scaling policies (Option D) for the Auto Scaling group, specifying when to add or remove instances based on performance metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain49_10',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_49_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

